      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>BOWLING NOTES.</head>
        <p>BOWLING NOTES.</p>
        <p>(Continued from Page 2.) W R oughley had to play exceedingly well to gain a three point win over A Regan which he did after 26 ends, having been in arrears for the first l'rends.</p>
        <p>W Daniels (Rylands) met .1 Hands and although he won 21-18 in 28 ends he had all Ms work cut out, for the Bull's Head man is no mere novice at the game.</p>
        <p>T Rigby (Bull's Head) was, as usual, in good form against A Stringer and after only wanting one at the 31st end, in the last two ends his opponent added two to his 19 and won by the odd chalk.</p>
        <p>There was much speculation as to which team was in the final for the game had been so changeable and the score to wipe off such a small one-29—that although it was felt Rylands were in, everyone were surprised to know it was only by four points.</p>
        <p>CF:OLTERY HOTEL V JUNIOR CONSERVATIVES. As we predicted a fortnight ago the Juniors were given a warm time on the Hotel Green on Saturday and with only 12 points lo wipe off the Cemetery men were easily the favourtes. Only five games were won by the Juniors who finally lost by 33 points the Cemetery men winning by 21 points on the home and home matches.</p>
        <p>There were some big wins and there was only one defeated man who scored 20 points and another had 19 to his credit.</p>
        <p>R Holbrook and H Eligharn (Juniors) were out of it altogether for they lost 9-21 and 5-21 respectively against T Bolton and IV Butterworth.</p>
        <p>E Gleave had some difficulty in shaking off J Monks (juniors) and he only won by the odd one in 31 ends. At toe 27th end he led by 20-17 but before he could gain his final one Monks had run his score to twenty.</p>
        <p>A Forster was the Cemetery Hotel trundler to lose by only two points to '2 Quarry who is at home on nearly every greenin town. The players kept well together from the first but at the 223 d end the Junior led 18-13. At the 27th end the score was 19 each buts good double on the next length placed Quarry over by two points.</p>
        <p>The Cemetery men played fine bowls and they well deserve the honour they have gained. The Juniors have kept up well throughout the competition but they had only a slight lead to go up to the Cemetery green and expect to come away the conquerors. Many a team have tared far worse on this green.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>CONSOLATION COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>CONSOLATION COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>The return matches in the semi-final of the consola- tion competition were played on Saturday and as was expected the Liberal Club are to figure in the final. They will have as their opponents Penketh Park who made such short work of the Cemetery Hotel No 2 team on Saturday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>LIBERAL CLUB V. KING AND QUEEN._</head>
        <p>LIBERAL CLUB V. KING AND QUEEN._</p>
        <p>With 70 points to commence with from the first 'dame on the Bewsey-street green the Liberals had every confidence when they made the journey to Padgate-lane on Saturday and eventually they increased their win to 96 on botb matches.</p>
        <p>H Cook made a good start for the Liberals when he defeated R Savory 21-12 in 23 ends, but A Gleave (Liberal) was not having such an easy time of it with J Makin and he eventually lost by five points in 26 ends.</p>
        <p>ii Hitchmough was having a stiff time of it with F Clare (K and (4) and only won by two points in the 32nd end. The Liberal led from the first to the 2ith end when his score was 18 to his opponents' 17. Clare, with a good double, gained a lead of one on the next end but Hitchmough made matters equal on the ensuing length and in the other two ends got home by two points.</p>
        <p>S Rigby (Liberal) played a very steady game against W Locker and only lost by the odd one in 34 ends. Locker gained a lead of 13-ti at the 20th end and maintained a slight advantage up to the 30th end when Rigby peeled with 18. Again at the 32nd end the score was equal with 19 each, and on the next length the Liberal led 20-19. A beautiful double placed the Padgate-lane bowler over the post, he winning 21-20 after a very exciting and interesting game.</p>
        <p>H. Gatley (K. and Q.) fared very badly at the hands of W. Dainteth aho won 21-3 in 17 ends.</p>
        <p>The remaining games were fairly evenly contested and proved most interesting.</p>
        <p>The Liberals should have every confidence for the final but they must not reckon without Penketh Park who are terrors on their own sward.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>CEMETERY HOTEL V. FERRETTI..</head>
        <p>CEMETERY HOTEL V. FERRETTI..</p>
        <p>To win by 71 points and only lose one individual game is indeed a noteworthy performance and this the Penketh men did when they entertained the Cemetery men on Penketh Green on Saturday. They had a score of 48 to wipe oft which was the majority of the Ceme- try men on the Manchester-road Green. There were only two of the Cemetery men who put up a very tight game and these were It Brobbin ana J Melhoutne who were each beaten 21-20 by 1:1 Bowyer and J Finch.</p>
        <p>Bowyer ougLt to have made his min more decisive for at the 25th end he led by 19-12 but Brobbin got some good woods in and at the 31st end led by 20-19. Bowyer got a double on the next length and won by the odd point.</p>
        <p>Finch and Melbourne were very evenly matched for they kept near each other all the through the game and Melbourne made his final score when he led 20-19 at the 32nd end. Finch got two singles on the next two ends and just scraped home by one point.</p>
        <p>When the villagers and the Liberal Club meet in the final, the tussle will be a keen one and whichever team wins will have to play remarkably well.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>CRICKET NOTES,</head>
        <p>CRICKET NOTES,</p>
        <p>'Farrington experienced a disappointment at Cheetharn, for at one stage of the game they had high hopes of victory, but their prospects crumbled away and in the end they were beaten by five wickets. This time it was the bowling and fielding that were below par, the bitting being a great improvement on recent displays. Warrington's chances fluctuated surpris- ingly. Making a promising start they had compiled over 70 for the loss of four wickets, J Callon playing sound cricket for his 22, while other useful innings came from S L Partridge (17), Fisher (15) and J T Bradburn (I 3 Then four wickets fell very cheaply. Towards the close Richardson distinguished himself with a splendid stand, having made 23 when he was out to a magnificent catch on the edge of the boundary, after a beautiful drive. The fact that Warrington had two men run out had a somewhat depressing effect on the side. The Rev. H. Gray was in good form with the bat and was not out with 18 to his credit. The Cheetham batting was not nearly so brlight and exhilarating as Warrington's. Only when they doubted whether they would be able to get the necessary runs did they attempt to force the game, and then they sacrificed wickets. As long as they played a steady, cautious game they were quite safe. The first pair of batsmen did much to place the issue beyond doubt, I: J Smethurst knocking up 12 and H J Masora amassing 39.</p>
        <p>The Warrington bowling failed to make much impression. For some unaccountable reason the Rev. H Gray was not nearly so deadly as is his wont —in fact he did not secure a single wicket. This is an experience which has not fallen to his lot in any other match for several seasons. The Warrington fielding left much to be desired. The ground work was poor and the out-field men were slow in picking up and returning. When play ceased Cheetham had made 135, with only five wickets down.</p>
        <p>To-clay Warringtoa will entertain Stockport at Arpley.</p>
        <p>Warrington 2n3 covered themselves with glory in their match with Cheetbam 2nd. They dismissed the visitors for the small total of .)4, Lowe being the chief destructive agent. The Warrington response was 179, W. V. Turton corning out with the top score of 33. S. Baxter had amassed 29 when he had the bad lick to he run out, and Lieutenant Ingram suffered a similar fate when he had made 22.</p>
        <p>The feature of the Warrington and District League matches was the downfall of Sankey who are running a neck-and-neck race with Warringt9n Conservatives for the championship. To Whitecross belongs the credit of inflicting the reverse. They sent the villagers to the right-about for 25 and then just passed that total with six wickets in hand. Barlow was chiefly responsible for the collapse, eight of the wick6ts falling to his account. The batting on either side presented nothing noteworthy.</p>
        <p>Rylands' Recreation made short work of Cairo-street Unitarians, who were all out for the insignificant figure of 10, thanks chiefly to the keen howling of WaywAll. Tbe Recs put together 83 for eight wickets, A Renwick batting splendidly for 44.</p>
        <p>Wilderspool Brewery met superior foemen in St Helens Cable Company who obtained a majority of 51.</p>
        <p>For the victors Raynor (25) and Lloyd (20) batted well, Fellows and Tweedle doing invaluable work with the ball.</p>
        <p>The League table to date is as follows : P. W. L. D. Pts. Warrington Conservatives 8 ... 6 ... 1 ... 1 ...13 Sankey 9 .ii ... 2 ... 1 ...13 Whitecross Cairo-street lO . 5 ... 4 ... 1 ...11 St Helens Csble ..... Rylands Orford Wilderspool Walkers 5 . 0 4 ... 1 ... 1 TO-DAY'S FIXTURES.</p>
        <p>St. Helens Cable v Sankey Orford v Whitecross</p>
        <p>kryids v Wilderspool Walkers v Warrington Conservatives</p>
        <p>In the Warrington and district Sunday School League Brick-street accounted for Walton Juniors by 20 runs.J Holloway and R Stevens each knocked up 13 for the winners and Stevens also wrought havoc with the ball. The match between Workingmen's Mission and Bold- street can only be described as farcical. The Bold- street men never had the ghost of chance, for they allowed the mission to pile on 106 runs and then made the ludicrous response of 6 I On the winning side the best batting display was that of E Thomas for 22, while other notable scorers were E Hughes (18), S Lloyd (12) and H Whitby (14). Most of the bowling was done by Corlett.</p>
        <p>Orford St. John's administered a severe shock to Stockton Heath who had suffered only one previous defeat this season. J McGarr again shone in the bowl- ing department, taking 9 wickets for 8 runs. His record tor the season is 50 wickets for 31 runs, and he is undoubtedly the finest bowler in the League.</p>
        <p>Excitement ran high in the friendly encounter between St Helens Cable Juniors and Sankey Villas,the Cable lads winning by one run. Mather (20) and Thompson (17) made up for the collapse of the Cable eleven in their first innings. Has (10) batted well for Sankey. On Tuesday the Cable juniors inflicted a severe drubbing on the Victorians. Mather (13) and Thoma 3 (17) were in form with the bat and ball, the former taking 5 wickets for 5 runs and the latter 4 wickets for 4 runs. Blease was the only Victorian to offer any resistance.</p>
        <p>Warrington Secondary School triumphed over the Old Boys, 11 runs separating the teams. F. W. Simcock headed the scores for the visitors with 13, and P. R. Leigh contributed a not out innings of 12. For the Old Boys F. Roddy (12) was the only player to reach double figures.</p>
        <p>Boteler Grammar School gained a- thorough-going victory at the expense of Hulrue G.ammar School, Woodcock being in brilliant form and compiling 43.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>CYCLIST'S NOTE-BOOK.</head>
        <p>THE CYCLIST'S NOTE-BOOK.</p>
        <p>[By " Silver Street."]</p>
        <p>AVIATION EXTRAORDINARY. I I have had my attention drawn to a fairly long paragraph in our ex-neutral contemporary contradic- ing my statements as to the boundaries of Cheshire. The writer lifts my own paragraph bodily from the" Examiner" of June 15th, and then proceeds to demolish it thusly : It is a fairly regular duty of the staff in this office to explain that Warrington is all-Lanca- shire and that no part of the borough lies in Cheshire. Now I would like to ask which b Tough is that harassed member of the staff referring to, for certainly a good part of the Parliamentary Borough of Warrington is on the south of the canal. Is that in Lancashire too? But the gem of the paragraph aomes later: The facts are as we state them, notwithstand- ing the " clinching" evidence of our having two Cheshire churches in our midst._</p>
        <p>I belie;;;;e'll—a;;;; -ioCi'd many Cheshire men and women in our midst, and Cheshire cheeses are often imported across both canal and river, but how a Cheshire church can stand in a Lancashire church- yard perhaps some of our aviators will explain. So we leave the question once more.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>BARTINGTON BANK.</head>
        <p>BARTINGTON BANK.</p>
        <p>Strange in how many cases life must be lost before improvements of the most evidently urgent nature are carried out.</p>
        <p>The times that the dangerous character of the hill from the canal bridge to Acton Swing Bridge has been pointed out must be without number. The residents iia the neighbourhood are always speaking of it, and are daily expecting the " unforeseeable " accident as it will be called at the inquest. • It was a four-horse each this time, on Walking Day. The driver was happily able to hold the shafters in and compel the leaders to take the right- angled bend at the bottom so as to stop safely at the "Horns," or another accident would have been added to the long list that week end. Hardly a day goes by without some minor mishap taking place along that space of only 300 yards. And why is the improve- ment delayed? Sir Gilbert Greenall. so Tom Barker tells me, is exceedingly reasonable in his dealings with the Council regarding his own share of the land, but one strip belongs to -- (we mustn't mention names in these dangerous days), who is bolding the Council up for the very top price. He is already an extremely rich landowner, and yet the lives or limbs of the happy boys and girls who are gaily flying into the country on coach or cycle weigh as nothing to him beside a few dirty sovereigns. By the way, who is Tom Barker? It would be a caution if I could so enthusiastically describe the wild flowers by the road sides of our dear Cheshire lanes, and forget the human flowers that we sometimes come across, the sweetest and best of all. A good many Warrington cyclists have seen him and know him too. He lives at the cottage on the Canal Bridge, going down through Bartington to the river Weaver at Acton Bridge. An old soldier, now pensioned, I believe, he is simply doubled up with rheumatism and can only walk, nay trail himself across the high road with two sticks I had missed him for a long time and found last May that he has been in bed for over a year, "but I'm better again now, and hoping the summer will put me all right again. This wet spring makes your knees springe and then it's a bit bad." A bit bad, indeed! A man who can scarce use a muscle below his hips and yet is cheerful as a sunbeam. It makes one wonder how anyone can ever set to a-grumblin4 about anything. There he leans against the white railings near the Canal Bridge, when it is fine, and when he is the better side out, and has always a merry word, a smile, a pleasant crack of friendly chat for everyone who will stop and talk with him. Cyclists have rare opportunities of discovering these gems of the country side, the hardly used victims of Dame Labour and Mistress Climate, whose bent backs and springeing joints, whose swollen and aching knuckles make them into the true right honourables of our nations, far more than any Birthday List or Prime Minister's favours.</p>
        <p>_ To J. F. EARLESTOWN.—ROUTE TO HARROGATE, I avoiding Manchester, Oldham, Halifax, Bradford and Leeds. Warrington to Eccles (Station) 14; over Pen- dleton Station and the Irwell to Broughton, Crumpsall and Heaton Park 6, Middleton, Rochdale 8, Littleboro, Blackstone Edge, turn right to Ripponden 12, turn down over station, and walk up the bank, to Barkisland Church. At Elland turn over Bridge to Brighouse 7, Bailiff Bridge, Wyke. At Wibsey Bank- foot turn right along Rooley-lane, from which you have a splendid view of Bradford ; through Dudley Hill out on to the Leeds new road. Stanningley, turn left down to Kirkstall, 14 (see the fine old Abbey) up to Headingley and Chapeltown or Chapel Allerton as it is called on the maps. You are then on the Leeds and Harrogate high road, which is of splendid surface as a rule. The run from Kirkstall is 15 miles,through Harewood and Spacey Houses. WESLEYAN CYCLING CLUB. By the kind permission of Mrs. Park Yates, 30 members were allowed the privilege of spending Saturday evening in the grounds of Ince Hall, Chester. A quick run before an easterly wind brought the club to Cornhill Farm, at Dunham Hill, where Mrs. Black- burne catered for them in her usual satisfactory style. Then the bye-roads were taken through Wimbolds Trafford and Thornton to the church at Ince. The roses were at their best, as the following day ware to be Rose Sunday at Ince, when everyone who comes is admitted to the grounds to see the display of blooms. After a couple of photographs had been taken before the hall the party remounted and reached the Stag Bridge before ten. Lamp time to-night, 9.31 ; next Saturday, 9.24.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <p>• EIFFEL.IOWER LEMONADE. This article is undoubtedly the most delicious. tool, refreshing drink obtainable, so healthful. to convenient, so cheap...7_4W. per bottla</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>CANVASSER'S DOWNFALL.</head>
        <p>A CANVASSER'S DOWNFALL.</p>
        <p>At Wigan on Tuesday Albert Bray, formerly a can- vasser, of Warrington, was sent to prison for fourteen days for stealing a porter's breakfast on the London and North-Western Railway. The defendant, it was stated, had called at a local public-house and had made a threat upon his life. He was afterwards found in the porter's room, his clothing being very wet. He was remanded for medical examina- tion and was stated to be sane. , .</p>
        <p>In.pector Wills said the prisoner had been drinking heavily, which had been the only reason for his dis- charge. HUSBAND THROUGH ADVERTISEMENT.</p>
        <p>At the Daresbury Petty Sessions. on Tuesday, before Mr A H Talbot (in the chair) and other magistrates, Robert Roberts of Walton Inferior, was summoned by his wife for persistent cruelty. Mr Steel appeared for the complainant and Mr Amos (Messrs Robert Davies and Co.) defended. Complainant stated that she advertised fora husband through a newspaper and the result was her marriage to the defendant. The com- plainant was sixty-nine years of age and defendant,who was a gateman employed by the Ship Canal Company, was sixty-two years of age. The parties were married about two years ago. It was alleged that the cruelty, which commenced very shortly after their marriage, had been of a very brutal character. The defendant was constantly telling his wife she could clear out, as be did not want her. Some four months after they were married, defendant took the complainant by the throat and assaulted her in a very severe manntr. The defendant had also assaulted his wife on other occasions about six weeks ago threatening to " out her up and throw her into the canal." On June 21st defendant took his wife by the throat and in the struggle they fell to the floor. Defendant got up and struck his wife three times on the head and she was compelled t ) leave him. Defendant said he had never seized hold of his wife by the throat and tried to choke her. The trouble was all the complainant's fault —A separation order was granted with alimony of us. 2d. a week, defendant to pay the costs.</p>
        <p>the 0 aid OURTS-Wil</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>DANGEROUS MOTOR DRUVING.</head>
        <p>DANGEROUS MOTOR DRUVING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON DRIVER IN COURT. At the Warrington Police Court on Thursday before Mr J Rymer Young (in the chair) Messrs W Roberts, A J Jolley, W T Williams, E %Vood and Dr Naden, John Henley, taxi-driver, 163, Orford-lane, was summoned for driving a motor car to the danger of the publics at Bridge Foot on the 25th June.—Mr Steel appeared for the defendant and pleaded guilty.—P.C. Skelton said .that on the day in question he was on point duty at Bridge Foot when he saw a motor car E.D. 250 driven by the defendant, coming from the direction of Wilderspool Causeway at a speed danger- ous to the public. Be (witness) put his hand up for the defendant to slacken speed but he came on over the bridge at the same rate, nearly crushing him, the officer, between his car and a passing tram car. In reply to the charge the defendant had said " You put your hand up and told we to come on." Continuing the officer said the defendant had no need to go over the bridge at such a speed.— Mr Steel said that the defendant was coming down the Wilderspool- road when he saw the constable put his hand up and the driver thought it was a sign for him to come on and he hastened his speed to get over the bridge. It was an entire misunderstanding. He was a very steady driver and he, (Mr Steel) asked the bench to deal as leniently as possible with him.—The Chief Constable pointed out that drivers should take more care when coming over the bride, especially now when alterations were being made.—The case was dismissed on payment of costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>SATURIM.Y.—Before Mr R T Fairclough (in the chair), Alderman Tinnion and Messrs A J Jolley and T</p>
        <p>Pemberton. ASSAULT ON OLD LADY.—Emily Brown, Mersey- street was charged oo remand with being drunk and , disorderly on June 29th and also with an aggravaled assault cn Mary Burke.—The complainant, who is over 70 years of age, said she lived next door to the prisoner and she had remonstrated with her for assaulting her (witness's) sister. Brown threw a glass jug at her which struck her on the bead, and she had to be attended at the Infirmary.—Brown was sent to prison for six weeks. 1%10/co/Y.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Smethurst, Bolton, James Evans, Messrs S Wright, W Furness, G Pilling and H Thornton. A LOGGER'S THEFT.—Walter Edward Tomlin, of no fixed address, was charged with stealing on July 3rd from 18, Pierpoint-street, two watches, articles of jewellery, and Ma in cash, valued in all at £2, the property of Edward Reardon.—The.Chief Constable said that tbree weeks ago the prisoner came to War- rington and obtained lodgings at 18, Pierpoint-street. He got work at Bewsey Forge and was earning 30s to , 35s per week. He worked at the forge for a short time but absconded last week and the property was then missed. He was arrested in Liverpool on Saturday night. The Chief Constable asked for a remand 'for a week.—Prisoner said he took the Watch and chain but did not take 10s., it was 2s. 6d.—The prisoner was remanded for a week. A WINDOW SMASHER.—Peter Corroboy, 15, Ship- yard, was charged on remand with wilfully breaking a plate-glass window in the shop of Messrs Eustance and Co. on the 3rd lust and doing damage to the extent of £l6.—The Chief Constable said that on Wednesday the 3rd inst., at about 10 55 a.m., the prisoner was seen by ex-Sergeant Evans, to go up to the window and kick it with his clogs twice. He was about to kick it a third time but the ex-Sergeant prevented him. Damage was also done to the stock, in addition to the broken window. Corrahoy was committed for trial to the Quarter Sessions.</p>
        <p>TuEsnAy.—Before Councillor Roberts (in the chair) Colonel Edelsten, Councillor C. Jackson Holmes and</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Locker. CRUELTY TO A HORSE.—Joseph A. Williams, New- field Farm, Robin Hood-lane, Helsby, was summoned for having on June 25 caused a horse to be worked whilst in an unfit condition and John Hewitt, of the same address, was charged with working the animal.— Inspector Dee, K.S.P.C.A., said he stopped the horse, which was attached to a lurry, at Bridge Foot, on the morning of June 25th. The animal was very lame or the near fore-leg and was suffering from sprained tendons and over-shot fetlock joints.—Williams said he did not think the animal was suffering so much as the inspector had stated, as it was in splendid condition and well kept.—The Inspector said he saw the horse at Helsby on tbe following day and it was still lame.—A fine of 20s. was imposed to covr both cases. THURSDAY.—Before Mr J Rymer Young (in the chair)' Messrs W Roberts A J Jolley, E Woods, W T Williams and Dr. NaClen,</p>
        <p>A NI.7ISANCE.—Robert Shaw, Widnes, was fined Ns. for having conveyed offensive offal through Bridge- street on the 21st June. For the prosecution it was stated that the defendant left a lurry containing bones outside a butcher's shop for some time and several passers by complained of the nuisance. The defendant was advised by the bench to collect the bones from the back entries of the shops.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Bolton, Pierpoint and Smethurst, Messrs A J Jolley, T Locker, W Furness and S Wright. WAS THE JAM STOLEN ?—Benjamin Gaskell, no fixed abode, was charged with stealing on the previous day ft pot of jam the property of some person or persons unknown.—ln asking for a remand the Chief Constable said that about 12.10 the previous day. Detective Sergeant Reddiugton was in 13utterrnarket-street when he saw the prisoner coming in the opposite direction to which he (the officer) was proceeding. When he saw the detective the prisoner turned sharply round into Scotland-road. The officer observed that he had some- thing bulky in his pocket and when he caught up to the prisoner, he asked him what he had got in his pocket and liaskell said he had a pot of jam which he was taking to his mother in Fennell-street. The officer pointed out that he was going in the wrong direction for Fennell-street, and prisoner then said he had bought it from Altrincham. There was some straw in the prisoner's pocket, which looked as if he had taken the jam out of some case.—ln answer to the Chairman, the prisoner said he had bought the jam at Altrincham the previous day, and had set off at seven o'clock in the morning to come to Warrington,—The Chief Constable pointed out that that was a very early hour for shops to be opened.—Gaskell was remanded until Mon- day, the Mayor stating that if he was found innocent before then he was to be discharged.— Prisoner : "J am innocent."</p>
        <p>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS. TCESDAY—Before Messrs A H Talbot (in the chair), Roger C Parr, T Baxter and W Fletcher. TRESPASSING IN SEARCH OF GAME —John Lawton, a Warrington labourer, was fined 5s for trespassing on land in the occupation of Joseph Bradbury, at Moore, in search of game on July lst.—George Wright, Frederick Massey and Walter Harper, also Warrington men, were summoned for a similar offence on July 4th on land in the occupation of Samuel Battersby. The gamekeeper said he saw the three defendants with a greyhound and a dog running rabbits. The defendants denied the offence, and said the dogs were never out of the slips. A fine of 58. was inflicted on each defendant. THEFT OF CHANG E.—Micbael Smith, a farm labourer, was charged with stealing Ds 21d at Appleton on June 15th, the moneys of Elizabeth Britch, manageress of the Thorn Inn.—On the day in question she served the defendant and another man with two glasses of beer, for which the defendant gave her threepence. She also received an order from a man named Morton, who tendered half a sovereign. She put the change on the table in front of Morton and it was alleged that Smith picked it up and kept it. Morton afterwards complained that he had not had his change.—Smith, who denied the offence: was fined 10s.</p>
      </div>
