      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <p>American politics may THE appear mixed to many PRESIDENTIAL of us; but out of the CANDIDATES AND confusion one thing PROTECTION, stands clear. Both the</p>
        <p>candidates for the Presidency are dissatisfied with the great American tariff, which our Protectionists here hold up to us as America's salvation. President TAFT said on February. 12th that his party "has come to realise that high tariff duties encourage combinations of capi- tal by suppressing competition." That means simply that American Protection enables the Trusts to charge higher prices. President TAFT hopes to remedy this and other faults of the tariff. He confessed the other day that every tariff America has ever had " has been put together hit-or-miss." He desires a " scientific " tariff. Such a one, we suppose, as Germany has got. MR. J. ELLIS BARKER told us in May "Nineteenth Century" .that Germany's tariffs "are made to suit the Government's particular requirements," and are "largely shaped by party pressure." That is true in Germany. It is true in America. It would be true here. Let the other Presidential candidate speak on the point.</p>
        <p>DR. WOODROW WILSON, the Democratic candidate, is the candidate of a Free Trade party. One of the most popular public men in America, COLONEL HENRY WATTERSON, said a few weeks ago that "The Democratic party is a Free Trade party, or it is nothing. The conflict between Free Trade and Protection is irrepressible and must be fought to the bitter end."</p>
        <p>Recently in New York, DR. WOODROW WILSON said.that the tariff is a " huge make- believe." Read: "It begins to dawn upon the whole country that the tariff is no longer a statesman-like plan of protection, but a privately managed game for profits. A big game—a huge scheme—carried out through the votes of enormous numbers of men, who are deceived by the old phrases and do not face the new facts ; a game in which the powerful the subtle, the unscrupulous, are more likely to prevail than any others. It has become a huge make-believe. It stands as ugly and as full of unwholesome secrets and hidden places, to which the light has never penetrated, as an old Bastille." And read again: "The American working-man is beginning to see he was misled as to the genuine sources of prosperity and good pay. He now sees that the trusteeship resulted in enormous benefit to the</p>
        <p>few and a questionable benefit to the many. Evil, indeed, is a custom which allows that the spirit of paternalism should be so monstrously encouraged that a disposition should be created to look upon the Government as an instrument of gain instead of an instrument for the promotion of the well-being of all. We are rich enough safe enough in our prosperity, sure enough of our capacity, of our skill, of our resourcefulness to set ourselves free at last." These are the words of a man who knows that the American nation can never rise to her full height till she sets herself free from the greatest of all her bonds. "To me it appears," said the Grand Old Man of America the late John Bigelow, "to me it appears that' Protection is even more firmly fixed in its saddle than ever slavery was, because the property it gives to the protected every year exceeds the total value of the slave property of the United States when it was at its maxi-</p>
        <p>MUM. ' ,</p>
        <p>So clear is it that America wants more Free Trade and less Protection that in April an American Minister, the Secretary of the United States Treasury, declared that" Both parties are equally enlisted to revise the tariff downward. It is hard to see how any- one can doubt that Free Trade will ultim- ately be the policy and practice of this country (United States of America), and of all other enlightened nations. . . . It is simply impossible to conceive a free road to civilisation and human progress unless the tariff walls are eventually broken down." While they are breaking them down in the States, we in this country are being pestered to build them up. The warning of Germany and of America is writ large!</p>
        <p>We are sorry that his THE MAYOR AND Worship, the Mayor, SMOKE, has taken the criticisms</p>
        <p>of our correspondents anent the smoke nuisance in a resentful spirit. It should hardly be necessary for us to point out that we have printed no criticism which was personal to Dr. Joseph, except in his capacity of Mayor of the Borough. As our leading townsman his utterances naturally carry weight, and just as naturally we con- sider that they are open to honest public criticism. There is no question in the opinion of either our correspondents, or ourselves, that his Worship's pronouncements on the smoke nuisance and public health are unacceptable in the light of modern science and conviction. We give him full credit for holding a considered opinion on the question, and onethat cannot be lightly ignored but we reserve the right to criticise that opinion in all fairness and in the interest of what we claim to be the public welfare. In another column we publish an interestingand sugges- tive letter from Mrs. Higgs, of Oldham, who has earned widespread fame as a pioneer in the campaign against smoke, and whose views cannot be considered as other than authoritative. She makes it quite clear that the sins of omission in municipalities like Warrington are as black as the smoke.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Crosfield WHERE WAS received an enthusiastic</p>
        <p>HAROLD? welcome at the opening of the Old English Fair yesterday, but Thursday's proceedings were something like " Hamlet " might be without the Prince of Denmark. Where was the Member, who had been announced to open the fete? The chairman explained that Mr. Harold Smith M.P. was "engaged in London in the High Court, and therefore unable to come to Warrington. He had sent a telegram expressing great regret etc." This seemed a most adequate explanation, even to us, until we read in the Manchester Guardian" a glowing account of the old English tourney at Earl's Court on the same day, Thursday. Here Mr. F. E. Smith was a notable absentee, but "his place was taken by his brother." Harold seems to have been doing a little swash-buckling in armour a la Macbeth, when he should have been wearing morning dress and a proud smile at the great fete in his own constituency. The confusion between the High Court and Earl's Court seems to call for an explanation from the Member for Warrington. To complete the story we will quote what our distinguished and reliable Manchester contemporary has to say in yesterday's account of the tourney: "After the tourney was done there came a mell between ten knights on horseback, when they laid on to hack the plumes off one another's helms. Earl Winterton took part in this, but Mr. F. E. Smith, who it was th ought would put in an excellent piece of legitimate swashbuckling, was not present, and his place was taken by his brother. This was a furious and exciting event, and one of the champions was thrown from his horse and had to be carried off on a litter for the</p>
        <p>attention of two !among surgeons who were</p>
        <p>present in the lists." Unfortunately the name of the injured champion is not disclosed, but we hope Nemesis has not overtaken the member for Warrington thus inopportunely. We hope to see him laid low in Warrington!</p>
        <p>It is but a short time THE SWAN AND since we had occasion THE MAN WITH to comment upon the THE GUN. barbarous impulse which invariably = prompts some "man with a gun" to slay any interesting stranger—animal, bird or fish— which happens to visit this locality. Last time a porpoise was the victim of this mis- placed marksmanship. This week a swan has suffered a similar fate. A few months ago two swans which were doing considerable damage to the trout in the lake in Bank Park ware removed to the river near Victoria Park. After having been carefully penned up, watched, and fed for several weeks, they were liberated and speedily adapted themselves to their new haunts. They became great favourites with dwellers in the neighbourhood and the girls employed at the Florence Mills made a daily custom of taking scraps from their meals to the river side and the birds, which had become quite tame, promptly came to the bank to be fed. One of the swans would even answer to the name of" Peter" which had been bestowed upon it. The men employed on the flats alongside Howley warehouse were also greatly interested in the birds. At about 5 o'clock on Sunday morning the birds were up the river near Dobby's Locks where a fresh stream enten the Mersey. A man was seen deliberately to take aim with a gun and shoot poor " Peter " whose lifeless body was soon afterwards found float- ing down the river. Unfortunately the identity of the miscreant is unknown but we are pleased to see that the Mayor has offered a reward of £2 for information that will lead to his conviction. It is to be hoped that the perpetrator of the wanton and dastardly act will soon be discovered and made to pay the penalty for his crime—for such it undoubtedly is.</p>
        <p>- -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <p>Last week we brietlY A TORY REBUFF commented on the cora' AND SOME OF THE plete fiasco whiell CAUSES. of wsey the,attended nt meeting not g in4Be Junior e c effortson s e atives to bold a open-air Ward. Theywere practically hooted from the field, and took their defeat in very poor spirit as the following quotation from the local "Tariff Reform :Gazette " indicates:</p>
        <p>If you want to see the kind of people who prise and support the Radical Party you shomu Street, attend a Conservstive Meeting in Catherine some evening. The language that comes, fr°l these ignorant individuals on these occasons I enough to make even strong men tremble, allus ' this from the lips of children in the presence 01- their parents. We remember Miss Crosfield, si9ter of the Radical Candidate, saying on her rears from Germany a few years ago that the 3"131 and children of Warrington were dirty slatternly, but we did not realize the force cf statement until we saw the Radicals over ilewse"ci She had evidently been down the street mentions to gain her information. It is so terrible that 016 . cannot even imagine without seeing Yet thl are the people who profess to support the right ° free speech, free trade, and certainly fre''',„ ignorance. A considerable amount of temper an 21,98 equal lack of "breeding" are:displayed by tt writer of the above precious paragraPn• - Why should the people of Bewsey, or of ani other district listen to the vulgar abuse ° speakers from a public platform whethel? Tory or Liberal? They have the rights free men and women and free citizens, and they heard things from the One too delicate mouths of Junior Conservative° which roused a very just resentment. Th.! chief speaker on this occasion commenced b, an attem-ot to" show cause" against lionlet Rule. I-I:is principal argument? was tbt , the Irish were "a race of cowards who fouPe , against us in South Africa." As there , more than a few people of Irish descent in t'il hitherto patient audience such a f°llo. slander provoked a storm, and it was not 10°,° before the speaker and is friends were hasty and ignominious retreat. The sponsol' of this kind of gutter politics retaliate bit calling the people of Bewsey " ignoran individuals," and their women and children "dirty and slatternly." What an exhiblt,i,°ll2 of manners from "the gentlemanly party</p>
        <p>The Tory Press 1191‘ WHISTLING FOR A been loudly expressly( —FOG. its confident expecta' tions that the 50her0 of National Insurance ,will be inaugurat amidst wild confusion. Unless the Vr.„ Party have totally :renounced the Plato'11" affection which they formerly professed fc? the principles of National Insurance, etc_ notion of the correct attitude for the Gover ment which desires to put that principle iri""k. practice is the attitude of the centipede sc/' when asked which leg he put forward 61 in walking, rolled himself up into a ball consider the problem at leisure. -4-% great measure of social reform 51.1.1'il in its working give rise to large number of financial and administrati/ questions that can only be solved as arise. In the case of National Insurance I is true that there has been an unusv</p>
        <p>element of congestion for the depe. which is responsible for its workili_, factitious element. The Tory Press ha. reminded all the busybodies who persistent fill the waste-paper baskets of our ne'</p>
