      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>LOOKING ALONG THE FOOTWAY.</head>
        <p>LOOKING ALONG THE FOOTWAY.</p>
        <p>[Photos, Burrell. Alderman Bennett yesterday secured, for a consideration, the privilege of being the first passenger over the bridge. He ran some danger of providing a missing Alderman.</p>
        <p>After Warrington Bridge, the level crossings! We note that the Street Improve- ment Committee has instructed the Borough Surveyor "to report upon all possible alternative methods of constructing a new road to avoid the level cr. )ssing at Wilderspool." If there is no way of abolish- ing the railway crossing, it seems to us that r. Longdin has got a stupendous problem before him. He has, however, the chance of earning undying fame by finding a practic- able solution of the difficulties that beset the crowds of road-users on this busy thoroughfare.</p>
        <p>Musical Successes, The following pupils of Miss M. Jennings, A.V.C.11., 142, Wilderspoolsroad, pissed very successfully at the recent examination in connection with the Victoria College of Music, Loud on, Dr. Lewis the examiner complimenting the cat,didates on their fine technique. Senior grade, piano, James Whittingham, 43, Lovely- lane. 85 marks, let class with honours ; intermediate grade, piano, Hilda Baker, Penketh, 89 marks, let class with honours ; junior grade. piano, Evelyn Fraser 69, Gaskell-street, Stockton Heath, SS marks Ist class with honours, she having gained a honours certificate last year %Aith 94 marks and the year previous 90 marks. She is DOW only 10 years of age. The Plctueedrome. Another capital progiamme 1 as been submitted at this popular home of amusement tiring the week and at the matinee tn-day tle find represetration of the Walking day procession will take place. There will also be nutnenus other attractions. For next week the management have been fortunate in securing two exceptionally good star films in addi,ion to a host of other excellent plays. For Monday the chief film will be " Her Sumeme Sacrifice " and for the rest of the week from Thursday " Nemesis " will occupy the top of a good bill. DRESS RASKE7S, 193 Gd., 214. Cd., 245. 6d., special value in Hand Baas, rrunks, Sint cases. etc.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>* " * We are not sure that the most pertinent comment on the Triangular Politics now in full blast at Hanley and Crewe is not to be found in Mr. Punch's principal cartoon. There we have the "Electorate" Cow. The Master of Elibank has her by the head, Mr. Ramsey MacDonald by the tail whilst the Unionist candidate with a contented smile is doing the milking: LIDERA', WHIP : My COM I think. LABOUR PARTY LEADER: My cow, I think. UNIONIST CANDIDATE : My chance, anyhow. It is just possible that the happy Unionist may find the milk rather sour * *</p>
        <p>Pencil Society's Exhibition. The people of Warrington are favoured in having on view at the Museum until July 27th a fine collection of drawings by member s of the Pencil Society,London. Nearly 100 drawings have been hung and they are so varied in subjects and execution that an insrection is well repaid. To Warringtonians the works of Hedley Fitton, R.E., a former student of our School of Art,are of special interest. They are very striking pictures, the titles being " The two mills," " London Bridge," " Site of the County Council Hall, Westminster Bridge-road." " The Christ of St. Maclou. Rouen," 'The old Savoy Chapel, London. ' and " Rue Saint Romain, Rouen." Other artists whose drawings are exhibited are David Wilson, Steven Spurrier, R 0.1., William Hatherell, RI., Frank Gillett, R. 1., James Paterson, R.S.A., R.W.S., Joseph Simpson, R.8.A., Gilbert Holiday, Sir Charles Holroyd, R.E , C Ross Burnett, R. 1., George Belcher, J A Shepherd, A Carruthers G tuld, R.8.A., Cecil King, H R Mibar; Arthur Garratt and J D Ferguson. Death Under Chloroform.</p>
        <p>* - The Government has decided to get Parlia- ment up early in August, and to start the Autumn sitting early in October. The Prime Minister announced yesterday what the business would be until the adjournment. After the Reform Bill has been read a second time to-morrow, there will be fifteen days of Parliamentary time in the next three weeks. These will be spent as follows: Supply (six days) and Apppropriation Bill. Indian Budget. Trade Union Bill (second reading). Finance Bill. Supplementary Naval Estimate (one day July 22nd). It need hardly be said that the Opposition grumbled at the amount of work to be done, and in particular wanted more time for the Finance Bill. As to that Mr. Asquith said: "By all means." But every extra day spent on the Finance Bill means one day less of holiday in early August.</p>
        <p>On inquiry into the citcumstances i.urrruncling the death of Richard Holbrook (41), of Heath-place, was conducted by Mr. S. Brighouse at Warrington on Tuesday.—Mr. Jenkins (instructed by Messrs. Henry Greensll and Co.) appeared for Messrs. Pearson and ' Knowles, Ltd.- From the evidence it transpired that about ten weeks ago the deceased crushed his finger while working at Dallam Forge. On Saturday morn- ing he attended at the Infirmary for an operation, and he died under an anesthetic.—Dr. Bishop, senior house surgeon, said D. . Evans gave the deceased the anwithetic, but before he could be placed on the operating table he died. He afterwards made a post- mortem examination, and in his opinion death was caused by heart failure, due to an alcoholic heart and accelerated by chloroform. —Dr. Evans said before administering the anesthetic he sounded the heart of the deceased, and found nothing to suggest that the deceased might die under the antesthetio.—Dr. Bishop, on behalf of the staff of the Infirmary, and Mr. Jenkins, cu behalf Messrs. Pearson and Knowles, expressed their sympathy with the relatives of the deceased.— A verdict of "Death from misadventure" was returned.</p>
        <p>LOCAL PARS.</p>
        <p>The Infirmary. The Hon. Treasurer gratefully acknowledges the receipt from Lady Greenall of a cheque value £3O as a donation to the funds of the institution.</p>
        <p>Eve!yn-street School. The Evelyn-street Council School will be opened on Thursday, August 15th, by Councillor Henry Roberts. •</p>
        <p>Personal. Miss E. Bennett, pupil of Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus.B. has been successful in passing the senior pianoforte examination Trinity College London. Music In the Park. - • -</p>
        <p>BABY CARRIAGES, special value at 395. Gd. Go-carts from 10s. 6d.—J. t..-'. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. _</p>
        <p>Visit of the Rey. J. D. Jones, M.A., B.D. It will be seen in an advertisement in this paper, that the above named minister is to occupy the pulpit of Wycliffe Church on Sunday next, at both services. His visit is gladly anticipated by the Wycliffe people, for they are all well acquainted with his high reputa- tion as a preacher of the first rank. He is pastor of the Richmond Hill Congregational Church in Bourne- mouth, which seats 1,200 people, and is attended by a congregation which crowds the Church. There is a membership of nearly 800, and it has a staff of 22 lay preachers. Mr. Jones is an es-chairman of the Con- gregational Union of England and Wales, and in eon- Junction with the Rev. C. Sylvester Horne, M.P., of Whitefields, London, he has made himself worthily conspicuous as a preacher in the Congregational body. He is thoroughly abreast of the times, and in earnest sympathy with every Evangelical movement. His power and fame as a preacher of the gospel place him in the very front rank of present day preachers, and War- rington people, of all denominations, will be richly repaid, who may find their way to either or to both Wycliffe services on Sunday next. He is kind enough to say that he desires by this visit to testify his regard for the retired Pastor. Mr. Yonge has known him as a friend from the days in which he was student at Lancashire Independent College and Victoria University.</p>
        <p>The Blue Coat S;1;nOl hand will plßy a selection of popular rcusic in the Park on Thursday evening July 18th at 7.15. FIRE GRATEs.—if you want a new grate our large and varied stock will interest you.—J. S. GARLAND, Tronmonger,Bridge at, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Photographic Society. Another successful outing arranged by this society took place on Saturday when, under ideal weather conditions, about 15 members and friends explored Cuddington, emaking goon use of their cameras on the many beauty spots around the district.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Students at Warrington Classes. The Cheshire Education Committee have granted a capitation grant of 103 Es. to the borough of War- rington on account of students from the administrative county of Chester who have been in attendancs at the borough evening classes during the session 1911-1912. Death Of Mr. John Pierpoint. The death is announced of Mr John Pierpoint youngest son of the late Mr and Mrs B L Pierpoint, of Wan ington, which took place at Toronto on June 22nd. He went to Canada nearly three years ago, and had since been cc nnected with the firm (if Wm. Rennie and Co., Ltd., the largest s-ed house in the Dominion, whose head office is at Toronto. He was 32 years of age and bad made many friends not only in Warrington but in his new home in Canada and his death is deeply regretted. TENIVIS, Croquet, Cricket and other outdoor gamer.— J. S. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. ' Child Burnt to Death. Mr. S. Brighouse conducted an inquest at the Warrington Coroner's Court on Tuesday, on the body of Herbert Cecil Saulkin, aged 20 months.—The mother of the deceased, who resides in Elaine-street, said that while she was attending to the fishmonger on Saturday afternoon her son's clothes become ignited. A neighbour smelled something burning and witness ran into the house and found the child in flames. She wrapped the rug round him and the flames were extinguished. Ile was taken to the Infirmary, where he died later in the afternoon. The only way she could account for the accident was tliat a box of matches must have been left on the gas stove, and her son must have been playing with them. There was no fire, and some charred remains of matches were found on the hearth rug. A verdict of 'Accidental death" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>RAILWAY TRAFFIC RECEIPTS.</head>
        <p>RAILWAY TRAFFIC RECEIPTS.</p>
        <p>The official returns of the fifty-two principal railways in the United Kingdom for the week ended July 7th, the first week of the hulf-year, as published by the " Railway Times," show total receipts amounting to £2,492,706 an increase of £141,234, as compared with the corresponding week of last year, while the mileage was 21,53 a, or 21 more than for the corresponding week. The total receipts were at the rate of £ll5 14s lid per mile of line open, an increase of of £6 9s. id. a mile as compared with last year. Thirty-four of the returns distingnish between the receipts from passenger and goods traffic, and these show an increase of £3,297 (or 2s. per mile) in the passenger receipts, and an increase of £132083 (or £6 5s id per mile) in the receipts from goods traffic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>RARE ORCHID FOUND IN KENT.</head>
        <p>RARE ORCHID FOUND IN KENT.</p>
        <p>Two fine specimens of the rare lizard orahid have been found on the chalk hills at Harriet sham (Kent). Until a year or two ago the species was believed to be extinct.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. B. Boyd Carpenter was on Thursday night adopted ss prospective Unionist candidate for the Western Division of Bradford, in opposition to the sitting Labour member, Mr. F. W. Jowett. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>SMOKE.</head>
        <p>SMOKE.</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR REPLIES TO HIS CRITIC.</p>
        <p>A VIGOROUS REJOINDER.</p>
        <p>The Mayor "(Councillor Dr. Joseph) took occasion at the monthly meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday evening to reply to the criticism by an "Examiner " correspondent of his views on the smoke nuisance. Speaking on the minutes of the Health Committee, his WorShip asked the indulgence of the Council while he alluded to a personal matter. It was •in relation to a communication to a certain section of the local Press—a section by which he had, until recently, been treated with the utmost courtesy. He felt, however, that, in his capacity as Mayor, his position had been attacked and on that account he craved their indulgence for a short time. The criticism to which he referred was an anonymobs communication which appeared in last Saturday's " Examiner" under the heading "Smoke.." Of course, one did not expect a very high form of ethics to be associated with anonymous letters or communications, but the inaccura- cies and mis-statements in this case were such that he would be justified, if he were to use plain Saxon, in stigmatising them with a word of one syllable. The most convenient way would be for him to criticise the points in the letter which he had underlined. He did not know whether any of the Council had read it—</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON: No, I never read it. (Laughter). The MAYOR: lam sorry if you have not, because you would better understand my feelings. Continuing, his Worship said he would first of all appeal to the Council. They knew what he had said about smoke and his position had always been perfectly plain. He had always said that a manufacturing town like Warrington could not be expected to be as free from smoke as a Cathedral city or non-manufacturing town, and until some economical, scientific means were devised to minimise or dd away entirely with smoke, they ought to exercise a certain amount of broad-minded tolerance. That was a definition of the position he had taken up all along and he did not recede from it one iota. Having given that definition, he would just read a few extracts from the anonymous communication. He might say he had not treated the matter in an anonymohs manner ; he had always spoken openly in that Council or somewhere else about it. The communication stated "A correspondent calls our attention to the statement made by Dr. Joseph, the Mayor, in the discussion in the Council on the smoke nuisance, that it is not desirable to discourage smoke on the ground that the sulphuric acid produced is a valuable disinfectant and specially</p>
        <p>useful in killing the germs of diphtheria." He (the Mayor) never mentioned sulphuric acid, but he did mention sulphurous acid. Sulphuric acid existed only in very infinitesimal quantities in dense black smoke and, if it did, in the presence of heated carbon such as recently evolved smoke was mainly composed of, it lost part of its oxygen and became sulphur- ous acid. He never said sulphurous acid was especially useful in killing the germs of diphtheria. He said it was sueful as a disinfectant to destroy the germs of scarlet fever or small pox, but he never mentioned diphtheria in that connec- tion. He did mention diphtheria as being prevalent and he also mentioned by the way that it was a singular fact that the smoky waids were most free from diphtheria, but he did not venture to enunciate a fact which he was not in a position to. The communication talked about him as "the worthy doctor " damning him with faint praise and that kind of thing all through. It further stated : " The amount of sulphuric acid evolved with the smoke is no evidence of imper- fect combustion, and has practically nothing to do with it." He never touched upon that point so he would not say anything about it. They all knew that different samples of coal might have different constituents, varying slightly, and that was all that point dealt with. The writer next referred him to the opinion of Dr. Niven, medical officer of health for Manchester. He (the Mayor) had a high respect for Dr. Niven as medical officer of health_ The communica- tion added that Dr. Niven was acknow- ledged to be a high authority upon smoke. "I don't know whether he is, and I don't care," interjected his Worship. Dr. Niven, it was stated, " attributes most of the irrita- tion, pulmonary congestion and high death rate in periods of fog to the acids which attach themselves to the moisture and the soot in the atmosphere that is breathed. " Well, fog was not smoke. It was a compound —a very mysterious and dangerous compound in some cases—but it was not smoke such as we got Warrington. The communication went on :</p>
        <p>"A further health point we think the doctor has overlooked is the production through imperfect combustion of carbon- monoxide, which is a poisonous gas with neither taste nor smell." He agreed that carbon-monoxide was a poisonous gas with very little taste or smell. He thought the quantity of carbon-monoxide in smoke was very, very infinitesimal and that, singular to say, was converted rapidly into carbon-dioxide, which was negatively poisonous—that was to say, that if they had nothing but carbon-dioxide to breathe, they would die. It would kill them just as quickly as the most virulent poison, because it was irrespirable. Carbon-mon- oxide in the smoke as they got it in Warrington practically did not exist and need not be considered, although it was mentioned as a great bogey to frighten him and other people. (Laughter). "When combustion is perfect," con- tinued the writer "carbon-dioxide (car- bonic acid gas) is produced instead of the gas which is deleterious to health. The CO 2 is a comparatively innocent gas ; we inhale it every time we drink crated waters." It was innocent, commented the Mayor, if they did not get too much of it. The com- munication next stated : "We would respectfully suggest to the worthy doctor that he should study this question a little more closely." With regard to that, he had only to say that when he was medical officer of health for Warrington something over thirty years ago, he was considered worthy to be placed on a committee and to be asked to give evidence before a Smoke Commission.He was not called because his evidence was not required, but the fact that he was invited gave him some ground for saying that he had studied the question thirty years ago, if he did not know anything about it now. He could say very seriously that alt through his life it was a matter in which he had taken great interest. To tell him to study it was nothing short of impertinence. The writer spoke of a gentle- man of the medical profession "defending the</p>
        <p>production of smoke from a health point of view." When did he (the Mayor) do that? If they remembered the definition he gave of the position he had taken up, they would see the statement was palpably wrong. He had never recommended smoke as an absolute benefit, but he said they ought to make the best of it in every possible way, and that we could not be without it under existing circumstances without damaging trade. He looked at the question in a broad-minded light. He never said for one moment that smoke was actually a good thing, but he did say that, like a certain personage whom they all despised and condemned," it was not so black as it was painted." The communica- tion concluded</p>
        <p>"What effect is it likely to have upon the manufacturers to know, or have reason to suppose, that the first citizen of the borough, who is a medical man, appears to be of the opinion from a health point of view that the more smoke they fail to consume and turn into the air the better ?" The grammatical construction of that was somewhat doubtful, because it appeared to mean that the more smoke they failed to consume and failed to turn out, the better. (Laughter.) He appealed to the Council's sense of fairness as to whether he had taken up that attitude. Had he ever said that the more smoke turned out by the manufacturers, the better for the town? (" No.") He had omitted to mention the fact that he was put down as one of the greatest enemies to c)eanline.s and sanitation— especially among the poor, he presumed—that Warrington had. He appealed to them as to whether they thought that was correct. (" No.") He was sorry to have taken up their time, and probably to have.excited some impatience. (`' No, no.") But he could not, in justice to himself, to the office he held, and to the town of which he had the honour to be the first citizen, remain silent under such a virulent and unwarranted attack. "It is unwarranted and unwarrant- able: were his Worship's concluding words._</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON: Take no n-otice of them. They are not worth reading. (Laughter). When the mihutes of the Electricity and Tramways Committee were submitted, Alderman BENNETT said he had noted with pleasure the resolution of the committee to reduce the charges for electricity for power purposes. He was not going to attempt to discuss the smoke question, but one of the most hopeful lines of advance in that matter was to provide alternative methods of power. The more the Gas Comtnittee and the Electricity Committee would cheapen their supplies for purposes of that character, the better it would be for everyone concerned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>JOB FOR POLICE.</head>
        <p>A JOB FOR POLICE.</p>
        <p>MRS. HIGGS (OLDHAM) MAKES A SUGGESTION.</p>
        <p>[To THE EDITOR "EXAMINER. "J</p>
        <p>Sir,—Having read the correspondence in the Warrington Press on the Smoke Ques- tion I am reminded of pleasant visits to War- rington in connection with the Beautiful Warrington Society. I trust this society is influencing your town policy in the direction'of the creation of open spaces, garden suburbs,and last and not least, smoke prevention. In Oldham we are at a disadvantage owing to the fact that 1/3 more spindles have been added during recent years and have increased the call for power, but the general consensus of opinion is that the efforts of our Council have led to decrease of smoke in spite of increase of chimneys. Flowers will grow that formerly would not, the Corporation has even fined its own electricity works chimney, and the employment of an expert inspector (instead of Health Inspectors), and the decrease in the time limit for black smoke certainly led to improvement. The Stoker's Union was also stirred by interest in their calling to form a library of books on the Prevention of Smoke, and to aim at a good record for their members. One of its members assisted at the formation of the National Smoke Abatement League, through which legislation is hoped for. We should go far to solve the smoke question if the " shovel engineer" was a recognised craftsman. There is a key to every problem. What is the key to the smoke trouble? Every problem is thought insoluble till solved. Especially is it necessary to solve this one on which converge so many lines of public and private interest ? It is against the interest of the manufacturers to make smoke and certainly it is against the interest of the com- munity to have to endure it. Clean skies, clean houses, clean clothes,who can say what we pay as an unconscious tax when we lose them. The money value of extra paint, clothing, doctor's bills, hospitals, holiday trips, &amp;c., &amp;c., would astonish us if we realised it. The tax on time and temper,and the physical and the moral deterioration caused by uncleanliness, we cannot realise. Some day it will be written by the historian, "The nineteenth and early part of the twentieth century witnessed an almost incredible folly. The use of steam generated by the combustion of coal provided men with the power of swift production. This com- bustion instead of being scientifically con- trolled was extremely wasteful and resulted in the emission of unconsumed fuel through tall brick chimneys, which fuel was gradually precipitated liking falling snow upon the surrounding inhabited areas, which tracts of- land were covered by cities from which beauty had departed. The air was charged with fine particles of carbon, dust, and acid fumes, which produced and aggravated numerous lung and throat troubles. Houses, faces, and clothes were begrimed, vegetation sickened and perished." My interest in the smoke problem in Oldham led me to consider the position and interests of the stoker—or, as he loves to be called at least in Oldham, the "shovel iengineer." He has a right to the name. It is a mistake to think any Tom, Dick or Harry can feed the mechanical giants on whom depend so many hundreds of workers. Stoking should be a skilled trade. In many American cities no one is allowed charge of a boiler without a certificate of efficiency. This hangs in the boiler-house and is continually supplemented by records of smoke prevention. The stoker is to some extent a public servant. Picture to yourself at the foot of eath tall chimney one or more men presiding over the giant fires, as an engine driver does over his own iron horse. Kindle pride in the profession, better its con- ditions, put a premium on skill, make arrangements for fair hours, treat the man as a man. Thus you will go far towards solving the smoke question. It is a service of danger, it is exhausting, and it calls for high intelli- gence. It is as much to the public interest to have a good stoker as a good policeman. Competency, honesty, and knowledge go hand in hand. But how can we get trained firebeaters, if we work them 13 hours a day, often with Sunday work, paying a standing wage without overtime? The effectiveness</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <p>of stoking depends largely on the human instrument, and the conditions of labour are such as make it .a "sweated industry," not as regards actual wages, which average 275. to 30s. per week, but as regards payment per hour. A foolish plan used to prevail of giving a bounty per iton of coal consumed. This t''has been done away with, but not. replaced by a bounty on coal consumption such as exists in the case of many railway engineers. In this case a normal coal con- sumption is fixed, and they receive a bounty - on every ton by which they can reduce con- sumption while keeping up steam. Such a. bounty would encourage good firing. Another American expedient for keeping down smoke. is worthy of mention. Until the world is ideal we must have inspection. But the question is who is to inspect? Smoke inspection does not depend wholly- on "taking observations" on black smoke. Neverthless the idea of spreading observa- tions over a number of men is a good one, and in America they have hit on the right ". unemployed man'' to do it. A policeman's life is somewhat monotonous: He has to be out at all hours (before inspectors are out of bed.) He has to keep his eye open and observe many things, but he soon gets a little weary of "the daily round."- Therefore in some American cities they give him -What really must add to the interest of a policeman's life. All the police in the town are on the job. A man carries his notebook and takes " observations " at all hours, seasonable and unseasonable. It is impossible to corner the whole police. A man has only to keep his "weather eye" open to spot any particularly sinning chimney, and report. This smoke inspection, however, is not all with a view to exposing evil-doers. It also :rewards those who do well. Every firebeater who has kept his chimney within legal bounds receives once a quarter a merit note. At the end of a year if he has four merit notes he receives a certificate, which is added to the framed charactbr that hangs in the engine house, and these form a gradually accumulating record of efficiency the man is. proud not to break. Bent Cottage, Oldham.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>MARY HIGGS.</head>
        <p>MARY HIGGS.</p>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. " Foiled. by a Woman."</p>
        <p>This drama appers to have been designed chiefly for the purpose of enabling the heroine to execute a bewildering succes-iou of quick changes, and thus to foil the villain at every turn. There are plenty of hair-raising incidents and the climax provides something unique in the shape of a melee in which shooting and wrestling are pleasantly combined. 'Miss Hilda Burnley gives a spirited and popular portrayal of Sylvia Cat,evv, the hay of many guises, who is always on the spot when the villain menaces her lover. Miss Maiy Brarnmer has an unattfactive role as Florence andyke, the disappointed and revengeful one, but she noes justice to it by clever and convincing acting. She has an unscrupulous accomplice in Michael Blaydon, NI ell imneisonated by Mr. James Garrett. Mr. Cecil W. Parke is a manly, dashing Harold Seward and Mr. Jack Armitage does capable work as his ill-fated friend Charlie Travis. All the other parts are well-played and the mounting of the play is good. The orchestra, under Mr. Torn Boothroyd's conductorship, plays some choice , selections in capital style. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. Next week B. M. Fox's drama "The price she paid" will be presented twice nightly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>CRIPPLED CHILD WALKS.</head>
        <p>CRIPPLED CHILD WALKS.</p>
        <p>Amazing Cure of Infantile Paralysis by DR. CASSELL S TABLETS.</p>
        <p>Mothers will understand the joy of Mrs. Annie Austin, of 32 Albert Road, Long Eaton, when her little paralysed baby girl was restored to strength of limb, and bright, laughing health, by Dr. Cassell's Tablet's. It is a wonderful story, but only one of many wonderful stories called forth from grateful people everywhere by the supreme nerve and body- building power of this truly great remedy. Mrs. Austin says :—" One day I found that my little Doris could not stand. All use had gone out of her left side. We had a doctor, and another - eight altogether —but none of them 'did any good. They said it was Infantile Paralysis. We took her to the Children,s Hospital, then a General Hospital, then a Special Hospital for Paralysis, in London, but she was pronounced incurable. We spent a little fortune try- ing to save our child—in vain. Her leg was abso- lutely useless, she couldn't even move her toes, and it ' wasted to an appalling thinness. Then came Dr. Cassell's Tablets and—cure. In an incredibly short I time I thought there was movement, soon there was no doubt about it, and in a few weeks my child was ab,nd again, active and bright. That was two years ago, and she has never given me a moment's anxiety since.</p>
        <p>his the healing and strengthening force in Dr. Cassell's Tablets that effects such cures. All chemists and stores sell Dr. Cassell's Tablets at l J and 2/9; trial size, 101 d.</p>
        <p>GOLF.</p>
        <p>THE ACADEMY CUP. RESULT OF THIRD ROUND. E J Hall beat E Wharton. A Bottomley beat J M Murray. W Ricbardson beat J E Birtles. J Fairhurst beat L A Voisey. WARRINGTON. The fourth round for the captain's cup was played on Saturday, A Knowles qualifying in the first division and G W Brooke in the seoond. Scores :</p>
