      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>tAT I RIGHTS RESERVED.]</head>
        <p>tAT I RIGHTS RESERVED.]</p>
        <p>THE ROMANCE OF A PROMISSORY NOTE.</p>
        <p>BY LAURA KINGSCCTE Author of "Pauline's Dilemma," "St. Cecilia," kc.</p>
        <p>"The Cottage, Rottingdean, "December 27th, 188—. "DEAR BROWN,—I want to atone to you and the other members of the A.M.C. for my dastardly desertio* of you all at our annual dinner last New Year's Eve, by which desertion, by the bye, I broke two of the initial rules of that same club, viz.: Ist, Never to be absent from its annual functions except on a matter of life and death; 2nd, Never to have any commerce with women or Jews. "I want you all three to dine with us on Neu Year's Eve, here at my 'cottage by the sea'; and when you have heard the authentic account of last New Year's Eve's adventure, and seen the 'woman' and heard the story of the 'Jew' in question, I am sure you will forgive my secession from the 'Anti-Matrimonial Club,' and per- haps be inclined even to revise the rules. "Dinner at seven; carriages for the last train (if you must catch it) at 12.45; but my port is old, and so is a small hotel on the promenade, where you can get good ac- commodation for the night. (I wish my cot- tage were large enough.)—Youre, gs ever, Jac Gwatem."</p>
        <p>Of course, Matthews, Carruthers, and I agreed when we met for lunch in the City that day that we would accept Graham's in- vitation, for we had learnt from sad experi- ence the year before that four are rattling good company while " three " are truly, ae well as proverbially, "none." Besides, we were all dying to see the "woman " whose charms had, at first sight, it was rumoured, converted our most bigoted anti-matrimonial- -Ist into an ardent Benedict. The allusion to the Jew still further whetted our curiosity, for Graham was as hotly anti- Semitic as any of us. Surely the "woman who had beguiled our poor friend to eat of the forbidden fruit of connubial happiness was not a daughter of Shem as well ,as of Eve! Heaven forbid! How then Mild we dine with her at seven?</p>
        <p>As we debated this over our chops, who should enter the restaurant but Graham himself!</p>
        <p>We had only met him a few times since the tragedy (from cur point of view) of his mar- riage, so felt slightly constrained. He to- vanced smiling, and said eagerly, as - grasped our hands: ' "You'll all come on the 31st?"</p>
        <p>A moment's hesitation, then I said. falter- ingly : " Ye-es—er—thanks. Shall be happy to—but—er—excuse me, old fellow, but she— your mife—is she. a Jewess? "</p>
        <p>For a moment Graham looked puzzled, then his brow cleared and he burst out into a hearty laugh. "'A Jewess'! Good gracious! No won- der you—" "All right, old man! So sorry!" we all hastened to say. " Shall, be delighted to come, of course. Expect us at seven."</p>
        <p>• . . . . . New. Year's Eve, seven o'clock, found us all sitting round Graham's little dining-table in his pretty "cottage by the sea" at Rotting. dean, Brighton. To say that the dinner was excellent. Graham's ten-months-old bride a pretty and charming hostess, and that we found five even better than our traditional four, would be to be very moderately what .we felt when, din- ended, we three anti-matrimonialists rose respectfully while Graham opened the door for his wife, and smilingly told her we should not be long before joining her in the draw- ing-room. I can only say for myself that whether it was the effect of Graham's 1870 port. or of a certain pair of bright hazel eyes unde,r a g.ringe of golden-brown hair, I felt distinctly inclined to question the wisdom of the second article in the rules of the A.M.C. "Now, boys, leave the table. Put your glasses on the mantelpiece, and your feet also, if you like, and light up," Graham said, setting us the example himself. I restrained myself till my cigar was fairly "going," and then said: "Now, then, old man, tell us your story. We quite understand -why you seceded from the A.M.C. The flesh is weak, and those hazel eyes and golden hair, of course—" I was not able to finish for the deafening laughter and cries of: "Oh, oh! so that's how the wind's blowing!" &amp;c. "All right, Brown," said our host, when order was restored, "I'll begin my story be- fore you further commit yourself. You all want to know what happened to me a year to- night, and I feel I owe you an explanation. I am proud enough to think it a romantic story, and should give it the title, 'Won 137 a Promissory Note.'" This sounded exciting, and we all remem- bered that there was a Jew involved ip the story.</p>
        <p>"-Go on!" we cried, simultaneously. "I was living in Brighton then, as you know," Graham continued. "and, being an irresponsible bachelor, indulged in the luxury of a first-class pass to London, going up at nine every morning and generally returning by the 5.40 express, which only stops twice—at Croydon and Redhill. "I had arranged to dine at the club with you all on New Year's Eve, as you know. "The previous Thursday, December 28th, a curious adventure befell me. "I had just 'taken my customary corner in the 5.40 at London Bridge Station, and tucked the rug round my legs, for it vas a bitter night, when the door opened brusquely, and a voice with a nasal twang exclaimed: " '4ust in time, thank Heaven !' "The speaker's accent betrayed him, and without the confirmation of his physique I knew him at once to belong to the ancient House of Israel.</p>
        <p>"In spite of my anti-Semitic prejudices, to which I was committed, as you fellows know, both by temperament and vow, I replied, with Christian civility: "'Yes, you are fortunate,' then withdrew into my rug. We steamed slowly out of the station.</p>
        <p>"He was an unpleasing spectaclet even for the unprejudiced. About sixty year of age, but grey and wrinkled at that. His law head was covered with a shock of grizzly hair, that fell in straggling locks about his fur- rowed forehead and over the shiny collar of his old green-black coat. Two fierce black, beady eyes looked out from shaggy, beetling eyebrows, and a prominent, beak-like nose leant down over the cruel purple slit of a mouth. "I was commencing some self-hypnotic tricks, taught me by a medical friend, which I usually practised on thee journeys, that I might seem the sooner to arrive at Brighton, when a strange sound caused me to open my eyes. My neighbour, who was sitting on the same side of the compartment as myself, was carelessly scattering papers, envelopes, and lett.es 'in all .krertions. as. with ,his Glad- stone bag on his knees, he dived among its contents as if seeking something difficult to find. "So nervous and violent were the efforts of his Lean, withered hands, that several papers Fere thrown along the seat till they lay close 10 me. "One, an open letter, written in a delicate feminine hand, caught my eye, k and held it as if fascinated. "By the way "—and here Graham sud• denif paused, shook the ashes out of his pipe, and, while he slowly refilled it, turned and faced us all, with a curious look in his eyes, as he continued—" there are certain moral and legal aspects of my ittory which you fellows may find rather troublesome to adjust cor- rectly. I should advise you all to reserve your judgments till you have heard the end of my story." (Like Graham, I modestly fol- lowed law, and felt an unusual thrill of ex- cited expectancy at his we,rds.) "What I would not have done under ordi- nary circumstances for fifty pounds I felt suddenly and unaccountably compelled to do that moment, viz., to read that letter—aye deliberately, and ender th 3 very eyes of its tesner.: too. It ran thus: "'6, West-street, Brighton, "'December 24th, 189-- 1 e" write to ask you to have mai.</p>
        <p>_ -</p>
        <p>ence us just a few weeks longer. It is impossible to pay you the hundred pounds due on January Ist. "'My poor father is paralysed mith fear and anxiety. The rheumatism in his bands has been so bad lately that be has not been able, as we hoped, to finish the literary work the payment for which would have enabled him to discharge his debt to you. We are deeply grateful for the two months' respite you have already given us, though 1 fear it was accorded under false hopes. Once for all, I must tell you that the alter- native condition you offer—that I should become your wife—is impossible. I cannot give my hand without my heart. Oh! sir, do you not see that it is against the laws of nature and of God? You are old enough to be my father; besides which, our respec- tive religions forbid it. "'Sir, have pity on us! If you were a follower of the meek and lowly Jesus of Na- zareth instead of the stern Jehovah of the Jews, you vcould know the blessedness of forgiving and shewing mercy. In the name of that Christ, I beg you to have pity on— Yours in terrible distress, JANET FALCONER.' "'Portia pleading with Shylock,' I mur- mured to myself; then I glanced nervously up, fearing to encounter those fierce black eyes under the bushy, beetling eyebrows. But no, Shylock had fallen back in his seat. Those terrible eyes were shut, so were also those cruel, thin, purple lips over the flowing iron- grey beard. I was heaving a sigh of relief at having escaped detection when I caught some thickly-muttered words:</p>
        <p>"(You Gentile dog! You pork-eater! Ah ! I love her, I 10-ye her! I'll have----' Here the words, half articulate, went off into a diminuendo of faint snore. Several more seconds I waited till the snore became more pronounced and deep, then I glanced again at the letter, and discovered near it an envelope, on which was the address:</p>
        <p>"'Aaron Samuelson, Esq., " The Ivies. " Redhill, "'Surrey.' "The old usurer, then, lived at !Whin. now seemed to remember having seen him oa the line before. A wild temerity seized me. I felt prompted by some super-conscious force within me. Without a single moral scruple I mechanically opened the envelope and took out a small folded paper; unfolded it—ah! just what I had expected--tan 1.0. U. for a hundred pounds, to be paid on the ensuing January Ist, which was now in two days' time. It was signed 'Cohn Falconer.' "A 'canny Scot' in the clutches of a still 'cannier Jew.' It was interesting. Ah! but it was terribly sad, too, for was not a woman's happiness at stake? How could she be saved? How? My hand shook as with ague as I put the promissory note into the envelope again. I looked up at the slumber- ing Shylock. Was it safe? Dare I? Was it right? Was I mad to do it? Quickly I put and answered the questions, then, still im- pelled by that powerful, invisible force, I swiftly—as MN iftly as my shaking fingers would let me, that is—and with ray heart jumping about in my chest till I nearly suf- focated, tore that envelope and its contents into twenty pieces; leant softly out of the open window, and saw them float out on the wind like snowflakes, till they were lost to sight. Then, as quickly, I caught up the let- ter and hid it in my breast-pocket, hardly knowing why I had not let it share the fate of the envelope. "I took out my handkerchief, wiped the perspiration from my forehead, and tried to think what next to do. We were nearing Croydon. Should I get out, and let the inno- cent victim of my crime find it out when he awoke and account for it as beat he could? I could not decide. Cold drops rose again on my forehead. Good Heavens! He was waking! There was no time to lose. His keen, dark eyes opened. He started. The train slowed up. " Redhill?' he inquired.</p>
        <p>"Instantly I saw my salvation. Yes,' I said, 'you must be quick; we only stay a minute Ilene. Allow me to help you., "As quickly as I dared, to avoid suspicion, I gathered together the scattered papers and letters, crushed them down into the bag, gave the old man his umbrella, opened the carriage door, and almost pushed him out. "Would a signboard or an inquiring pas- senger or some unfamiliar detail on the plat- form undeceive him? I trembled as I sank on to my seat. Would the train never start? Yes, it moved! I could not resist leaning out to cast a last look at that dishevelled old figure with the Gladstone bag at its feet as it stood in a confused, half-awake huddle (on the platform. "We were off, thank Heaven, at last, and I could now think calmly of what I had done. "Legally I was a criminal, of course— there were no two opinions about it." (You could have heard a pin drop at this point of the story.) "Morally I was right—l also felt certain of that. I don't profess to be very religious, as you fellows know" (here Graham smiled; somehow, we couldn't, as we didn't agree with the suggestion he made about him- self)—" but I took my stand that day on the side of things as God sees them, and not as man does." (Graham being a lawyer some people might think made it all the more wonderful.) "I sat in a moral and physical maze till we came to Redhill. This was Shylock's home; how should I be able to come up and down from Brighton every day without encounter- ing the old villain? And if I did, would he associate his lose in any way with the fellow. passenger who had played the trick upon him at Croydon Station? I tortured myself with this and similar questions till I was nearly at my destination. Then it occurred to me that the thing was not at an end as far as the victims of the old usurer were concerned. He would, of course, keep them to their pledge, not mentioning the fact of the loss of his only legal bond—the 1.0. U. I must lose no time in acquainting them with the fact. Of course, there was the other alternative. I might paT the hundred pounds myself; I was well able to do it without any great sacrifice. Here my humanity spoke, but worldly wisdom replied that so much a-as hardly necessary in the in- terest of mere strangers; at any rate, that must be the last resort, for the moment delay was all that was-needed. The creditor might finish that literature, and then—well, we should see!</p>
        <p>"The excitement of the journey had muddled my mental faculties and robbed memory of her customary accuracy, besides which it was nearly twenty years since I had ....a my Sllnkesneare so I sat in a confused state in the corner of the 'fiarrfage, vaguely wondering how Portia in the ancient story had settled the question of that pound of flesh and so saved her lover; and that set me thinking of the writer of the letter in my pocket. Perhaps she was beautiful and young! How splendid it would be to save her! I could see in imagination those cold, skinny fingers clutching a fair young body, and those thin purple lips pressing trembling and resisting red ones." (It was stranr how really prophetic Graham had been in his imagination of the fair Gentile!) "'The thought set my heart, a heart that had never known love, boys, beating wildly, and the blood in my head—the very head that had helped to formulate the rules of the 'Anti-Matrimonial Club '—madly whirling. Fact is, I forgot all about the -A.M.C., and you fellows, and everything else, except that I•must save that woman from the clutches of this old bloodsucker." (And he had not then seen those hazel eyes and golden hair. Dear me! I could never have imagined that Graham had so much imagination or such warm humanity.) "To make the story short." Graham went on, •• I determined to see both 'lather solo daughter the very next day and tell them everything. "But next day brought fresh business, and I did not get back to Brighton till late at night. "The following day was the 31st and New ' Year's Eve; the poor journalist's debt was due the very next day. There was no time to belt. "As I walked down Wcst-street to catch an early train to town, in order that I might re- turn earlier in the afternoon. I looked up at the windows of No. 6. The blinds were down, but as I half stonped a moment by the gate I saw a hand pulling tip one of them. A ser- vant. no doubt. No That small, white hand</p>
        <p>p</p>
      </div>
