      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, July, 13, 1912.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July, 13, 1912.</p>
        <p>belonged to no maid-of-all-work, neither (ha the slender, graceful form, surmounted by a shapely little golden head that the slowly-lift- ing blind partially revealed to me. It was she, no doubt. Here was an adventure in- deed. And, boys, you will never beleye it, know, but for one single diecond I thought of the clause In the rules of our A.M.C.: 'Never to have any commerce with women or Jews.' And here were both of them in one episode! "I returned, about three in the afternoon and nervously pulled the bell of the gloomy, unpretentious No. 6, West-street. Is Mr. Falconer at homer, I inquired of the maid.</p>
        <p>" Yee: sir, but he's too ill to see 'visitors. I will cell Miss Falconer.' . _ _</p>
        <p>"I was shewn into a scantily-furnished litfle front room. What a sordid setting for the dainty vision I had seen that morning at the window. Well, she came in, and—well, of course, she was beautiful, as you all know now." Here our host smiled proudly, and I re- ferred mentally again to the hazel eyes at dinner.</p>
        <p>"I think I fell in love at first sight, mem- ber of the A.M.C. though I was; but that must come later. It was difficult to introduce myself and my errand. "I began by telling her briefly the story ef that strange railway journey, and I shall stever forget how the varying emotions of in- tense surprise, terrible excitement, fear al- most, then of relief and gratitude passed swiftly over her sweet face. "'And now,' I said, a short rcelittlels gained. When the Jew demands his money you must ask to see the note, and as not be able to produce it he will have no legal claim on you. Do you see? I am a lawyer myself, and therefore know what I am saying.' "She looked puzzled, then said, hesitat- • ingly, 'But morally, surely the Jew has a claim?'</p>
        <p>"'Yes,' I admitted, 'and after he has been sufficiently punished he shall have his money; your father will be able to finish his literary work. perhaps.' "I did not mention the fact that there was a cheque-book in my pocket; that might not be necessary to-day. "'And now, Miss Falconer, will you go to your father and so far relieve his anxiety?' "I had got no further when a loud knock on the front door startled me so much that I broke off my sentence, and, scarcely knowing 'what I did, moved to the window and looked through the half-closed Venetian blind. It was Shylock himself, looking, if possible. more dishevelled and agitated than when I last saw him on Croydon platform. I hastily stepped back into the room. _ _ . _ _ . _</p>
        <p>;Janet Falconer's face was blanched with fear. It is he,' elle whispered. 'Oh! what shall we do?'</p>
        <p>"'lt is all right,' I whispers—l back. 'Re- ceive the enemy in here. I will hide myself in the window. Remember to insist on the nroduction of the promissory note, and if you have any difficulty appeal to me. I am your "family lawyer come in unexpectedly"; do you understand?' "I could only hope that she did. I believe then, as far as I can remember, that I pressed reassuringly her little white hand, then ensconced myself behind the window curtains. "In the brief interval which elapsed be- tween the knocking on the door and the old Jew's entrance into the little sitting-room I seemed to live through an eternity. So long indeed it seemed that I had time to thank Providence for that interesting episode be- tween London Bridge and Croydon, and all it had led to; to wonder how long it took a woman under ordinary circumstances to fall in love, and how long also she might reason, ably be expected to do so under extraordinary ones; also to wonder even more how short a time it took a man. I was roused from my reverie by the voices behind me. The one was low and slightly trembling, while the other, unlike the gruff surliness that had characterised it when last I heard it, ran smoothly and suavely, like the historic oil that once flowed down the beard of its 'owner's ecclesiastical and Biblical ancestor.</p>
        <p>"'You have, of course, brought the pro- missory note?' the low voice was saying. "'Well, no, my dear young lady,' the oily one was replying, 'I did not think it neces- sary. Although your father is a Christian, I presume his word is his bond, therefore----' "'We must see that note,' continued the other. What insistence that gentle voice was, after all, capable of! "The next few sentences were lost on me. Then I heard a low, terrified cry. I turned quickly. The old man had seized those white, slender wrists with his skinny, claw-like hands, and, holding her forcibly before him, was hissing low, quick, muttered words under his breath. The girl's terrified dark eyed were turned beseechingly in the direction of zny hiding-place, and I experienced a thrill of pleasure and sense of exultation in my power that no legal client had ever yet inspired me with in all the ten years of my legal practice. " ' Pardon me, sir,' I said, emerging from L y retreat and advancing towards them. The old wretch loosed his victim, who sank tremblingly into a chair' and turned and faced me insolently, though not without a look of baffled fear in his eyes. "'And pray, who are you? And—and— and—where have I met you before? Your voice seems familiar, but—' „</p>
        <p>"For a moment i thought I was lost, and that the old man's memory had served to re- call the image of his fellow-passenger who had played so mean a trick on him at Croy- don Station three nights before. However, I replied with all the assurance I could command: 'I am Mr. Falconer's family solicitor, and, having overheard your conversation, I venture to suggest that I may be of service in the present difficulty. With Mies Falconer's permission I shall be glad to be informed of the particulars of the case.' "'And I shall be glad if you will mind your own business, sir,' fiercely replied the old man. 'lt is no legal affair that I discuss with this lady, but a personal and private one. I shall be obliged, therefore, if you will leave us alone,' and he advanced as though to shew me the door. "'Since you force me to extremes,' I con- tinued, coolly, 'I must inform you that I am already in possession of the facts. My client owes you one hundred pounds, for which she save you a promissory note, due to-morrow, anua ry let. In the meantime, you have legally forfeited your claim to the money by losing the 1.0.U.'</p>
        <p>"He staggered back, uncertain how to act. did not bring it with me, certainly. I Srusted to the honour of my client, and—' "'That is all very well,' I interrupted. `lf you want your money, fetch your only legal guarantee for it. We will wait for you here,' and I calmly seated myself. "For a second Shylock was baffled, then, gemembering my profession, he said: 'Legally, of course, you are right, the 1.0. U. should be produced, but'--something in my face no doubt causing the old coward to change his tactics--:L` to tell the truth I have mielaid the note. But I rely on the honour, as I said be- fore, of my client, on his boasted "Scotch integrity," ' this with a withering sneer. 'Morally the money is mine, with or without a promiaaory note.' Then, with a return $o the suave oiliness of tone with which he had addressed his fair victim, and looking to- 'Wards her, he continued: 'I have, however, offered other conditions to this lady, which if lic fulfils—'</p>
        <p>" 'Oh, I cannot, I cannot!' Janet FalcOner aried, rising in extreme agitation. "'MI&amp;4 Falconer,' I said, 'leave me as your solicitor to settle with your creditor. ,p 0 at once to your father, and I will join you later.' " I then opened the door and gently pushed Tier out and shut it. The thin, purple lips were now livid with anger and hatred, and the skinny fingers twitched as if they would fain have been at my Gentile neck.</p>
        <p>"I realised, on turning back into the room, that to make the missing 1.0. U. my trump card was useless and only waste of time. Be- sides, who was the real criminal? The man who merely demanded a legal debt, however villainously brought .:bout, or the man who wilfully stole and detriv.yed what belonged to another? This is a nice moral problem for you fellows,' Graham interrupted himself to say, smiling round on us as we eat breathless, hardly daring to keep our pipes going. , "I felt, to be brief, that though Shylock tnust be robbed of his 'pound of fleah,' he had still the moral claim, if not the legal bond of which I had robbed him on that</p>
        <p>eventful journey on the south Eastern, and I determined to bring matters to a speedy and satisfactory conclusion in the only pos- sible way left to me. "Taking my cheque-hook from my pocket I hastily wrote out a note for the required hundred pounds. Then. as the old usurer's skinny fingers closed round it and his eye searched mine in incredulous inquiry. I took Lint gently but firmly by the ehoulelere, hustled him out into the hall, and for the second time in our brief acquaintance helped him to make, Gladstone bag and all, a speedy and undignified exit. "I need not tell you of the-scene that en- some few minutes later in the room of the invalid iturnalist, nor why an hour later you received a telegram at the club excusing myself from dinner, nor why from that even- ing I ceased necessarily to be a member of the A.M.C."</p>
        <p>Graham paused as if there were no need for more. And from the little drawiug-room across the hall came the etrains of the New Year's Song, in a low, sweet voice:</p>
        <p>"Ring out old shapes of foul disease, Ring out the narrowing lust of gold; Ring out the thousand wars of old, Ring in the thousand years of peace. "Ring in the valiant man and free, The largest heart, the kindlier hand; Ring out the darkness of the land, Ring in the Christ that is to be."</p>
        <p>"She sang it that night," continued Graham, rising. "And now let us go to her. but first"—filling up his glass—" let us drink to the health of the A.M.C."</p>
        <p>And simultaneously three voices replied: "No, no! but rather to the health of our charming hoistess, the bride who was 'was by a promissory note.'"</p>
        <p>[THE END.]</p>
        <p>- .0117 WI "unlit 3</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>_Walking Day and Church Loyalty.</head>
        <p>_Walking Day and Church Loyalty.</p>
        <p>- - St. James' Latchford Parish magazine, contains the following :—Walking day is often spoken of as though it were solely a children's festival ; we are inclined to think it is looked forward to and enjoyed by the grown up people almost as much as by the younger generation. Walking Day undoubtedly brings out enthusiasm for the Church and school of many a parish and this in itself is good. Would that all those who profess their loyalty to St. James' on Walking Day were thoroughly consistent Churchpeople throughout the year ! We should like to think that all our Walk- ing Day friends and supporters would become, if they are not so already, regular and steady members of our congregations..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Processionallst Slaughter.</head>
        <p>Processionallst Slaughter.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell has the following in the "Buttonwood Parish Magazine" :—The number of little ones who have been victimised by open-air processions admits of no statistics. Of the wounded and the slain there is no possible record. Processionalist days demand a heavy toll. As days of light summer clothing in keen east winds,of saturated garments never changed, "Whitsuntide Processions," and "Walking Days must mean serious physical trouble to the fragile and delicate. The seeds of consumption are easily and surely sown by such insane exposure ; not infrequently pneumonia is the quick, sharp, fatal result of a Lancashire Walking Day. One such tragedy as reported from Manchester this Whitsuntide. In a drenching downpour for "show" purposes, a fragment of what otherwise would have been a gigantic procession processed the streets of Cottonopolis. At least one innocent was slaughtered out of hand. 'Tis a sorry citizenship and religiousism which puts show and display before the safeguarding of health and life. One child is of infinitely more value than many processions. For processionalist slaughter there is ordinarily no excuse. There is but one course to follow when the rain descends and the winds beat down upon the lambs—abandon the outdoor function in toto ; grin and bear the disappointment—citizenship and religion should be at one—they must save life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Soclety.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Soclety.</p>
        <p>At the Brotherhood meeting on Sunday last, Mr. W. Beswick presided, and Mr. Dymond (Hampshire) gave a stirring address, on tbe manifold goodness, mercy and grace of God through the Lord Jesus Christ ; how for our sins, we have manifold mercy, and for our trials and temptations, the manifold grace of God. Mr Walker sang effectively " The Watchman " and " Come unto Me." Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. dec. presided at the organ. Mr. .1. Hirusworth led the prayer and Mr. Beswick read the Scripture lesson. A vote of sympathy was sent to our brother Mr. C. Cotton, who is mourning the less of his wife.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Wycliffe Congregational Church.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Congregational Church.</p>
        <p>Every effort is being made to maintain the interest in the work and institutions of Wycliffe Congregational Church during the vacancy in the pastorate caused by the Rev John Yonge. The services on Sunday were conducted by the Rev A .I Viner, F.T.S , secretary of the Lancashire Congregational Union, who is acting as moderator for the church during the interregnum. At the morning service he interested the young people with a charming story, illustrating the virtues of innoceoe. honour and love. He also spoke to the children in the afternoon cbool who were delighted with his observa- tions on" Footprints." His discourses, morning and evening, were listened to with close attention. " Faithfulness " was the subject of the morning sermon in which he sbovied that the success of a church depended more upon the faithfulness of the occupants of the pew than upon the efforts of the minister or the officers. At night he based his remarks on the text" I am the good Shepherd," and subsequently presided at the communion service.</p>
        <p>Rev. John Yonge s Valedictory Message. The Rev. John Yonge writes as follows in Wycliffe Congregational Church Magazine :—The editor of the magazine suggests that I should pen a few valedictory words to the readers of the magazine, and I will try to do so. There is no necessity for me to repeat what has already been laid. But perhaps I may accentuate and emphasize my previous words of gratitude to everyone at Wycliffe for the goodness, forbearance, and loving kindness that have been shown tome, with scarcely any noticeable exception, by the entire community. Ido not actually know who the generous benefactors have been either in Wycliffe or outside it, who conspired together to make me the recipient of such handsome presentations. It would, therefore, be impossible to I express to them individually my sense of their good- ness. But I rejoice to believe that the kindly words and generous gifts have come from truly loyal and loving hearts, and 1 desire again to thank them one and all. The valedictory meeting was truly a supremely memorable, occasion, faced as it was by so remarkable, and, at least in Warrington, so noteworthy and catholic an assembly upon platform and in pew. E shall always cherish its memory with feelings of the deepest satis- faction and gratitude. I particularly desire to proffer my grateful acknowledgments to the Deacons and the Committees who so admirably combined to prepare and arrange the presentations and the proceedings. The event was both devised, and carried forward to its successful issue,with considerable and manifold thought and care. Its novelty in the history of 36 years rendered its achievement the more difficult, and the most praiseworthy. So far as I can imagine, nothing was left undone which could possibly have sezured a more complete success. And so, my beloved friends, I tender to each and all, the sincerest and deepest thanks of my wife and of myself for the entire'aclaievement.</p>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church. Preaching on Sunday evening from the Old Testament story of David's conflict with the Philistines, the Rev. W. Reid said that God doubtless chooses and calls His soldiers, but the final and determining word in every case is uttered by the individual life itself, and so to every life is the battle presented,whatever the response. The warfare, he continued, is generaband whatever con- tribution we make is in aid of, or against the ultimate success of the entire campaign, nevertheless every man has his own particular foe. It may be objective. It is always subjective. The battle is a religious one, not creedal, the creedal being an excrescence. In the deepest sense life's battle is religious. They who enter the lists, join issue with Dagon or Jehovah. Dagon's armaments are ornate and perfect from Dagon's point of view. Jehovah despises not armaments but will show Israel that there is something much more effective. The armour of St. Paul's soldier, that of personal worth, the shield of faith which forges a breastplate of righteousnep,the faith which brings God down so that he becomes incarnated in the youth who steps to meet the enemy, The sincerity and earnestness of life which as a girdle of truth sustains him, is at once the protection and the inspiration which attacks the beastly in the lion and bear as well as the impious in the Philistine. The simplicity of his life was also a strong offensive and defensive weapon. First attempt- ing and then laying aside Sauls armour, he in effect tells us that many things, quite legitimate are not expedient. Even a useful looking sword that will help to lose the battle had better be left behind. But, said the preacher, his greatest weapon and proteotion was his consciousness and reliance on God's presence "Thou comest to me with sword, but I come to thee in the name of the Lord." Much can be done by the instruments of civilizations and culture, sometimes more by the pebble well worn by the floods that have</p>
        <p>passed over it, but DP means Can ever compemate for the absence of the souls consciousness of God's presence, says Bishop Paget. There is no equipment for our work whatever it may be that is so necessary as the knowledge of God, and the recollection of His presence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>Scores of people were, perforce, turned away from the doors of the Hippodrome on Monday evening, when George Formby commenced his week's engagement at the Friars Green house. Formby's popularity is remarkable, and there can be but few places where he has a more numerous or more loyal crowd of admirers than Warrington. One hardly needs to describe Formby's typically Lancashire style of humour. It will be familiar to most of our readers. But the fact again impresses itself upon one, that the drollery of George Formby is natural to the man rather than cultivated. In a quiet vein few men can be more genuinely humorous. There was just one cause for regl at which we found unavoidable after hearing Formby in his new songs. They were not as good as the old favourites— at least there was nothing with the compell- ing attraction of "John Willie, come on!" to which the artiste owes not a little of his celebrity. The best of the three new ditties was "We all went to Leicester Square," though there was plenty of character and quaint fun in "I was taking my father's tea." Musical turns are in strong force this week, and the one supplied byJessie Berg, "The Russian girl with the fiddle" will require some beating by a solo performer. Miss Berg possesses an enviable technique and touch, and her sympathetic rendering of popular ballads with subdued orchestral accompaniment fairly brought down the house. In a different line Miss Victoria Campbell was equally charming and successful. A typically Scotch lassie, she lays good claim to the title of " The Scottish Nightingale," and her beautiful soprano voice found adequate expression in songs reminiscent of Burns' land. Miss Campbell has also the distinction of being god- daughter to the late Queen Victoria, and daughter of James Campbell, late chief piper to King Edward VII. Baker and Balfe present a bright example of zylophone melodies, the turn concluding with a neat step dance by Baker, which his lady partner accom- panies on the zylophone. A clever gymnastic exhibi- tion over, the bounding net is given by the Flying Julians. One of the most dramatic sketches seen here for a long time is " The Proof," presented by Gerald Wyatt and Denise Dirome. The scene is one between the faithless wife and her doubting husband. The way in which he wrings proof" from his partner is very clever, while the subsequent denouement leaves the audience silent and startled, until applause spontaneously breaks forth. Bernard Clifford, character bass vocalist, completes a refined and attractive " bill of fare," which is a credit to the management.</p>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. The programme for next week is replete with items in infinite variety, as follows: Dan Whitley, an eccentric funniosity ; Lyons trio in pantomimic absurdity; the Dennisons, in singing and dancing; Ernest Crake and Company in comedy act, "The electric tram conductor " ; Spry and Monte in miscellaneous comedy, Harry Raymond and 17 ; Horace Horner, upside-down dancer; Louise Zetlaud, vocalist; and Lil Sprightley, comediene.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>MINIATURE RIFLE SHOOTING.</head>
        <p>MINIATURE RIFLE SHOOTING.</p>
        <p>NATIONAL SERVICE LEAGUE v. APPLETON _ RIFLE CLUB.</p>
        <p>The first match of the season, between the League and Appleton Rifle Club, took place on Thursday last on the Club's range. The weather was generally line, but a changeable light prevailed. The conditions, selected by the League, were :—Eight men a-side; ten shots and a sighter, at 100 yards, at a three-inch bull target ; Bisley conditions. The result of the match was a victory for the Appleton Club by 33 points. Scores:</p>
        <p>THE LEAGUE. - E Powell 44 C Shipley 43 S James 43 T Richardson A Hutchinson 3 Ridyard' J Davies P Harrison</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>PPLETO7A.</head>
        <p>A PPLETO7A.</p>
        <p>C P Day I Broady F J Grocott 46 Colonel B Fairclough A Down J Towers .... . 43 E Pollard 43 A Shepherd 43 _ 358</p>
        <p>PIER TRAGEDY. VISITOR FROM LEIGH DROWNED AT DOUGLAS.</p>
        <p>A coroner's irgniry was held at Douglas on Tuesday into the death of Ed ward Blackburn, a spinner, of 6, Derwent-strest, Leigh, whose body was recovered from the harbour on Sunday. A man standing at the end of the Red Pier close on midnight on Saturday heard a voice and then a knock, as if a man's foot had struck something. Turning quickly, he saw the form of a man dropping over the side of the pier. Search was made without result. When the body was recovered a sum of over £22 was found in the pockets. A verdict of " Pound drowned" was returned, the jury adding a recommendation that railed pretection be provided on the pier.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
