      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <p>Payment for teaching is regularly made up top 1832. At this date Edward Gaskell receives 4 guineas as his salary for one year. Save for singing the accounts show no further expenditure for tuition. There are no minutes from 1809 until 1819 At this meeting Mr. W. Smith is in the chair and it is decided; That in future the children be taught in the vestry the boys in the forenoon from 9 to 12. The girls in the afternoon from 1 to 4 and writing to the whole school from 4 10 5. That one male visitor shall attend with S. Bibby to instruct the boys and also in the even- ing. And one female vieitor to instruct the prls. A list of two dozen " visitors " who are to "attend in rotation" is given and the names suggest that the school did not lack the interest of the congregation. In these brief notes we have confined our- selves to such facts as may be gleaned or inferred from terse minutes and unimagina- tive balance sheets. Perhaps there are War- ringtonians who could add to our knowledge by making a draft upon memory or tradition. A.M.</p>
