      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>HOW BRIGHT IS THE SUN.</head>
        <p>HOW BRIGHT IS THE SUN.</p>
        <p>-- - Now that the hot days are hero it is appropriate to talk about the sun and about the heat he lavishes upon us. The sun gives away his heat like a gentleman. He never stints, be never economises, but just pours it out as though it were the ,cheapest and most plentiful of all things. But heat is not cheap. It is the most precious thing our world has got and , we can't help feeling just a little queer as we think of the immense quantities of it that I fly off into space apparently wasted. For, ' after all, our earth and the planets are only specks in the sun's universe aid the little heat they catch is a trifle compared with ' that which is given out lavishly in all directions. Perhaps my readers would like to have a few facts which the astronomer of the Paris Observatory has been setting down. He has been studying the sun's heat and measuring it. Of course when astronomers tell us things they run into strings of figures such as plain folks can't comprehend. This gentleman puts the sun's heat in candle power and when he writes the total it is 18 followed by 27 noughts. Let somebody write :this down and tell me whether it is billions or trillions. He tries to help us out by saying that the most powerful electric arc-light ever made has an illuminating power of only 20,000 candles, and then goes on to say that every square inch of the sun's surface gives a light equal to nearly two million candles. A square inch of the sun's surface would therefore give light enough to illuminate all the streets of a big city by night. So much for the light ; now the heat. This he fixes at 6,482 degrees centigrade, a figure which doesn't help us at all. But since we know what the sun can do at us 90 millions of miles away when the cool clouds happen to get out of the way we don't feel any desire to put our fingers into that 6482 degrees centigrade. Then the Professor shocks us by saying that the sun is fairly cool compared with Sirius, for that great sun shows 190,600 degrees centigrade. In other words it is about 30 times hotter than the sun, a state- ment which quite takes our breath away and leaves us perspiring. PINK AND BLUE. When she went to the party, Elizabeth Lou Had her hair tied on top with a ribbon of blue, But she hadn't a very good titre, and I think ' 'Twas because of a girl with a ribbon of pink. "For all the girls chose her, the whole evening through ; Just because she wore pink !" sobbed Elizabeth Lou. At the very next party, Elizabeth Lou , Wore a ribbon herself of roseate hue. But would you believe it ? That same girl was there With a bowknot of blue, tying up her brown hair. And the very same thing proved again to be true : "They still like her best l" wept Elizabeth Lou.</p>
        <p>Now betwten you and me, it had nothing to do With those crisp little loopings of pink and blue. But one little girl wore a smile on her face, The other a frown in the very same place, A smile and a frown ! Now which of the two Do you fancy was worn by Elizabeth Lou ? UNAFRAID.</p>
        <p>Who's afraid of a cow ! They're so gentle and kind You can .go up quite close and they none of 'em</p>
        <p>mind: An' they like little girls, so I've heard people say But I wish, 0 I wish, they was furder away !</p>
        <p>Pooh I—who's afraid? They're as good as can be, An' one's a child cow that is younger than me. An' they give us good milk—an' there's nothing to fear,— But I wish, 0 1 wish, that my Daddy was here!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>LOCAL TERRITORIAL NOTES.</head>
        <p>LOCAL TERRITORIAL NOTES.</p>
        <p>The annual Church Parade for "E" and "G' Companies (Newton Detachment) 4th South Lancashire Regiment, mill take place on Sunday, 21st July, 1912. The Detachment will fall in on Earlestown Market Square at 2 p.m., and will march via Market-street, Bridga-street, Crow-lane and high-street, Newton to Newton Church, where service will he held at 3 p.m.— By kind permission of Lieut.-Colonel B. Fairclougb, the brass band and fife and drum band of the Battalion will attend. " and G" Companies will be engaged in Musketry Practice at Altcar to-day, Saturday 13th inst. A Battalion Parade will be held at Warrington on Saturday, 201 h inst., when the local companies will leave Newton by. the 1-33 p.m. train, and Earlestown by the 2-13 p.m. train. The annual prize shoot held at Appleton on Monday, 24th June, Saturday, 29th June, and Monday, Ist July, was very successful almost 300 Non-Corns, and men competing, of which stout 70 were local. Two competitions were fired. The best shot competition (7lrds at 100 yards at a 3 in. bull's eye) was the qualify- ing stage for the 2nd part, to be held at Altcar on 7th September when seven rounds at 200, 500 and 6CO yards will be fired. A good nun'- her of local Non-Corns. and men qualified. The highest aggregate of the four distances becomes the best shot of the Battalion for the ensuing twelve months and receives first prize £4. There are nine other prizes varying iron' £3 to 10s. The other com- petition was the battalion in which 60 prizes were com- peted for, the conditions being 7 rds at 100 yds at a 3in. bull's eye and (2) 7 rds at 100 at a vanishing figure target, 6 seconds exposure for each shot. The tinder- named local N.C.O.'s and men won prizes as follows : Z s. d. 2 Peivate Eckersley, G 8 C.S. Boardman, B 0 15 0 19 Sergt. Lewis, 1111 0 14 0 13 Sergt. Sutton, E 14 Corporal Dudley, G 17 Recr. '2O Private Ball, B 21 Private Naylor, B 0 10 0 , 26 Lance-Corp. Heywortb, G 31 Lance-Corp, Tear, U 35 Pte. Roberts, B 39 Sergt. Jeffs, G 0 5 0 44 Corp. Edwards, G 45 Lance-Corp Derbyshire, G 53 Lance-Sergt Newport, B 58 Recruit Hill. G Captain Avory, G Company, won the officers' sweep- stakes with a scare of 32, possible 35.</p>
        <p>A man Alto was sentenced to three month's imprison- ment for kousebreaking at Hull, had surrendered him- self to the police, stating that he was sick of thieving. There was no pleasure or satisfaction in it, he said, and he could not enjoy the money,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>KING AND QUEER AT HENLEY.</head>
        <p>THE KING AND QUEER AT HENLEY.</p>
        <p>The visit of the King and Queen to Henley on Saturday attracted an enormous crowd of people, and the spectacle was one of the most charming and brilliant it is possible to imagine. Their Majestic&amp; progress in the Royal Barge to the Pavilion was greeted by</p>
        <p>the assembled thousands with one long shout of welcome!. Their Majesties stayed up the river for five hours, and after the races the Queen presented the prizes. Our photograph shows the King and Queen and Princess Mary in the Royal Barge, and the inset their arrival at the waterside. . _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>THE FLY PEST. That the insect which has come to be known as the house fly is In th filthy and dangerous is fully recog- nised by all health authorities, and many are this season adopting drastic measures for its suppression. I read in my " Examiner" that, on the advice of their medical officer, the Colne Corporation have instituted a vigorous campaign with the idea of com- passing the wholesale desti uction of these nasty pests. All this is highly pi aisewee thy, but it set me w oeder- ing what system of sewage treatment Colne has. On the very by on which I read of the latest form of municipal enterprise in regard to the flies, I noticed that the Huddersfield Corporation had been applying for increased borrowing powers in respect of their sewage disprsal works at Cooper Bridge, and that the inhabitants of the district in which it was proposed to further extend the works in position were hotly pro- testing and opposing the scheme on the ground that the works already existing had brought into their midst a plague of flies, these creatures breeding by thousands in such congenial surroundings. lam told that is the experience of the unfortunate dwellers right along the valleys which modern " sanitary reform " has turned into so many costly cesspools. I quite expect that one of these days, w hen the powers that be have compelled every town and villge to make provision for sewage treatment on some such lines as these, the whole expensive system will be condemned, and the business will have be done over again accord- ing to some newer method. U.M.C. CONFERENCE. The annual conference of the United Methodist Church opened at Hanley on Tuesday, and the pro- ceedings extend over just a week. It is the fifth since the consummation of the scheme which was designed to embrace in one happy family the Methodist New Con- nexion (which, despite its name, was really an old body, the founders having receded from the Wesleyans upwards of a century lige), the Bible Christians, and the United Methodist Free Churches. The movement which ended in the amalgamation of the three was exceedingly creditable to the respective leaders, who went about the c:wntry delivering inspiring addresses on Christian charity, brotherliness, and the advantages of organic union; but some of those whose hearts were SO touched that they hastened to hold up both hands in favour of the proposal have since seen reason to ask themselves if it would not have been as well to have allowed their heads also to have some part or lot in the matter. They complain that the scheme was imbed through at such a rate that there was no time to examine it in detail and to consider it in relation to the future. Lest the organisa- tion should become too unwieldy,it was found neccssai y severely to curtail the number of delegates, and it is said that the wholesale reduction in the representation of individual churches has had the effect of breeding indifference among the younger laity ; those, who under the old system would have been active participants in the conference are now excluded from the deliberations, and the withdrawal of incentive has caused many to lose interest. I have gathered this and much more from conversation with aggrieved members ; and if the adverse feeling be at all wide- spread, it is indeed regrettable. I do not myself pretend to any sympathy with it whatever. It was a revelation to me. I had imbibed the notion that the supposed union had been agreed to with practical unanimity, and had proved all it was expected to be; and I am frankly disappointed to discover that there is dissatisfaction with it even in comparatively few instances. I cannot myself conceive of substantial reason why, first all the Methodist sections, and eventu- ally other great Nonconformist bodies, should not unite as one church ; indeed, I believe the time will come when, if there be any division remaining between British Christians, there should be only sacerdotalists on the one hand and evangelicals on the other. THE WESLEYANS. I have referred to the Methodist branches, now for a word as to the parent stem. This is an especially busy time for the leading cfficials and laymen of the Wesleyan Church. First, the Coq mittees of Depart- ments sat for days in London. Then, last week, came the turn of the Home Missions Committee, followed by the Stationing Ccmmittee. This week the authorities responsible for the examination of ministerial candi- dates have been sitting at Richmond and Didsbury ; and the weeding out process in which they had to engage is a difficult and delicate business. By the way, many, many years ago, I accompanied a serious Wesleyan friend on a pop visit to Didsbury College, and be appeared greatly shocked and ecandalised to find the students so light-hearted and merry— or, as he put it, frivolous. It was an " off " afternoon, and many of them were indulging in cricket, tennis and other outdoor exercises. One from our district whom we bad gone specially to see left the playing field to show us round.As we entered the chapel we had to pass a bust of the venerable founder of Methodism The careless student horrified us by making a hatpeg of this. As we went by, he tossed at it the cricket cap which he was wearing when we arrived, and as it alighted sideways on the head of the grave and reverend seignior, and was there left until we had completed our tour of inspection, it gave Master Wesley quite a rakish look. My friend confided to me his profound and unalterable conviction that a candidate for the ministrj who could rerpetrate so flagrant an outrage unashamed would come to no good. However, so far as I know, the worst that ever happened to him was that he was reported to the " Fathers in Israel' for alleged breach of promise of marriage. These young fellows ; are solemnly asked among other things— . " Have you a matrimonial engagement?" When Dr. Rule was in that position, and the question was put, he replied "No, but I desire to have one!" Speaking of theological students, I knew one whose ambition to become a parson was nipped in the bud; but, nothing daunted by his failure, he forthwith sought the r ost of hangman. In that quarter, too, his offer was rejected. All this he told me himself.</p>
        <p>A BIT OF NONSENSE. That must have occurred more than 30 years ago, and about the same period I picked up the following document :—" Missing, about the 3rd of next month, a tall, stoutToomplexioned gentleman, five feet six inches of age, 27 years in hi iEht, pink hair, green eyes, blue eyebrows. He had on when last seen a pair of swallow-tailed trousers, with sausage-striped sleeves, fashionable mutton cutlet waistcoat with cast iron ttimmings and knit&amp; d mahogany legs ; a double-barrelled frock coat, with tripe collar and tubacco linings, ado.ned with three flounces ' • water-tight canvass boot-t, with porce- lain tope, laced up at the soles; match box hat, low crowned, trimmed square round the edges, without the nap ; a pair of green and white stockings, with potatoe heels and sides; a Hance nectie, rather broad of belief, tied reund the forehead : a shirt of cabbage cloth, which buttons up behind his back ; his gloves were of cast steel Honiton lace, with air-tight venti- lators at the points ; he is deaf and dumb of one eye, and limps very much with the other; is slightly pock- marked at the back of his constitution, sits down higher when he walks crooked, has a Wight impedi- menl in his look, and wears a Grecian bend on his upper lip, whiskers cut if short inside ; was carrying an empty carpet box in each band, and a cobweb bag in the other, containing the Suez Canal and the Metropolitan Underground Haile ay, together with a bottle of specific against the effects of an earthquake, and a quantity of unused fusee and matchbox stamps,' of no value except as waste paper to anyone but the owner : was born after his younger brother,bis mother being absent on each occasion. Was supposed to be seen at the Mouth of Vesuvius, with the Falls of Niagara under his arm." Isn't it awful? BANKRUFBCY TOO EASY. -</p>
        <p>" Bankruptcy is becoming far too easy," said Judge Sheraton Baker, at Grimsby Bankruptcy Court not long ago. "A man goes bankrupt, gets whitewashed of his debts, and the unfortunate creditors have to suffer." We know how true it is ; we have all had examples of it at some time or other—l have myself been constantly ccming across them for many years, and, so far as I remember, in every instance, the debtor had not kept proper bock. A tradesman, or a farmer will find it an immense advantage t,o keep books; the one values his stock-in-trade, the other his stock, implements, corn and fodder. If this be not done at regular periods, it is impossible for a man to know just how he stands from year to ycar. The bank balance alone will not tell this. This duty, evident though it be, is often neglected. Some people say that they do not know how to set about it, and so they neglect it altogether. THE WAY TO --- Last week's story in the Children's Corner reminded me of another one—l don't know why it should, for there is no connection between the two. Years ago, when a lady cyclist who was touring in " Bloomer" costume was refused admittance to a hotel where she intended to obtain refreshments, one of the same sex similarly attired halted on the highway to inquire as to her Ivhereabouts. Accosting an agricultural labourer, she asked—" Is this the way to Wareham ? " The yokel scratched his puzzled pate, and sheepishly eyed over the nether garments of the up-to-date woman. "Come, my man," said she impatiently. "1 asked if this is the way to Wareham ? " Then the man partially recovered his power of speech, and stammered—" I dunno, Miss, I'm sure. Yer see, I've nevver wore 'em ! "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>"THAT FREE PRESCRIPTION IS WONDERFUL."</head>
        <p>"THAT FREE PRESCRIPTION IS WONDERFUL."</p>
        <p>"I'M ENTIRELY RID OF MY PAINS."</p>
        <p>A. message from Hull : " You ought to publish this for the benefit of others as I am sure the prescrip- tion is the most wonderful thing I have ever tried. My pains were so bad that I had not slept for weeks, and the night the chemist filled it for me I slept for eight hours. Now I haven't a pain, the Sciatica or Lumbago, or whatever it was, is gone, and my wife is cured of her bad neuralgic headaches. It's truly wonderful.</p>
        <p>"This is the prescription just as I cut it from the paper. Ask your nearest chemist for 60 grains of Kephaldol in tablet form Two tablets will give instant relief, and an occasional one will banish the pain entirely, I think permanently. My family always keep the prescription handy,"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>CANING REGULATIONS.</head>
        <p>CANING REGULATIONS.</p>
        <p>NEW CODE FOR CHESHIRE SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>To the Cheshire Education Committee, at Crewe, on Tuesday, the Elementary Schools Committee recom- mended the adoption of the amended corporal punish- ment regulations. These provide that head teachers shall use every endeavour to reduce all forms of puniFbment to a minimum compatible with the welfare of the children and the school, and corporal punishment shall not in any case be inflicted (except for grave offences) until other methods have been tried and failed. Corporal punishment shall not be inflicted in infant schools or classes. Head teachers are held responsible for all corporal punishment inflicted in their respective depart- molts. Corporal punishment shall be inflicted only by the head teacher or by fully-qualified assistant teachers of not less than three years' experience, who shall have had the power delegated to them by the head teacher with the consent of the managers. The committee strongly deprecated the corporal punishment of girls, but, where such punishment is necessary,it shall only be inflicted by a fully-certificated mistress, or, in schools where there is no mistress so employed, by the senior assistant mistress. All blows by the hand or ruler, boxing the ears, striking on any part of the head, end other irregular modes of inflict- ing corporal punishment are forbidden. The recommendations were adopted.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE LAND SALE. NEARLY £40,900 FOR LORD DELAMERE.</p>
        <p>• Messrs Knight, Frank, and Rutley offered at Crewe on Monday 1,048 acres of the Vale Royal Estate, on instructions bora Lord Delamere. The rent roll is £2,264 a year, and the estate lies in the heart of Cheshire Many of the tenants purchased their own property, and the sale realised £39,545. The principal farms realised the following prices :--Knight's Grange Farm, containing 131 acres, let at .£139 Zs 6d, was sold to Mr Thomas Platt, of Winsford, for £3,520 ; Westholme Farm, 59 acres and let at £lO6 per year; was sold to Mr J B Chirnside, of Westholme, for £2,575 ; Salter's Wall, 47 acres and let at £lOl, was sold to Mr Pimlott, Winoham, for £2,360 ; Marton Hall Dairy Farm, 100 acres, let at £315 a year was with- drawn at £7,200; Marton Bank Farm, 47 acres, let at £lB7 10s. was sold to Mrs E Bradbury of Wincham, for .C 2,410 ; Chester-lane Dairy Farm, Dodd, the tenant, for a dairy of 172 acres and let at £255 was sold to Mr A £ 6,300. Littler-lane Dairy Farm, 120 acres, let at £lB5 a year, was sold to Mr T L Sherwin, the tenant, for £ 5,000,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>OPEN-AIR POLITICS.</head>
        <p>OPEN-AIR POLITICS.</p>
        <p>THE CONTRAST BETWEEN LIBERAL AND TORY LAND POLICIES.</p>
        <p>A Word About the Chamberlain Celebrations.</p>
        <p>Ideal weather conditions favoured the Progressive Labour League's open-air meeting on 'Monday evening and there was a very large crowd to listen to the speeches delivered from the Liberal van near Arpley Station. The meeting was also one of the most enthusiastic held by the League this season. Mr .1 Jarman presided and briefly touched upon the leading features ef the Government's efforts for the benefit of the workers. .</p>
        <p>Councillor JAMES KENDALL, of Manchester, WAS the principal speaker At the outset he alluded to the relstions between the Liberal and Labour parties in the rending by-elections and deprecated anything in the nature of a serious break between sections of the pro- gressive forces. Only the Tories could gain by such a rupture and the country as a whole stood to lose a great deal by it. The Government had taken great risks on behalf of the workers and he wanted each</p>
        <p>motion of the progressive ranlks to realize that</p>
        <p>NOTHING COULD BE MORE WICKED OR SUICIDAL than for them, at this point in their progress, to enter into conflicts among themselves. The two great parties were never more clearly separated by policy than they were to-day. He did not know whether the working- men of Warrington had followed the doings of the party to which their distinguished Member belonged; if so, they could not feel flattered by the bill of fare it put before them. (Laughter.) Mr. F. E. Smith had again unfurled the old, worn-out flag and had declared to the world that the policy which he and his brother and the rest of the party stood for, Was the bringing in at the earliest moment of tariff reform. He (tbe speaker) was not going to insult a Warrington audience by arguing that question; it was stale eight years ago. The plea by which the country was originally asked by Mr. Chamberlain to swallow it—that it was urgent in the interests of our industries—had been falsified by events. Never in the commercial history of this country had we been so successful; never had our com- merce been so active, vigorous and prosperous as in the past eight years. Never during the last twenty years bad unemployment been lower than at the present time. The people had discovered that if they had taken the so-called medicine of the tariff reformers, it would have poisoned them. (Hear, hear.) The Tory party had tried everything else, but without avail. They tried to get up an agitation against Home Rule, but Home Rule was as good as carried. (Applause). They tried another agitation on the score of religion, but the people were too religious to be taken in by it. There never was a time aben the masses of the people had a clearer and stronger grip of the Golden Rule—and that was everything in con- nection with religion. It did not consist of saying creeds and certain phrases but of living as man to man and trying to put into practice the true spirit of brother- hood—and never in the history of this country was there more of that feeling among the masses. (Applause.) The question of Disestablishment in Wales had hardly created a ripple on the surface of English society and it was to be carried through on the princ'ple of Home Rule—that if the people of Wales, through their repre- sentatives, declared that they wanted it, they had a right to have it. Even the AGITATION AGAINST THE INSURANCE ACT.</p>
        <p>was failing to catch votes, for the workers were beginning to realise that in return for their fourpence they would get a greatdeal more than they had been able to obtain from the very best benefit society in the past. (Applause.) They would not only receive the result of their'own payments, but something from the " hors as well. (Elear, hear.) The attempt to make party capital out of that measure was one of the meanest ever made in British politics. (llcar,lacar.) Councillor Kendall quoted the view of a Tory employer in Man- chester who told his workpeople that the Insurance Act was the greatest and most ben ficent measure ever placed on the Statute Book, and urged them to take full advantage of it. Parsing under review leading Liberal measures, Councillor Kendall said Mr. Lloyd George's famous Budget was a nasty pill fcr the Tory party because he had discovered how to raise a lot of fresh taxation without taxing afresh the workingman. The speaker called special attention to the Shops Act which he said would give some relief from drudgery to the most unprotected of the toilers :of the country—those who worked behind shop counters. Discussing the land question, the speaker said that if they wanted to know what the Tory laud policy was they had only to read history. They could not wither grapes from thorns or figs from thistles. The TORY LAND POLICY lIAD BEEN ONE OF GRAB</p>
        <p>all along the line and hundreds and thousands of acres which ought to be open to the " common " pecple now belonged to people who had no right to them except that they had had friends in Parliament to enable them to enclose the land. The measure which some of the Tory party were now proposing for "the settlement of the people on the land " would simply tighten the grip of the landowner, while getting people to put their brain, thought and muscle into the improving of estates. On the other hand, Mr. Lloyd George had been taking a revaluation of the land so that at the earliest moment tie might assess it at its proper value and raise a very large amount of revenue from the land which was in so few hands. The result would be either to make landlords put their land to proper use or to make it not worth their while to withhold it from cultivation. (Applause). It would in future be an expensive luxury for any man to hold up thousands of acres when there were so many people who Ranted to make a living on the land. Behind all the present un- rest there was the one great fact that the democracy had realised something of their force and power and were determined to have a greater say in the manage- ment of the nation. The Liberal policy was to give them that chance and the Government had brought in a Franchise Bill which would recognise that the work- ingman had as much stake in the country as the man who happened to be wealthy. (Applause). Mr. E. VVYNNE JONES, in proposing a vote of thanks (which was heartily carried) to the speaker, said it had been hinted that some employers of labour might leave the Liberal Party because of the insurance Act. If there were any such, he should be prepared to let thorn go. The party would be better without them. (Bear, hear). He would be prepared to see the Liberal party fail, if necessary, over the Insurance Act, if they had to depend upon support of that kind, because if they did fail it would be in one of the grandest and noblest efforts at social amelioration that a Government ever engaged in. (Applause). The time would come when the people of this country would bless the day that ever a Lloyd George existed in British politics. (Applause). Every penny of the money that was paid by the great mass of the workers through the National Insurance Act would go in benefits to the workers, and be defied any body to challenge that statement. The Tories had devoted much of their energies to finding fault with the Act but if they got into power they would not raise a finger to repeal it. (Applause). In the Crewe by-election the Tory candidate had not said a word about National Insurance in his address. The servants who went to the Albert Hall in luxurious motor cars to demonstrate against the Act were having the time of their lives. (Laughter). But they could not get a single Unionist M.P. to stand on the platform and denounce the Act. Why? Because the day was coming when the benefits of that great scheme would be reaped by the people. (Applause). That day the Tariff Reformers were CBI EBRATING MR. JOSEPH CHAMBERLAIN'S BIRTHDAY. They were all sorry that Mr. Chamberlain was not in the fighting line ; be would not have been a party to some of the trickery that was taking place in connection with tariff reform. (Hear, hear). He (the speaker) would guarantee that at the birthday celebrations they would not quote Mr. Chamberlain's words : "If you give a preference to the Colonies, you must just put a tax on food." Whether they talked about Imperial preference or tariff reform, it all meant taxing the food</p>
        <p>W</p>
        <p>of the people. Taxing the foreigner meant taxing them selves. To stop imports into thecountry meant placing a limit on the exports from the country. Even as a revenue-producing machine, the tariff reform proposals were the greatest fraud ever attempted to be perpe- trated. In conclusion the speaker expressed the hope that there would be no disunion in the progressive forces, but that they would unite to break down monopoly and privilege of every kind. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>• 4., ~. ~,;,,, ,_,..., ~.„...... , 4,,, --o•46th . -, i i \\--\,\ ~.;..:-, . , . .\ . . 1 , , 0 • _.,- •. . -......+•+% /.1 I \ i t • A • 3 . ... \ r ' .„,, ..• 'L.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>WALKER'S BREWERY TRIP.</head>
        <p>WALKER'S BREWERY TRIP.</p>
        <p>A Day at Blackpool.</p>
        <p>Blackpool, with its thousand and one attractions, is indeed an ideal resort for the day excursionist, whose object it is to get the most enjoyment possible cut of a day's sojourn by the briny. It is true there are far more picturesque places, but then, does the average • tripper go away on a day (xeursion with the intention of searching for beauty spots ?—No, his main idea is to obtain the maximum of amusement during the short time at his disposal. Once in Blackpool he is not left long in doubt as to which course to adopt by which he can enjoy himself to the fullest extent, and whatever his tastes may be, he is more than satisfied. It is the possession of these exceptional facilities that has made this west coast resort so popular with the working class people of Lancashire and elsewhere. Naturally, when a works trip has to be arranged the organisers have Blackpool on the list of likely places and they know that should they decide upon \a visit there, success is assured. During the long number of years it has been the evstom of Messrs. Peter Walker and Son, Limited, to grant their employees an annual day excursion many places of interest have been visited, but it is questionable whether any of them proved so popular with the majority as did this year's outing to Blackpool ns Erich took place on Saturday last. The arrangements which were in the hands of a spccial committee were admirable and once again the clerk of the weather, influenced, it is suggested, by the captivating smile of "Little Peter," was on his best behaviour for the sun shone radiantly all the day. Early in the morning large numbers of employees and friends thronged the platform at Bank Quay station. The first train load left shortly before six o'clock and it was followed by two other specials, the total number of excursionists (employees and friends), numbering 1,500. The heavily laden trains arrived at the gay seaside town in good time and the triprers soon commenced a round of pleasure. In addition to the usual allowance to the workmen with a double railway ticket, there was free admission to the North pier and Pavilion, Tower and entertainments and the Winter Gardens. Full use was made of these privileges and everyone appreciated the fore- thought and kindness of the firm. 13Iackpool was crowded with visitors, many foreigners being include d, but neveitheless wherever one went a Warringtonian was to be seen. On the cars to St. Armes and Lytham and cn the circular tour, at the South Shore and in the various places of amusement one invariably came across a familiar face from War- rington and many were the cheery greetings exchanged. After having had a full day— one of the best—the trippers boarded three .srecial trains which left Blackpool at 8 25, 9.0 and 935 and arrived in Warrington in good time, the last train drawing up in the station about midnight. All were exceedingly grateful to the firm for such a delightful holiday.• Such outings as these cannot fail to create a good sense of fellowship between employer and employee and it would indeed be gratifying if other firms would fellow the example set by Messrs. Peter Walker and Son, Ltd.</p>
        <p>In the evening the annual staff dinner at which all the heads of the departments and other guests attended, was held in the Hotel Metro)) de, Mr. T. Fulton-Smith presiding. About 05 sat down to an excellent repast and the function proved a pleasant close to a delightful day. In submitting " Success to the firm of Peter Walker and Son " the Chairman said he believed they had all enjoyed themselves that day. (Hoar, hear.) The com- mittee had again been fortunate in securing the appre- ciation of the clerk of the weather who had smiled upon them with golden sunshine. They had not only had the pleasure of natural sunshine, but sunshine and happiness bad been reflected in the faces of all pressnt. (Hear, bear) Everyone knew about the firm of Peter Walker and Sons nowadays. (Hear, hear.) A great deal of all the furore made about "Little Peter ' was due to the energies of Mr. Hutton, who unfortunately, could not be with them that day. It was ovt,ing to his extraordinary exertion and skill that they had been able to put on the English market an article that defied even German competition. (Applause.)- He asked all present to join with him in drinking success to the firm firm in whose prosperity he was as ranch interested as anyone present. lie believed they were all loyal to the firm. (Applause.) This toast was received with musical honours. Major A F G Potter briefly proposed the toast of " Our Guests." Mr Townley responding particularly on behalf of the railway comp 'riles' representatives, said they certainly esteemed it an honour to be included amongst the guests. They wished the firm every success. They were "up against" each other occasionally, but this annual holiday together was well calculated to rub off the corners and to smooth the roughuesses which might have accumulated during the twelve months. (Hear, bear.) They were all, collectively and individually, , anxious to serve a firm which they knew to be one of the most able, generous and up-to-date firms in Wei rington. • (Hear, bear.) Labour troubles and the like were unknown at the brewery, largely, he thought owing to the very spirit which prompted them to give that , annual trip. (Applause.) ; A vote of thanks was heartily accorded the chairman ; who in reply said that great credit was due to Major Potter and Mr Johnson, who had done the whole of the bard work which had contributed to the success of the trip. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
