      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>ST. ELPHIN'S PARK AS A</p>
        <p>PLAYGROUND.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>"Council Set at Defiance."</head>
        <p>"Council Set at Defiance."</p>
        <p>ALLEGED EXTRAVAGANT PRICES FOR HORSES.</p>
        <p>The d:scussions at the monthly meeting of the Warrionton Town Conned on Tue!.day evening did not wage round questions of outstandieg inipertance, but at the same time some interesting—not t say winning pt juts were raised. The Mayor (Councillor Dr Joseph) presided and there were also present :—The Deputy Mayor (Alderman Fcrshax), Aldermen A Bennett, W Bolton, T Burton, John Evers, James Evans, W Vierpoint, .1 Smethurst and D Tionion ; Councillors G Archer, T 11 Ashcroft, W 11 liellbun, J a Craik, E Cunnington, J Dickenson, J Halliwell, J Hawthorn, R Henshall, C Jackson Holmes, F T Lashford, F' Lindley, J T Lockrr, Dr McKee, J Pemberton, H Roberts, J Sankey, J Skelland, T S Steel, 0 Toole, J Wilkinson and W J Wilson ; Messrs J L Viaaittle (Town Clerk), T II Spinks (Aisistaot Town Clerk), J Fairhurst: (Borough Treasurer), T longdin (Borough Surveyor), W S Haddock (Gas Engineer), F V L Mathias (Electrical Engineer), J M Murray (Director of Education) and Dr J C Ilibbert (Medical Officer). 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>CONDOLENCE ACKNO NLEDGED</head>
        <p>CONDOLENCE ACKNO NLEDGED</p>
        <p>The Mayon read a letter from Miss M. White, Holly House, conveying sincere thanks for the resolution of sympathy passed by the Wuncil on the death of the late Dr. Charles White. The family were indeed grateful for the universal kineness and sympathy sho-rn to them.</p>
        <p>Mr. EDWARD R. PICKNEKRE wrote thanking the Council for their vote of condolence on the death of Mrs. Pickmere,a former Mayoress of Warrington. Mrs l'ickmere always took the f eenest interest in any matters relating to Warrington and it was a great trouble to her that during for somewhat long illness she was unable to take part in the public life of the town and especially that she wis prevented from con tinning her wotk at the Infirmary which she to much loved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>ILLNESS OP MRS. W. J. FORSHAW.</head>
        <p>THE ILLNESS OP MRS. W. J. FORSHAW.</p>
        <p>The MAron said he was sorry for the a tv.oce of Alderman Fernhaw, and he was sure they would kin with him in express,ha great forrow at the cense oh his absence, which they all knew. (liar, beer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>OAKWOOD AVENUE SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>THE OAKWOOD AVENUE SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS moved the adopti•or of the minutes of the Ednoatijit Committes. Alderman 11F.NNETT took occasion to draw attention to the acceptance of plans fir the Oakwood-avenue Council school. Ile did not take:exception to the plans themselves, but he thought the suggeq-d laying-out of the site and buildings might lie improved up. n. Alderman BOLTON Vliat be wan's to say is tat the architects, the committee and every body else are i wrong and he s right. Alderman BENNY:IT said that if the plans were sent to the Board of Education n 1111,114 reeervatbn, it might be too late to consider the position of the buildingt• Notwithstanding the fact that at its last meeting the Sites and Buildings Committee authorized the Director to consult the architects as to whether the lay-out was the best. When they contemplated spending about £12,000 they wanted to gr t the beet possible results. The 111 A von : 1 am afraid it is a matter for the Education Committee. It is not a question of finance that you are raising. Councillor PEMBERTON pointed out that in preparing the plans the architects hai been tied down to a certain coat per head. If the committee insisted unlit altera- tions it might be that that cost %mild have to be aim erb.cl, and then it would destroy the conditions of , competition and would not bi far to the others who , had sent in plans. Councillor lionsatrs said the fi -anciul (pus ion was not before the Education C ammit•ee at Ibis stage. They were only discussing thef acceptance of plans. It would be competent later on for the Council to rejoct or modify the scheme on the ground of finance. Councillor l'lntligßTON : If we pass this minute, it will mean that the Council agrees that the plans are to be passed just as they stand, and it is your last word on it.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : Have you any &amp;finite ol j ,ction to the plan.? Councillor PKNI BERTON : Pees, nally, I hare not. I don't believe in altering them. If you begin to chop and change, you are altering the conditions of competition. Alderman TrroN remarked that Alderman Bennett ' had said practically the same thing at the meeting of the Sites and Buildings Committee,when the matter was thoroughly gone iuto, end Aldeirnan Bennett was the only member of the committee aho had any objection to the laying-.dit of the plans. The committee were convinced that it was the only possible arrangement from an educational point of view. They mere guided in coming to a decision by blr. Longdin and 11r.blurtay,and they also had the advice of the Medical Officer. The plans were very suitable and would give satisfaction. The arrangement might not please Alder- man Bennett but that dil not moan that the com- mittee bad not done the most sensiule thin. Alderman BBNNE rr said that after Alderman Tinnion's spoech he had no option but to move that tho minute be referred back.</p>
        <p>COUlleilivr ARCHER thought it was a reflection on local architects tbst Alder nsn Bennett should raise objection to the plans. Alderman BFNNETT: It is not, at all. Tl,e Mavoit said he did not see that any definite point bad been raised. The minutes were approved, Alderman Bennett being the only dissentient.</p>
        <p>INCREASE OF OFFICIALS' SALARIES. THE CHIEF CONSTABLE.</p>
        <p>The ?vetch Committee presented the following reso- lution :—That the salary of Mr. Martin Nio'lolls (Chief C, n,table) he increased from £lOO to £IO per annum.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>DEPUTY TOWN CLERK.</head>
        <p>THE DEPUTY TOWN CLERK.</p>
        <p>It was resolved th it the salary of the Deputy Town Clerk (Mr Holloway) be increased from £3OO to £lOO per annum by the following increments :-50 as and from the let July, 1312 ; £25 as and from the lot July, 1913 ; £25 as and from the I t July, 1914. Both thmo resolutions were passed without comment. - DANGEROUS PLACES. -</p>
        <p>- The Street Improvement Committee instructed the Surveyor to prepare a list of all dangerous places in the borough, and that danger signsla be erected in such places. PROPOSED NEW ROAD AT WILDERSPOOL.</p>
        <p>The Street Improvement Committee also instructed the Surveyor to report upon all possible alternative methods of cons'a noting a new road to avoid the level crossing at Wilderspool. Alderman BENNETT, in moving the adoption of the minu: CP, said he was glad the committee was n facing the question in a broad, statesmanlike way. They were all familiar aith the danger spot on Wilderspuol Cause- way, leading to Chester New-road. Various suggestions bad to en made from time to time as to the bast method of mininthing the danger. Ilia personal feel'ng was that anyhow, and apart from any larger improvement, it was desirable that that danger spot should be removed, but Councillor Bonier) properly contended that While they were doing that they ought to consider the whole position in all its bearings. That was a sound proposal, but it meant a considerable outlay Chen there was the questien of the level crossings which bad been a bugbear to Warrington for many years. The difficulty had alwapi been how to get over that inconvenience and danger in a way which would not be a disfigurement to the town or too expensive. The committee had thought over the matter at many meetings and various suggestions had been made. They bad now instructed the Borough Surveyor to report on all possible alternativo methods of constructing a new road to avoid the level crossings. The Instruction was as wide as it was possible to make it and in carrying out such ao important work, which w mid form an approach to the new bridge, they mist coasider appeir- ance as well as con%epience</p>
        <p>Tt'e NlAvou seconded and the minutes were approved SHOPS ACT, 1912. TILE WEEKLY HALF-HOLIDAY.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Health Committte, petiti, n from 17 tobacconists asking for a closing order for tobatwo shops on Thursday fr ms 1.30 p.m., was read, and instructions were given for the preparation of a regittsr of all persons selling tobscm in Warrington. A petition from 16 hairdressers asking the Council to make an order fixing Thursday as the weekly half-holiday in respect of their businiss, and also the hours of doting on other days, as spe2ified in the closing order now in fore. was read, and inktructions were given for the preparation of a register of all hairdressers in Warrington. A petition was read from the Off•Licence Holders' Asso- ciation in NVarrington asking for exemption for mixed businesses from the prov'sions of the Act, and it wits pointed out that the off-licence holders were already exempt for the sale of intoxicating liquors. It was retolyed that the association he informed accordingly, and that the committee cannot grant exemption for mixed businesses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>mina( a %ere remed. ALLEGED EX rRAVAGANT PRICES FOR HORSES.</head>
        <p>The mina( a %ere remed. ALLEGED EX rRAVAGANT PRICES FOR HORSES.</p>
        <p>_ The mibutrs of the Sanitary Corr niitiel included the following : 4th Jene. he Clakirman laid before the committee a c. talogue of horses for sale by auction at NVrexham on Thursday next, the nth instant,- resolved that the Chairman, Mr. Alderman James Evans, the Cleansing Superintendent, and the Veterinary Surgeon</p>
        <p>had, however, filed to turn tia. Did Councillor 'Ash- ford not admit that he said his business would not allow Lim to go 7 Councillor LAsliFnn: No. I don't remember saying Such a thing. Alderman EVANS continuing said Councillor Lat-h ford had to n proposed as a member of the horses and stables sub-committee hecaus3 of his expert knovel,dge. (A Voßce : "Question.") The member could question the statement if he liked, but what be had said was quite true. No animal at the sale was offered for less than 85 to 99 guineas, and some were offered at 100 guineas. The wish of the Ciuncil was that the Cor- poration should own good animals. The average life of the horses they had was 11.1 year;, and that wee value for rn niey. (Ile ir, hear ) If their horses could rest in the stable at night instead of during the daytime, the average would be more wonderful still. Alderman JoriN EVANS said he had been a mertber of the committee for seventeen or eighteen years and during the whole of that time their policy had been to get the best quality of horses. That was the cheapest method in tho long run. Ile diJ not think any horse had been bought for less than 50 guineas and the lea were higher now than they us3d to b?. Alderman JAMES EVANS having consulted the records of the committee, said that Councillor [Ashford was appointed a r.eruber of the Dories and Stables Sub-committee° in December, 1909, and since that data had been summoned to every meeting, not only in regard to the purchase of horses but also of provender. Councillor LAsitFortn : I have not been put on the Purchasing Committee. Alderman JAMES EVANS The Stables Committee is the Purchasing Committee and you have had notice of every meeting. Councillor LASH FORD : I haven't had an invitation to go to Wrexham. Councillor ROBERTS, in justifying the policy of the committee, said he had had to do with horses for nearly 40 years, and had paid as high a price as the Corporation had pail for animals for his own mecum! use. In horse flesh, as in everything else, they could not get a good article without paying a good price for it. (hlcer, hear.) Taking the average they got very good results. lii was proud of the hors .s whic'i the Warrington Corporation °Ailed to-day. In spite of the development of motor traction, the prices of atoll-bred horses, capable of doing heavy work, had gone up. The amendment was defeated, only Councillor's Lashford and Pernberton voting for it. The minutes were then approved. PROPERTY OWNERS AND THE PAVING OF PRIVATE STREETS.</p>
        <p>Councillor llEN,itau, asked for information with reference to a minute of the Paving and Sewerage Committee to the effect that a deputation fr.un the Property (SA ners' Ass Nciation htl attended and placed before the committee the views of the association upon private streets.</p>
        <p>CoUtlCilior STEEL asked what wcra the view of the association ?</p>
        <p>Alderman-TINNIoN said the views of the deputation were many and various—mostly selfish. When he said "selfish " he meant to save their own pockets. The matter was such a gigantic one that it could not be settled in a few minutes, days or weeks. There were at present no tenders for new streets. They had all been let for the work in hand and there was no necersity for a change until they were preparing for other tenders. The committee had had n 9 time to take the matter into consideration. The deputation asked for a great deal more than Viey were likely to get or, he thought, they hoped to get. (Laughter). Councillor HENSIIALL What was in my mind— The MAYOR said that, if Councillor flenahall wished to make a speech, he should have done so before the Chairman .replied.</p>
        <p>Couocillor'Pcmii CRTON : Ile c mid not. Councillor IlEvsitaLt. : Whit I was going to say was dependent on the answer of the Chairman. Alderman TINNIOS : Then you ought to have given notice. It is not fair that a Chairman should be expected to reply to any conundrum that may be put before him. Councillor PENIBERTON : I appeal to the Town Clerk. (Cries of " Order ''). The minutes were then pissed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, July, 13, 1912.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July, 13, 1912.</p>
        <p>be appointed to attend the sale with full authority to purchase two suitable horaes.-7th June. The com- mittee met and inspected the two horses purchased at the auction sale at Wrexham, at the following prices : Bay gelding, 9l guinea, dark brown gelding 87 guineas. Councillor SANKEY having moved the confirmation of the minutes, Councillor LASIIFORD contended that the prices paid were extravagant. There were scores of horses, at half the price, which would have been more suitable for the purpose for which the Corporation required them. It appears that the committee wanted the horses fur show purposes and were prepared to pay any price. The only explanation the Chairman hid to offer was that the bidding was run up. There was, however, no need to buy the la( rses. He moved that the minute be referred book for _reconsideration.</p>
        <p>Councillor I'KMIIERTON seconded and said too much money was given for the horses for the purpose for which they were wanted. He did not think there were many members of the Council who would be prepared to pay such priers in their own pi ivate businesses, unless they were purchasing for show purposes. The Corpora- tion ought net to throw money away like that. Several Warrington contractors would never think of giving even hslf these prices fur horses. It looked as if money was no object. It would be all right to give the prices named if they sere going in for breeding, to exhibit at a show to win a prize, of if they were millionaires. (Laughter.) Ninety-one guineas fur a hors 3 was a ridiculous i rice. Itailfflay compinies did not give On h pries. Alderman 1301.T0N : .£6O is enough. . .</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON further contended that it was improper, according to the standing orders, to carry out a pirchase of over £2O without ads ertising. Aldermai JAMES EVANS said the question of the price given fur horses was discussed year by year, and the discus3ion always started from the same quarter, namely, from Councillor Lashford,who bad been invited again and again Councillor_ ',ASHFORD : Never.</p>
        <p>Aldetrran EVANS : That is questioning my word. he MAYOR (to Councillor Lashford) :—Did you know what Alderman Evans was going to say—what the invitation was?</p>
        <p>Councillor LASIIFORD: Yes. Ths MAYOR : 1 d:d not. (Lnu,gl.tcr.) Ald•rman Bvatis : What was it? Councillor LASHFORD I have not been in. ited to purchase any horses }lit. Alderman JAMES EVANS said Councillor Lashford had been rung up from the Town Hall to attend 'ales, arid a looter car had been left tohind to take him to the place where tho horscs were t ho purchased. lie</p>
        <p>laid out on somewhat similar lines to Queen's Gardens (('No, No "), The throwing open of the ground to the children had he tinderstood, met with objections from many of the dwellers in the neighbourhood, tie did not however, think of that, because whether people were rich or poor they had no right to object to the children having reasonable opportunities of recreation. Alderman BOLTON : What is he talking about? The MAYOR: Order, order, please. Alderman BENNETT : If Alderman Bolton would listen he would appreciate what I am trying to talk about. lam really fond of Alderman Bolton, but when he gets on this tack I never can trust myself to ray what I think of him. (Laughter.) It is extraordinary how much I like him In spite of his faults—(laughter)— and I don't want to say anything offensivo to him. But really this is a matter of importance and he ought to have the courtesy to listen. My point is that the committee has over-ridden the Council. Councillor ROBERTS said the question of what the committee had or had not done was not before the Council. The only resolution was one ordering a special meetiog of the committee to bo convened to visit the recreation grounds. Under cover of that, Alderman Bennett bad very cleverly brought in an attack upon the committee on another matter. The MAYOR : It is a question of policy with regard to the management of the recreation grounds. What is the object of visiting them, if remarks upon the results of .the visits are not to be allowed ?</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT : Now is the accepted time, now is the day of silvation for the comrniet, e. (Laughtei). I have not mmb faith in them, but I. h -pa before they go further on their ill-advised course they will reflect and try to do something to make the best of tha open spaces. Continuing, the Alderman expressed satisfac- tion that the committee contemplated the provision of a lake in Victoria Park, and were approaching the Ellesmere trustees with a view to acquiring land for the purp, se. He only wished they would obtain the whole of the adjacent land. If it could be bought on reasonable torms, it would be an immense advantage to the town.</p>
        <p>Councillor CRAIK said it was wrong for the com- mittee to bare taken upon thzrnsr Ives the opening of the Park before the Council had given them permission to do so. The site in question was too valuable for children to use as a playground, and land had been obtained for that purpose near the school in Gorsey- -1 ne.The park was also on the main road, and there was danger to passengers and others when the children were playing at cricket, owing to the low hurdles. lii July, 198, a resolution was passed by the Council that the committee prepare plans and estimates for laying out the land.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>FEEDING THE STRIKERS' CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>FEEDING THE STRIKERS' CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>The strike of transport workers in the from the poverty-stricken homes of the e(eru. Port of London has entered upon its eighth strikers Are fed daily, and ffor week, and there is no immediate prospect of t(titsr piPeititrtrP°els.fhown-srea laeragrenicistitinybe raos f cthildr a settlement. Hundreds of hungry children being fed in a schoolroom in Poplar.</p>
