      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>ST. ELPIIIWS PARE{ AS A PLAYGROUND.</head>
        <p>ST. ELPIIIWS PARE{ AS A PLAYGROUND.</p>
        <p>"SEWING THE COUNCIL AT DEFIANCE." The Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee had resolved that a special meeting be convened at an early date for the purpose of visiting the bowling greens and recreation grounds in the borough. Alderman BENNETT said that in &amp;fiance of a res)lu- tion of the Council the "committee had thrown one of the sracos op-n to the public, without any notice or authority, lie referred to that in St. Elphin's Park, which was now being used as a play ground for children. He was glad to see the children enjoying themselves there, as it was desirable that there should be some playground in the district, but it was clearly the intention of the Council that the committee should submits scheme for the laying out of the land. If they had done so, they wonl.l not have perpetrated the abom- ination to which be had repeatedly referred in connedion with the bowling green lie was in hopes that some- thing would have been done to hide that monstrosity and that the remainder of the ground would have been effectively laid out. The idea of the Council was that the place should be treated in a different manner and</p>
        <p>A VOICE : Are you a member of the committee? Councillor CRAM.: I don't altogether enjoy being a member. (Laughter). It is a committee which passes any amount of resolutions but does not act up to them Teem is only one committee that passes more resolu- tions and that is the Town Planning Committee. (Laughter). Councillor LocEErt supported the protest of Alder- man Bennett and Councillor Craik and contended that the land should be utilized to better advantage. Ile %as assured however that, the arrangement was only temporary, and that the land would ultimately be laid out to the sati faction of everyone concerned. Ile hoped a scheme would he ,uruitted at the next meeting. Coun-illor PEAteErteobt : The Town Hall Committee is always settin; this Council at di fiance. The Chair- man pleases hms 'lf chat he d ; the committee have nothing t, do with it. (Laughter.) Things are carried out in a way that is only fit for coruia opera. (Laughter.) IA lay the Council allows it I don't know. If I had my way, r would " eack " the Chairman. (Laughter.) She MAYOR : It is not on the minutes. Councillor PFMRERT‘,N : No, it is a rity it is not on. (Laughter.) A resoluti n was passed four years ago, but nothing has been done. Why ? Because the Cisirman of the Committee does not want to do it. , You all stand it like lambs and lie down like good little school children. (Laughter.) When this Council passes a resolution, it ought to be carried our, Alder- mad Bolton or no Alderman Bolton, If Alderman Bitten is not in his place I wool I put him in it. The MAYOR : We are not discussing Alderman Bo'ton now. (Laughter). Counc•llor PEMBERTON : No, we are discussing this resolution. The MAYOR : You are not. Councillor l'ExtemroN : It is time the Council went round to all the bowling greens and open spaces in the town. We are responsible to the ratepayers for the good conduct and proper upkeep of those places. If the committee won't do their duty, the Council ought to do it for them. The MAYOR : I haveallowed the discussion,although it, is debatable whether it is in order. When I heard that Alderman Bolton simply pulled away some palling!' and allowed the children to go in there and then, I thought that was a very undignified and irregular manner of opening a park. %Vlso L found the matter bad not been discussed by the committee or pawed by the Council, I sympathised with those members of the Council who are protecting against it. I have allowed the discussion, not because I thought it was strictly regular, but because the circumstances %arrant it. Councillor AslicROFT expressed the hope that the park would be kept open to the children. The Council had paid dearly for the land and he hoped they would see it was provided with a rings and properly adapted for the children. They must not be influenced by people who would deprive the children of the privilege of playing in the park. Alderman BOLTON: I want to know how the position stands. Are we discussing these minutes or something about my private ch tractor or opinions ? (To the Mayor) : I think you must put your foot down. I must insist upon it. The MAYOR: lam the Chairman. Alderman JAMES EVANS said the Council had passed a resolution that the park should not be used as a play- ground until the committee had decided what form the laying-out should take. To open the park in the manner described by the Mayor was not, a dignified procedure. Councillor SEEEL said he was surpri,sed at the remarks which had been made by Alderman Bennett and Coun- cillors Crra and Locker He passed the park several times a day and he could assure the Council that when the gene.al public saw the children playing in it, some very complimentary remarks were made. Councillor CEASE: Nonsense. Councillor Sum, : It, is not nonsense at all. I dare say the remarks of Alderman Bennett and Councillors Craik and Looker may be the views of a few people who live near that particular spot, but they do not represent the opinions of the general public in the ward. I came prepared to congratulate the Chairman and members or the committee and I feel sure the Council will agree that the proper thing to be done is to throw open the park for the children to play in. Councillor DICKF.NSON said he was surprised at the attitude taken up by Alderman Bennett, because he was told on good authority that, the Alderman went into the park when the children were playing there and expressed himself delighted to see them enjoying themselves.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT : SO I was. Councillor DICKENSON : And then he went so far as to throw a lot of coppers among the children. (Laughter). Alderman BENNETT : Quite right. Councillor DICK ENSON : Having exprersed his delight at seeing the ground thrown open to the children, he comes along here and condemns the com- mittee for their action in opening the Park. Alderman TlliiilON Look what money it cost him. (Laugh ter). Councillor DICKENSON said he was told the opposition to the opening of the Park came from six property owners who owned six hotne.s —one each, (Laughter). They moat, as a Council, think more of Pie ratepayers generally than of six property owners. (fear, hear.) Councillor J ‘ck,o‘t llor..at IN said his idea of an open space had always been a place something like Victoria Park, where the children could ramble unimpeded and without any particular regulations such as were to be found in Queen's Gardens. It was conceivable that</p>
        <p>the children might be a sourco ,ome annoyance to those who lived in the immediate vicinity, but on the whole his sympathies were with the children.lle did not trouble much about the irregular way in which the park bad been opened, but he must say he was delighted to see the children playing in that unrestricted way. It was a very great improvement on having them running about the etre( ts in great danger of being knocked down by the tram cars or by one of the furious motor cars which so frequently passed along the road. Alderman BURTON pointed out that the opening of the park to the children was note permanent measure. It was only, in the first instance, for a short time. The MAYOR said it was a bad precedent to open the park and then close it again. Alderman BURTON Eaid that if the Council assented to the opening of the park, he was sure it would be a boon to thousluds of children.</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON : I dOn't-see what I have to reply to. All this Iris been irregular. It is not on the minutes.</p>
        <p>- The MAYOR : I beg to remind you that my ruling trade it regular and you have no right to cont.( st it. Alderman liourox The standing orders-- The MAYOR: You have no right to contest my ruling. Alderman BOLTON : Is it your ruling that I mus answer all these irregular remarks? The MAYOR: No, just as you like. You can answer them or not---</p>
        <p>Aleorman BOLTON H I do, I shall arswer thorn in such a style that some of them will know something about it. (Laughter.) If you wi nt me to stick to the minutes, I %ill do so and say there is nothing to answer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>minutes were then confirmed. HOWLEY BRIDGE. NO ItISKS TO BE RUN.</head>
        <p>The minutes were then confirmed. HOWLEY BRIDGE. NO ItISKS TO BE RUN.</p>
        <p>Alderman SMETHURST, in moving the adoption of the minutes of the Bridges Committee, said all possible haste was being made with tho erection of Howley Bridge. Unfortunately some parts were lost on the railway and they had to telegraph and telephone all over the country before they came to hand. It ells hoped the bridge might be opened in time for the fair in Victoria Park,but it was impossible to say definitely at present. It would bo a great boon to the public if they were able to do so. _</p>
        <p>A'lderman 13Elq-Nric-s-a.id be was very pleased with the appearance of the new bridge, although he always wanted a road bridge to be erected at Howley. He would, however, utter a word of warning. He knew the enthusiasm of Alderman Smethurst and the com- mittee, and their anxiety to complete the bridge, but he hoped that in their desire to accelerate the opening they would not run any risk. If the strueture were not properly tested and full precautions were not taken, it was corceivable that the opening might synchronise with a catast7.,The. The MAYOR : I will tent it by gr log over fi at. (Laughter) Alderman BENNETT : Your bine ore not heavy enough to break the wire hawsers. (Laughter). Alderman ; METinutxsT said it would be most unwise for anyone to advise the opening of Ors bridge until the m rmissien of the ern inner had been obtaieed. He (the Alderman) would be the last, person in the world to advise the opening of the bridge before it was abso- lutely safe. _</p>
        <p>Councillor JACKSON IJOLMr rai el the (pee ion as to whether, after the erection cf the bridge, it was intended to leave the road through Victoria Park open all night. It was stated that ro c!eflnite decision had bt en arrived at on the point. Alderman JA MES Ev.tss said one of the great advan- tages of the 13:idgs would be to afford Lei itiss to workmen who had to go to work in the early hours of the day. Councillor PENIBERTON : Will the chairman of the committee tell us ehrn the fist half of Warrington Bridge will be opened ? The MAYOR: The Chairman has replied. Councillor PEM BERTON : Under Standing Order 26 The 1111vott : The minutes are disposed of. Councillor PEMBERTON : I did not know that. I thought the C rairmen was only answerirg the questions which had I e 3n put to him. The MA YORR : I am sorry, but you have been unobservant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>BOY'S FACE BAD WITH RED SPOTS.</head>
        <p>BOY'S FACE BAD WITH RED SPOTS.</p>
        <p>Baldwin, little Easton, Dunmow, Essex. Eng.— " My little b ty's face was bad for twelve months or more. It came with a lot of little red spots with white heads to them, aid then it got all over like a dirty looking scab. Ile used to scratch it and it used to run and be made it bleed. It was not fit to look at; it made you feel sick to see it. The child did not got any rest with it, so I had to tie Lis hands up as he used to scratch it, and it was a bad sore. It was worse at night after begot in bed. They told me his face would be dreadfully marked. "I had him treated first, and it did not get any better, and then I read of -- and I spent eight shillings on that and it was no better. Then I sent after a sample of Cuticura Soap and Ointment and the first week they bad cleaned it all off and he was getting quite new skin. After using the next box of Cuticura Ointment and the Cuticura Soap he had a new face. Cuticura Soap and Cuticura Ointment made him well in a week." (Signed) Mts. J. Staines, Jan. 17, 1912. Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere. A sample of each with 32-p. book post free from nearest depot Newbery,27, Charterhouse sq , Loudon ; R. Towns and Co., Sydney, N. S. W. ; Lennon, Ltd., Cape Town ; Maclean and Co., Calcutta and Bombay; "Cuticura,' Boston, U.S.A. 'render-faced men should shave with Cuticura Soap Shaving Stick. Sample free.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>MINIATURE RIFLE SHOOTING.</head>
        <p>MINIATURE RIFLE SHOOTING.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS CIVILIAN RIFLE CLUB v. APPLETON RIFLE CLUB. The Appleton team fulfilled the return engagement with the St. Helens Club, on Saturday last. The weather was flue, with a moderate wind blowing, necessitating an allowance of about three inches of I wind at the 100 yards range. The conditions of the match were :—Eleven men aside, the brat eight scores to count; ten shots, at a decimal target, at 25, 50, and 100 yards ; possible score at each range, 100 ; S.M.R C. scoring and legulations. The Appleton team again reghtered a victory, as they did when St. llelens visited them in June and are naturally proud of their achievement, as all previous engagements wit's this club had gone against them. The individual and total scores are as follows : ST. HELENS. 25 50 100</p>
