        <p>t If ,riv% " most position in the van of fashion andlo Vour ac3orded itithas on its , to have firmly established itself in the gee° !'atie• is • dressmakers and their customers. lint adicioa ese made between a fussy and a draped skirt. bee el Inartistic, inelegant, and its fussiness ~,,ti0,,a„•06 raison d'otre, whereas the other is both sr •I•ool`,,•ett elegant and serves a useful purpose—since ;tr....to:be the outlines of the wearer's figure and setts' Jet 'ler arranged by an expert adds to the slimness used ot, woman given to embonpoint. '1 he materials litre the draped skirt must however be of the softest, de(' even flimsiest description. Then there is o,,rofirkiEte to the actual fulness, and the silhouette, still e`;ci iff • a feature of the dress of the day, is pfeserv- s entirety. efe,be Trifles in Dress.—The women of this 00115•,; is "to accustomed to be charged with carelessaes- ore be matter of details of the toilette, that very fevi,,iest take the trouble to defend themselves o.g-weePlie charge, which, like the greater number eli;t tiort'ity assertions, may be applied to the majoritY oiSsp the whole community. In this case the 0003-0 certainly study detail as carefully as any Freec otbo7 and these are our really well-dressed woree2, wesr„-0. ke the clothes they wear ni,y in themselves to la-or sire. Of the charming trifles that go so ,s'essosa, t a dress a success there seems no limit tbia 910P41.j5 flchus, jabots and dainty c3llars. A stock tie 'Ling muslin, quite new in design, has wide flred4e,° '0 which are scalloped and embroidered at turf' be lie button-hile stitch. The ends are crossed en- s. ;b. p2sition by one or two of the new long brooChed, for ere, tie costs shillings in the shops, but could be °at hera few pence by anyone who °wild US) a ne0C11,18: aistlithe Anothei novelty is a sash of ribbon or ve'4dtd'e 0; of star- like arrangement of loops in the lar re,' the back, that gives a finished look to a simPte t cotton. Long ends of ve