      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>• DISTRICT PI W 3</head>
        <p>• DISTRICT PI W 3</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>Lymm. 1 pelii:E DROWNING FATALITY.—The remains of Alfred stO'val, the little boy who was found drowned on the their: as reported in our last issue, were interred in snob Churchyard on Tuesday last. PARISH CHURCH.—DIOCeSIM Sunday was TC rated at the Lymm Parish Church on Sunday last. thee Rector the Rev G B Thurston, 'M.A., officiated at after Communion and morning service. In the raoon a children's service, addressed by the Rev th°rlca„ °IV, was held. The Rector, who officiated at the 'yenedng, spoke about the various objects to which e days offertories were to be devoted. L, CLARION CLUE MEETlNG.—tinder the auspices of the &amp;!'et10513.111 Clarion Cycling Club, a public meeting on , '448°3 was held at Lymm Cross on Sunday atter- inc'e• The chief speakers were Mr C Dukes, of War- tqlten, and Mr T Didsbury, of Esrlestown.—Several eestions were asked, to which Mr Dukes gave very etipc‘id,answers. The sentiments expressed by one of the zle'aiEers detrimental to Sabbath observance did not ,at With the approval of the majority of his hearers. alopp skit `- COLONISTS.—Amongst the passengers who 415 ed from Liverpool on Thurdsay on the C.P R. Co.'s it atter were two Lymm brothers Messrs Thomas ..vellrY, and Frank Appleton. Mr T H Appleton who is led known locally has for many years been in charge ,11tr Gotobed's gentlemen's clothing shop in the tkibage• Both brothers are en route for Medicine Hat, erta, Canada, where they have relations and friends. kind wishes for their welfare have been Pressed LCA-11PAI 'ester bi I.tectr</p>
        <p>xoeoov.—The quarterly meeting of the wench of the Behringers' League was held on ,ory lawn on Saturday afternoon. Visiting from Grappenhall, Stretton and St. Paul's Warrington, were present. At the conclusion eisiness the ;Rev G B Thurston followed his custom of entertaining the members to tea. ea the party adjourned to the belfry where .onches were rung on the tells. PARTY.—The members of the Lymm Avis Methodist Church held their annual garden 1 in the grounds of Foxley House, Broomedge' residence of Mr H Champ, whose kind 1,55it00 for their use had been given. ite the threatening disposition of the weather, Was a very large attendance, and the motor cars 'g from Lymm and Heatley respectively had a very ,time. During the afternoon and early part of the log several competitions both for skill and guess. Were held, and provided much amusement, Illy so in the cases of the hat trimming and nail ones. Excellent teas were served by a band of 7orkers, under the charge of Mrs. Edwards, ,ut 4.30 to 6.30, and all who partook of same iusiastic in its praise. There was a continuous entertainments in the form of clock-golf, an tub, Kinora pictures, cocoa-nut shy, donkey weighing-in," and tours of inspection of the Ily laid-out Hower gardens and extensive gardens. Other centres of attraction were on which fancy and useful articles were for .h Mrs Whiteway in charge and the refresh- ill under the care cf Miss D Smith. In the the Rev R W B Whiteway announced the if the competitions and distributed the prizes ;s:—Ladies hat trimming, Miss Seville; gents, gets; nail driving, Mrs. Morgacstein ; potato khan Radcliffe ; 2, Percy Clare ; taper relay 3. Champ's team ; clock—golf (ladies), Mrs ; (gents), Mr A B Edwards ; guessing number Slates, Mr N Beond ; guessing circumference of , Miss Shaw. No one guessed the doll's name ket w 5" Mignonette." leateFencert was afterwards held in which Messrs. A enews and Nightingale played pianoforte duets; Mr filierersws charmed the audience witn his violin solos. -7„vlsze gave a capital recitation. Mr J Ellis sang "narney " and " The Children's Home." Mr .3 H ale acceded to the request for a song by giving e into the garden Maud"- Very popular items given by the male voice glee party under Mr e Good items being " When evening "Baby on the Shore," " The comrades song kce °Pe " and Farewell." Mr F Champ acted as a"b?Panist to the solos. it grew dusk the grounds were prettily illuminated eeirY lamps. Mr and Mrs H Coarnp proved to be a ablrc'us hosts and Messrs H Burrell, F Champ and "er capable M.C.'s. A POPULAR LYMAI WEDDING. A es WY interesting and pretty wedding took place at "'arra Parish Church, Lymm, on Wednesday morn- The contracting parties were Miss Marion Fair- 41t Ardern, younger daughter of Mr and Mrs James ,ern, of South View, Lynam, and Mr Thomas Broom ,Idby, of Lowdham, Notts, second son of Mr and iV Whitby, of Primrose Villa, ileatley. Thecere- a.was performed by the Rector, the Rev G B 011, M.A. assisted by the Rev FC Bozman, M-A, ; 01 New Springs, Wigan. The bride, who was Etway by her father, was becomingly arrayed in a robe of fine ivory lace over silk, tastefully by the bridegroom. The tunic was slashed at side and caught up with a spray of orange The corsage was of cross-over design,the fischu - was caught at the waist with a gold brooch, Of of the bride's mother. She also wore a wreath ren,ge blossom with a veil of white Brussells net. 4113, other ornament, besides the-beautiful bouquet lte lilies which she carried, was a gold chain and 111158'V, the gift of the bridegroom. Her bridesmaid, I)3ldirh E. Ardern, sister, was dressed in a charming trie"ine gown of silk eolienne, with a black chip hat hilled with pale blue roses to match. She also wore Send locket, the gift of the bridegroom, and carried a efe2elet of white lilies. Mr. W. Frith of St. Annes on l'eted as best man. The conclusion of the ceremony ,„ big ee,gnelled to those outside the Church by the ring- to the bells whose merry. peals were kept up for a tridinerame time. On coming out of the Church the riite! found a pleasant surprise awaiting her, for on rtiside of the path leading from the porch was a Ilree?, girls from the National School who strewed i;re on the path before her. Despite the desire that 414 "f of the ceremony should be kept quiet there was VI% number of people present at the Church and as tiderat... and bridegroom were entering the car they ste , Vest_eq much good humoured attention from the ,rhators. Sale bride, who has for the past ten years been in " the Girls' Department (chiefly Standard 1,44;1 the National Schools, Lymm, was most popular tate, elle teachers, scholars and their parents, and many te signs of their good feeling have been shown e fteasion of her wedding. In fact among the eoue3; valuable and choice presents which the bridal {eV have been the fortunate recipients, were %rho from all the teachers in the Infants' and }lie e" schools and thirty from the scholars. On ireL43' from the church the old village custom of " the carriages with ropes was a further 33.4 If the bride's popularity. The wedding breakfast !tlast,,ueeld at " South View." the bride's home, the "elne confined to the two families to which the esill and bridegroom belong. The departure of the 11443' tennPle for North Wales, where they are spending ueart neneymoon, was the occasion of a shower of sieY,congratulations, and a stock of old boots and /hth; 'rota the large and enthusiastic crowd that had ~ elee eted round. The bride's travelling dress was a stpre fret4',4)stume with silk hat to . match. On returning tesiee'' °rth Wales the bridal pen- will take up their t bee at Locksley Villa, Geddling, Notts. , e4fts°,l`„ Sceues HOLD SALE OF WOHK.—On Saturday "•?, je:4 and evening last, the Heatley Troop of the larrittn, Scouts held a display and sale of work in the etee, Situated field adjoining Heatley Railway e()n, kindly lent for the occasion by Mr S Davies. rokrutral portion of the ground was occupied by the the a r sports course, and dancing space, while near ttt tee d eetrance a large marquee was pitched. It was ge rent f erremi nt of this marquee that the proceedings were 11148te °lnened shortly after 3 o'clock when Scout- ta T Nash stepped forward into the space reserved th"eeerdon of scouts and in a few words said 'Vrlge,ouglit it hardly necessary to introduce Mrs to the company present. He said that Mr and res ned Peet gwaY had always been great and highly Ohs ed friend 3 t the local scouts and he had much silre o Pra In asking her to open their sale of work.— eadewae then gave the opening address. ?bjeez Charming way she described the main 51 of the scout movement—the scout's future. e ove the scouts," she said, " not for what they teellhiztp but for what they will become. The 4113 f "11 goes straight to every boy's heart and 100,.9rth high ideals and it is in these boys that we etoelie"rig for the men of the future, surely they will jealti,,lnen of whom their country %ill be proud." 4ieeia gu; -R of the untiring efforta of the seo leaders 3311 y thli eq that ir Scoutmasters Webb, jun., eftern that (they might have some encouragement shown i therts ay• She said that the aim of the present C01"7"' to raise funds to take the troop sway to O teclrrle. ef Tent for a short holiday, and after describing 111 s 5 novelties one obtained on a first trip tire, 75 asked for the generous support of all present ---enable them to carry out their scheme. 2---</p>
