        <p>She then declared the Sale of Work open. Mr Jones proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs Ridgway. Scout- master J Webb, jun., in seconding the proposition endorsed Mr Nash's remarks about the kindnesses of The proposition was carried with hearty applause f ter which the scouts gave three hearty cheers for Mr and Mrs Ridgway. On entering the marquee one found a large stall on which a numercus' variety of useful and fancy household articles we e exposed for sale. Mesdames Jones, Doan and Littler were the stallholders, whilst Miss Lamb had charge of the dainty afternoon teas which were served in other parts of the marquee. Shortly after the opening the display programme was entered upon. This consisted of fancy cycling, signalling, physical and ambulance drills by the scouts; a picturesque country dance and a Maypole dance in which several varieties of plaiting were creditably performed by a number of pretty maidens who had teen taught by Miss Field; two solo dances by Miss Lucy Jackson of Altrincham, whose graceful movements received well-earned applause, and some aquatic sports comprising a swimming race, won by Jack Robinson, a barrel event and crossing the river Rollin on a greasy pole, the gambols of the competitors causing much fun. After the expiration of the tea interval a programme of sports was commenced. The list showed that the events were numerous, varied, and especially in the cases of the blindfold race, backward race, and football match with the players in sacks,,novel, and with the addition of several close finishes an attractive entertainment was provided. A Jumble sale was also included in the money raising efforts of the day. At the conclusion of the sports Lymm Subscription Band, who had given selections during the afternoon and evening, played for public dancing, which many persons indulged in. It was with regret and surprise that one noted the decreased attendance compared with that of the last similar occasion as both the day and events were most enjoyable ; no doubt the counter attraction at Dunham Park would account for some of the absentees. The field arrangements were executed by Scoutmasters T Nash, and J Webb, jun., and Assistant Scoutmasters F Cowsill and T Kay.</p>
