      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Winwick.</head>
        <p>Winwick.</p>
        <p>FREE CHURCH ANNUAL SUNDAY SCHOOL SERMONS.— The anniversary services in connection with the above Sunday school were held on Sunday, the preacher at the afternoon and evening services being the Rev .1 Morris Bold, of Didsbury College, Manchester. Special hymns and anthems were rendered by the choir, the solos being creditably rendered by Miss Lawson, of Wargrave. Much credit is due to the Superintendent of the Sunday school, Mr J Shallcross, and to M'si Ormiston, for the admirable manner in which the children had been trained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Moore.</head>
        <p>Moore.</p>
        <p>WESLEY GUILD.—On Wednesday evening the monthly devotional meeting was held in the schoolroom. There was a very good attendance to hear Mr A S Elliot who is very well liked in Moore, and his discourses on any subject always seem to command attention from beginning to end. The sublect of Mr Elliot's address Wednesday evening was "Self-sacrifice." He treated it from the paradox, in which so much scriptural teaching is expressed. The speaksr's aim was to illus- trate that, as we think of it. there is really no such thing as "self-sacrifice," true self-sacrifice being simply self-expression. Mr Lawton presided and Mr Sde Prez read the lesson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>Thelwall.</head>
        <p>Thelwall.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF AN OLD INHABITANT.—The funeral took place at Thelwall Church on Saturday of an old inhabitant of the district, in the person of Mrs Beckett, wife of Mr Henry Beckett, of Cedar Villas, Latchford. The Rev T Pym Williamson officiated. Mr and Mrs Beckett lived for many years in Thelwall, where they are highly respected. Miss Beckett is a teacher in Thelwall School, with which the has been connected for many years. The mourners were Mr Henry Beckett (husband), Miss M E Beckett (daughter), Miss B Beckett (daughter), Mr H Beckett (son), Mrs H Beckett (daughter-in-law), Mr and Mrs L Beckett (son and daughter-in-law), Mr A Beckett (son), Mr Robert Hughes, Mr Owen Hughes (nephews), Mrs Bradbury (sister-in-law), Mrs Arthan (sister-in-law) and Mr W B Cook (an old f riend of the family). I.enketn.</p>
        <p>MAIN ROADS A CYCLING RACECOURSE.—St. Mary's Great Sankey Magazine says :—Cycling accident or incident, which is it ? It is much to be regretted there is seemingly no law in Englabd to prevent our maia roads being turned practically into a cycling racecourse. The extent to which this is being carried out on the Penketh and Sankey main road has well nigh become' intolerable. Every Sunday morning during the summer time, at about ten o'clock, cyclists, who evidently are neither Sunday travellers nor ordinary pleasure seekers, rush" like mad" along our roads with no other purpose than that of record breaking. Purhaps, indeed, if they would kindly give us warning by ringing a bell, or give ns notice beforehand that they have appropriated the sole use of the main road tor an hour or so, and that pedestrians on the road at that time are there at their own peril, this might help us ratepayers, citizens, pedestrians, just a little bit, lint it seems it is not necessary for them to do even this. It seems they have as much right to be on the road as you and I have, and as long as they travel or rush, rather softly and quietly, at a rate less than twenty miles an hour (say 19 99-100 miles) then they are within the limits of the law. And presumably this is to go on until something happens some day. The inhabitants of Sankey and Penketh have already signed a petition calling the attention of the police to the condition of things in our neighbourhood. This petition has been forwarded to the Superintendent. The following reply has been received :—" County Police Office, Widnes, 11th June, 1912. Rev Sir,—l beg to acknowledge the receipt of the petition re reckless cycling, &amp;c., through Penketh village, and to inform you that everything possible will be done to put a stop to this dangerous praltice.—Yours faithfully, A. STRICKLAND, superintendent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>NEWTON DIVISION LABOUR DEMONSTRATION The annual demonstration and field day of the Newton Division Labour party was held on Saturday afternoon at Ashton-in-Makerfield. Representatives of miners' lodges and trades unions from Ashton, Haydock, Newton, Earlestown, and other parts of the division assembled at Ashton and marched in procession, aceom- panied by brass bands to Stubshaw Cross, where two platforms had been erected in the football field. Mr. W. Speakman presided at No. 1 platform. Mr. Thorpe proposed, and Mr. German seconded, a resolution stat- ing that it was imperative that all workers should join th.ir trades unions, thanking the Labour party for the , Minimum Wage Bill, and pledging the meeting to secure more Labour representatives.. Miss T 3 nan, Shop Assistants' Union, supported. Mr. Stephen Walsh, M.P., said that was the sixth or seventh of those demonstrations that he had attended, which were held for the purpose of inculcating the necessity of strong combination, both political and industrial, and he was bound to say that be was sick of repeating the same kind of speech. What was the reaaon that they had so many attacks OD trade union- ism? Once upon a time trade unions were regarded as most beneficent organisations, but when the working people, by the growth of a very slow intelligence, began to see that there were better ways of fighting the employers than by striking, then the trade unions were denounced as cruel and dangerous organisa- tions. They should recognise that everything depended upon the working people themselves. They need not blame the capitalists or the landowner. They were simply doing after their kind. It was no good the workers having representatives if they themselves were blind to the signs of the times. Now they were accused of being simply members of a coalition with the Liberals, and of being simply the tail end of the Liberal party. There never was a word of truth in that. (Applause.) They were fighting Hanley and they were fighting Crewe, and it would not be the Labour party's fault if there were not in the near future a bigger number of Labour members in the House of Commons. But more than that. Take every Labour man out of the House of Commons, destroy everyone, sweep the party out of the House altogether— would that destroy and put an end to the Labour move- ment (" No ")? No ; the Labour movement would go on ; the people with their growing needs and their , sense of growing power, the knowledge that they were level with their masters if they cared to exercise their power-all that was a growing force. , Mr. T. Watson presided at No. 2 platform, where the , resolution was proposed by Councillor J. Roberts, , seconded by Mrs. Sutton. Mr. J. A. Seddon, in supporting, remarked that when he was the representative of Newton Division he was charged with breaking all tbe Commandments except that of murder (laughter), and since he had been out the Conservative party had changed almost as often as the wind. Immediately after the last general election it was said by some Tories that "Seddon was all right ; a decent sort of a fellow for all that, and he ought to be in the House of Commons, but not for Newton. (Laughter)." A week or two ago a rumour was floated that he had taken a job at £750 under the Government. That was unmitigated nonsense. He would tell them, that whenever he made up his mind to sell his class it would not be to the Insurance Commissioners; it would be to the Conservative party, who would pay big figures for Judas's from the ranks of the Labour party. Mr. G. H. Roberts also spoke, and the resolution was carried heartily.</p>
        <p>Dr. Adeney's Retirement. The "British Weekly" states that at a 1 recent meet- ing of the committee of the Lancashire Independent College Dr. Adeney foreshadowed his intention to retire from the appointment of principal, at the end of ten years' service, in 1913. Dr. and Mrs. Adeney leave England for Carlsbad on the 15tb inst., and do not ex- pect to return until the first week in September,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>WIDNES CORPORATION APPEAL.</p>
        <p>The Court of Appeal on Monday dismissed the appeal of the Widnes Corporation from the judgment of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor of the County Palatine in the action brought by the Corporation against Mr. Thomas Davies, of Percy-street, Widnes. The laster of the Rolls described the appeal as perfectly hopeless.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>THEFT FROM STALL.</head>
        <p>THEFT FROM STALL.</p>
        <p>SUFFRAGIST SPEAKER FINED FOR STEALING ARTIFICIAL FLOWERS.</p>
        <p>A case arousing great local interest was heard by the Widnes magistrates at the Police Court on Monday, when Mrs. Ellen Boyle,of Irwell-street,wife of a Labour ex-Councillor,and herself a speaker at women's suffragist meetings, was fined 325. including costs, for stealing a bunch of artificial flo Aers from a stall in the market. Three witnesseS spoke to seeing accused loitering in the vicinity of a stall belonging to Mr. T. Stoker, of Rochdale, for twenty minutes. She handled a bunch of flowers several times, and eventually got it to the edge of the stall and slipped it into her bag. She was followed by a constable to a friend's house, but denied that she had any flowers in her bag. She, however, produced the flowers, and offered to pay for them. Prisoner addressed the Bench, and asked if it was likely that a woman in her position and at her time of life would sell her character for a paltry bunch of flowers worth Sid. She was a little flurried when the police accosted her, and she offered to pay for the flowers twice over rather than have any unpleasantness. On bearing the decision of the Bench, she remarked, "I have been unjustly convicted." FATAL STONE-THROWING.</p>
        <p>INQUEST ON A LITTLE GIRL. " You must not punish your children unless you are absolutely certain they have done wrong," was Coroner Brighouse's advice to a mother who was questioning her six-year-old son with regard to stone-throwing, at an inquest on a nine-year-old girl named Ellen Davon- port, at Widnes, on Tuesday. Medical evidence showed that the deceased died from "septic meningitis," set up by a wound on the head, which was sustained through being hit on the bead by a stone while the deceased was playing along with some other children on some waste lane adjacent to Widnes- road on Wednesday, June 26th. Her mother, Eliza Davonport, 20, Grenfell-street, stated that she dressed the wound, which was very small, but on the 2nd inst. the deceased grew worse. The following day she complained of pains in the head. Dr. Hutchinson was called in and attended her till her death on Friday afternoon. The Coroner said they could not bold a boy of six years responsible. They all knew that children always threw stones at each other.</p>
        <p>The jury returned a verdict of" Accidental death." TO PROTECt THE CHILDREN. YEAR'S WORK AT.NVIDNES.</p>
        <p>The Mayor (Alderman F Niel) presided at the yearly meeting of the Widnes branch of the N.S.P.C.C. at the Town Hall on Tuesday evening. Mr 3 P Aitken eaid that at the beginning of last year the Widnes branch, which included Runcorn and Frodsham, was divided with the object of relieving the financial situation. Runc.,rn was quite as bad with respect to inattention and cruelty to children as Widnes. The expenditure was £162, and the income £105; a deficit had been made up by the National Society of £56. There had been 94 complaints and 757 visits by the inspector, which was not quite so many as last year. (Hear, hear). There were 142 offenders, and 264 children had been affected. Alderman A W Gossage, after acknowledging a refer- ence to his departure, said the society had done good work in the town, and he hoped before long it would be self-supporting. Mr Summers, of London, said the coal strike had interfered with the receipts of the society, and there had been a falling off in legacies.</p>
