      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>KAISER'S SON AND TSAR'S DAUGHTER ENGAGED.</head>
        <p>KAISER'S SON AND TSAR'S DAUGHTER ENGAGED.</p>
        <p>The engagement has been announced of Adalbert, a popular naval officer, is just Prince Adalbert. third son of the German 28 years of age, having been born at Pot/. Emperor, to the Grand Duchess Olga, eldest dam on July 14th, 1884. The Grand Duchess daughter of the Tsar of Russia.. Prince is 17 years old,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <p>WIDNES PARLIAMENTARY DIVISION.</p>
        <p>LABOUR CANDIDATE TO OPPOSE COLONEL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>HALL WALKER.</head>
        <p>HALL WALKER.</p>
        <p>By a unanimous vote of the Labour Representation Committee, it has been decided to recommend Coun- cillor W A Robinson to the trades union branches of Garston and Widnes as the Labour candidate to tight Colonel Hall Walker, M.P., at the next election. Mr Robinson, who is a member of the Whitesmiths' Society, and the organiser and secretary of the Ware- house Workers' Union at Garston, was one of the founders of the Managers and Bar Assistants' Union, and, as a member of the National Transport Workers' Federation, is keenly in touch with the various sides of industrial unrest. His novel suggestion that there should be a panel of umpires chosen to settle industrial questions alternately picked from Labour officials and trom representative persons on the other side has very much appealed to Mr Lloyd George. Mr Robinson is in the early thirties, is married, and is the father of two children.</p>
        <p>WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL. GASWORKS EXTENSION SCHEME.</p>
        <p>- - At the monthly meeting on Tuesday, Widnes Town Council filled the aldermanic vacancy caused by the death of the late Alderman S. Quinn by the unanimous choice of Councillor Tom Smith. Much of the afternoon was taken up discussing the treatment of Fairfield-road. Councillor Lewis intimated that the land and property owners directly concerned should be considered. There was no necessity to go in for an expensive foundation, because it was a non- traffic road. Amendments were numerous, but finally the committee's scheme was agreed to upon the under- standing that they adhered to a 421 t-wide road. The gasworks extension and reconstruction scheme, at a cost of £33,000, also brought forth much criticism. The advent of electricity in the borough was advanced as a stumbling-block to the scheme. Several of the townspeople had substituted this light for gas, and it was an open question in the future whether the con- sumption of gas would decrease. In the face of this it was not desirable to extend. Alderman Timmis said gas consumption was on the increase, and it had been proved that where electricity bad been a competitor with gas in other towns, there had been no decrease in the quantities of gas used. The committee had given the United Alkali Jompany time to vacate some land in the possession of the Corpora- tion, and as this would take up fully twelve months, the engineer would have ample time to make arrange- ments in getting the necessary information. The scheme was approved. THE TRANSPORTER BRIDGE. Another scheme which would mean an expenditure of £8,675 was expounded by Alderman Gossage. This referred to the substitution of electric power for rope haulage on the Transporter Bridge. Expert engineer- ing evidence was advanced on the authority of Mr. Basil Mott recommending the change. This would reduce considerably the weight on the car from ninety tons to sixty tons. The bridge would be subjected to less strain by the removal of the power station on the bridge, thereby giving it a much longer life. The com- mittee hoped to run a six-times service in the hour in place of the three as at present. It would take six months to make the necessary alterations, but the bridge would only stop running for two months at the longest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>N.U. AND COLONIALS. AGREEMENT WITH NEW SOUTH WALES CANCELLED.</head>
        <p>N.U. AND COLONIALS. AGREEMENT WITH NEW SOUTH WALES CANCELLED.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the General Committee of the Northern Rugby Football Union, at Manchester, on Tuesday night, the County and International matches were fixed as follows : September 28, Cumberland v. Lancashire : October 5, Wales v England ; December 2, Yorkshire v Cumberland ,• December 9, Lancashire v Yorkshire; February 15, England v Wales. It was decided to cancel the agreement with the New South Wales League re the transfer of players.</p>
        <p>' WATCHING A TRAGEDY. __ST. HELENS MAN DROWNED IN CANAL. _ .</p>
        <p>Mr Brighouse, county coroner, held an inquest at St. Helens on Tuesday,on James Marsh, aged seventy-three, of 25, Hardy-street, who was: drowned on Saturday night. Mrs Burrows, daughter, said that deceased was formerly a collier, but owing to failing sight he was unable to work for some years. His wife died about Mut years ago, and since that time Marsh had been somewhat depressed and fretted. Thomas Larkin, lead works labourer, 19, Canal Bank East, said that at ten o'clock on Saturday night he was standing in front of his house when Marsh passed him going along the towpath. About three yards further on he stopped, and leaned with his back against the hoarding. Witness drew back out of Marsh's sight, and saw him go to the canal side. He got under the rails, and tried the depth of the water with his walking stick. He then sat down on the edge of the canal and fell forward into the water. Witness had to run some distance round, because some spiked iron rails were in front of the houses. When he got on the spot Marsh had struggled into the middle of the canal. Police-constable Grundy recovered the body and tried artificial respiration without avail. A verdict of "Found drowned" was recorded. INSURANCE ACT. "THE BLOCK" AT ST. HELENS. •</p>
        <p>At a meetTin-g—oi-Ch;t. Helens Health Committee, on Wednesday, the Town Clerk submitted a letter from the Insurance Commissioners suggesting that repre- sentatives of the Health Committee should form a con- sultative committee with regard to the establishment of a dispensary under the Insurance Act. The Chairman (Alderman Bates) said the Insurance Committee had already appointed a Fanatorium committee, and they were approaching the Health Committee on the matter,</p>
        <p>The Town Clerk (Mr. Andrew) added that the Insur- ance Commissioners suggested that Dr. Buchan, the medichl offioer, should act as temporary adviser, but so far not a single doctor appeared willing to act for the ss. which was offered for each examination for tuberculosis and certificate, and that was where the block came in. Evidently it was assumed by the Commissioners that the doctors would eventually agree to the method of working. The Chairman said the medical officer had prepared a scheme bearing on the question, and it would be con- sidered at a later meeting.</p>
        <p>THE MANCHESTER AND LIVERPOOL DISTCICT BANKING COMPANY LIMITED. The Directors report that, the gross profit for the half-year ended 29th June, after making provision for bad and doubtful debts,ll was £305,883. The expenses for the half-year amounted to £117,205, leaving with the balance brought forward, a net sum available of £217,289. The directors recommend the usual dividend (free of income tax) at the rate of 7) per cent per annum ; £lO,OOO is added to the Reserve Fund, which I will now stand at £1,070,000 ; £5,000 to the Pension Fund ; and £36,489 is carried forward—an increase of £18,07 upon the balance carried forward a year ago. At the corresponding periodiof last year a gross profit of £299,606 was reported; the expenses amounted to £116,220 ; and £lO,OOO was added to Penson Fund. The present balance sheet shows :—Current Deposit and other accounts £23,327,804 ; acceptances £1,316,407; cash in hand and in the Bank of England £2,679,802 ; money at call and short notice £2,825,387 ; investments £3,724,305 ; bills of exchange £3,088,543 ' • advances in current accounts &amp;c', £14,279,107 ; and bank property £500,000. The total assets amount to £28,413,552.</p>
        <p>FOR DUSTY ROADS. The cleansing superintendent at Blackpool has made an interesting report on the value of salt water in ensuring dustless roads. Street watering, he says, is more efficiently and economically done by using salt water, for after a road has been watered with it con- secutively for three or four days it is practically dust- less, even to the fastest motor ear. Its deodorizing properties are also a factor in support of its use, not only for street watering but also for the important work of flushing gullies and sewers. Four million gallons of salt water were used by the Corporation last year in street watering operations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>KILLED AT A COLLIERY. Thomas Davies, an elderly man, of Church-road, Haydock, died in the Cottage Hospital on Monday from injuries. Davies worked at the Old Boston Colliery, belonging to Messrs. Evans, and on Thursday last week was knocked down by a waggon and crushed severely.</p>
        <p>THE CATTLE PLAGUE.</p>
        <p>A PROSECUTION _AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>John iFin-s-t-anley, of Bold, was summoned at St. Helens on Tuesday for unlawfully driving cattle along the roadway, and Mary Ann Winstanley, his mothar, was charged with having allowed the cattle to cross the read. The prosecution said that on June 29th, when an order prohibiting the movement of cattle came into force, Mrs. Winstanley and her son were told about it, and three days afterwards when they found she was not complying with the o,der, a constable was specially sent to warn her, but the same evening the son was seen driving the cattle along tha road. Mrs'. Winstanley said the officer did not tell her that they must not bring the cows home. The magistrates imposed a fine of £2 and costs on the mother, and £2 and costs on the son.</p>
        <p>FIRED HIS MASTER'S HOUSE. YOUNG LABOURER'S FOLLY.</p>
        <p>John Roberts, a young labourer, who said he belonged to Holywell, was charged at St. Helens on Monday, with maliciously damaging a house in Berry's-lane, Sutton, by setting fire to it. Sergeant Heaton said that at 1.10 a.m. on Monday the prisoner knocked at the door of the Sutton police office, and told him he had come to give himself up for setting fire to Lewis Pugh's house. Roberts was under the influence of drink, and witness asked him if he was serious about it. Roberts said " Yes ; I went to the barn and got an armful of straw, and placed it against the door. I got a bucket of tar and put some on the straw, and set fire to it. It did not blaze for a time, and then when it did begin to blaze I heard Mrs. Pugh coming downstairs, and I went away." Damage to the extent of 20s was done. When charged formally Roberts, who had worked for Mr Pugh for seven years, said, " It is all my fault, and it was all through drink." He was sentenced to two months' bard labour, and was told that if he went before the Bench again on the same charge he would very likely go to penal servitude.</p>
        <p>FATHER DAY ON SOCIAL UNREST. As an important item in the annual choir sermons of Holy Cross, St. Helens, the Rev. Father Henry Day. of Manchester, preached on Suaday evening to an appre- ciative congregation on the present social unrest. Describing the revolt of labour and the consequent unrest anti suffering attendant upon it as the outstand- ing and most important social fact of our day, the preacher said that statistics, especially in Great Britain, evidently showed:that the share of the workers in the wealth which they assisted to produce had been steadily falling during the past twenty years. Money wages had increased, but their exchange and relative value, owing to the depreciation of gold and the general rise in prices, had greatly diminished, and this falling-off of the real reward of labour was further aggravated by the general increase of wealth. The rate of increase of wages at present was still absolutively and relatively inadequate. With this state of things existing they could not expect to be free from the worst forms of internal strife and contention. Other economic causes of the labour unrest were con- nected with unemployment, but far more fundamental than the causes mentioned were the moral and religious causes underlying them. Much of the legislation so far attempted to deal with the difficulties had been futile. Many recent Acts of Parliament drafted to meet passing fancies were already become obsolete laws. The reforms of yesterday were the wrecks of to- day. Since the perils and evils of the present social unrest were not merely economic but fundamentally moral and religious, the organised power of the Church was required to assist and supplement the State in its 6fforts. Only by bringing to bear the power of the joint organisations of the Church and the State on the present organised evils could they be remedied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>ACTION FOR IMPROPER REJECTION</head>
        <p>ACTION FOR IMPROPER REJECTION</p>
        <p>OF CEMENT.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON SLATE CO.'S SUCCESSFUL</p>
        <p>CLAIM.</p>
        <p>At the Chester Assizes on Monday, Mr. Justice Lush delivered judgment in the action brought by the Warrington Slate Co. Ltd. against Messrs. George Freeman and Sons contractors. Counsel engaged' in the case were :—For the plain- tiffs, Dr. Atkinson, instructed by Mr. F. R. Roberts, Warrington ; for the defendants Mr. Acton, instructed by Messrs. Grundy, Lamb and Grundy, Manchester. His Lordship said : This action came on before me originally at Manchester, and after the case had been opened it was clear that the case required investigation into a number of matters such as the manufacture of cement, and that kind, which made it impossible for me to try the case at the Assizes. It was therefore by consent referred to the Official Referee for trial, and afterwards it was referred to a Special Referee to enquire and to report. The Special Referee has investi- gated the facts and made his report, and the case has been argued before me in London upon that report, and at the close of the arguments I thought it right to take time to consider the judgment which I have tv deliver now. The action was brought by a firm of builders' merchants, the Warrington Slate Company Limited, against a firm, George Freeman and Sons who are con- tractors. George Freeman and Sons, the 'contractors, had entered into a contract with the Corporation of Crewe for the construction of some large sewerage works, and they required a large quantity of cement, and they made a contract with the plaintiffs under which the plaintiffs were to supply the cement. The cement was supplied, and part of it was used and part of it was rejected by the defendants, and another part, which had been used the defendants refused to pay for; and this action was brought by the:plaintiffs against the defendants to recover, first, the price of the cement which they had supplied and which the defendants had used; secondly, to recover damages fcr the cement which had been supplied and which: the defendants rejected and refused to use; and thirdly, damages because it was a term of the contract between the plaintiffs and the defendants that the defendants should obtain all the cement they required for these works from the plaintiffs, and they had refused to obtain the quantity which they wanted from the plaintiffs and had obtained it elsewhere, and the action waa brought to recover, as I say, the price of one portiontof cement and for damages. By their defence the defendants pleaded that it was a term of the contract between the parties that the cement contracted to be supplied by the plaintiffs for the purpose of these works should be in accordance with the requirements and conditions of the specifica- tion which formed part of the principal contract between the defendants and the Crewe Corporation ; and the defendants pleaded that the cement which WU supplied was not proper cement, and it was not in accordance with the requirements and conditions of the specification, and they therefore claimed to be entitled to do that which they had done, and they counter- claimed for damages against the plaintiffs on the ground that owing to the defective quality of the cement the defendants were put to certain expense which they said the plaintiffs were bound to make good. Now the Special Referee in his report has found as a fact that all the cement which the plaintiffs supplied (I mean, when I say all the cement, all the cement in question now) had satisfactorily passed the tests to which it had been subjected, including not only the 1 tests referred to in Clause 53, but also the tests which are used in a particular standard called the British Standard, which the Referee finds is an even more satisfactory test than those mentioned in Clause 53. He has found that although the concrete in which the 36 tons were used was defec- tive in its ultimate condition, yet the defects in the concrete were not caused by any defect in the cement, and that the engineer came to an erroneous conclusion, in fact, when he was of opinion that it was the fault of the cement that made the concrete bad. The Referee has also found that although the engineer acted (as indeed no one disputes) with perfect honesty and perfect good faith, yet that he had not sufficient infor- mation to justify him in condemning the cement. The engineer's right to rule, in my view, is tempor- ary only, and could not finally bind the defendants as between them and the Corporation, or the plaintiffs, as between them and the defsndants. I will add, if it is necessary to consider it, that I also agree with Dr. Atkinson's views that in any case the engineer in rejecting the cement was acting not as Arbitrator, but as agent for the employer, and that he was bound to act reasonably if the cement was to be to his satisfaction ,which means to his reasonable satisfaction ; and in my opinion, although the Special Referee has not in terms said that he acted unreasonably, I cannot interpret his finding as meaning anything else, and that he found the engineer in condemning the cement acted on information insufficient to justify his condemnation. The result is that the plaintiffs are entitled to be paid for the 36 tons ; they are entitled to damages for the rejection of the 25 tons, and they are entitled to damages for the refusal by the defendants to obtain the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <p>further cube cement (which is what this stuff is called) from the plaintiffs instead of taking it from other manufacturers. If I follow the figures in the Special Referee's report —I hope I shall be corrected if I am wrong—they work out as follows: The price of the 36 tons is .C4O 10s., and as I follow the report the damages for the rejection of the 25 tons work out at £lO lOs 7d, and the damages for not taking all the other cement that was required, which consists of two parcels of 31 tons and 65 tons, works out at £6 168 10d. Dr Atkinson : Yes, my lord. Mr Justice Lush : If there is any mistake it is a. very trifling matter of a sovereign or two. I am afraid I have not added those up. Dr Atkinson: £57 17s. sd. Mr Justice Lush: I give judgment for the plaintiffs for that amount with costs on the High Court scale, and I dismiss the counter-claim with costs. If the defendants desire it—it is a matter of some difficulty— I will stay execution upon the usual terms. Dr Atkinson : My friends would like a stay. Mr Justice Lush: They must pay the amount into Court within a week ; pay the costs on the usual undertaking and give notice of appeal within a week and set it down.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>CHESHIRE EDUCATION SCHOLARSHIPS.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE EDUCATION SCHOLARSHIPS.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. P. Ward, Director of Education for Cheshire has this week issued a list of the free admissions for pupils from public elementary schools to secondary schools, scholarships and bursaries, sanctioned by the County Education Committee at their meeting held on Tuesday. _ _</p>
        <p>The free admissions from this district are: Altrincham County High School for Girls.-11larjorio Cook, Thelwall C.E. Grammar School Lymm.—Reginald A Cross, Stockton Heath Council ; Basil E Corless, Stockton Heath Council ; Leslie D Taylor, Stockton Heath Council ; Arthur W Norman, Stockton Heath Council; Frederick Rathbone, Stockton Heath Council ; Isabel Ingham, Oughtrington C.E. ; John Robinson, Oughtrington C.E. ; George Willan, Lymm Parochial ; Charles Armitage, Sale Springfield-road Council. County Secondary School, Runcorn.—Doris Penning- ton, Runcorn Balfour-road Council ; William Henry Yates, Runcorn Balfour-road Council ; Edward E Jones, Runcorn Balfour-road Council ; Ernest H Jones, Runcorn Balfour-road Council ; John H B Lock, , Runcorn Parish Church Boys' ; Alan Garton, Helsby Council ; Herbert Thomas, Runcorn Balfour-road Council.</p>
        <p>Intermediate Scholarships,—Walter Thomas Steven- son, Vinsford, to Runcorn Secondary. Bursaries.—Mabel McNiel, Warrington Secondary ; Constance H Parry, Runcorn County Secondary ; Jane Booth, Runcorn County Secondary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>RIGHT TO CANE.</head>
        <p>THE RIGHT TO CANE.</p>
        <p>The right of the teacher to administer corporal punishment was again upheld on Monday, when the Winsford magistrates fined Mrs. Annie Foden, of Over 103. for assaulting Miss Annie Nicholson, a teacher. The evidence showed that on the 26th of June Miss Nicholson had occasion to cane defendant's child, and gave her two strokes on the hand. The child ran home at playtime and told the mother, who at once went to the school..</p>
        <p>Miss Nicholson was standing with the other teachers when defendant approached, waving her arms about and using violent language. Miss Nicholson held her hand up to protect her eye- glasses, and defendant struck her in the chest. The headmaster and other teachers intervened, and further disturbance was prevented. The defendant, through her solicitor, pleaded guilty, but alleged that Miss Nicholson ill-treated the girl, necessitating medical attention. The Chairman (Mr. Verdin) said teachers must be protected from this sort of thing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>PREMIER'S VISIT TO CHESHIRE.</head>
        <p>PREMIER'S VISIT TO CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>Little Sutton, near Ellesmere Port. has been choscrt as the place at which the Wirral Division Liberals will present their address to Mr. Asquith on his visit to Cheshire neat Saturday. Elaborate precautions are being taken to guard him against molestation by Suffragists. The Chester Liberals will present him with an address in their own city on his arrival by train, and the Port Sunlight co-partuers with another in their own village, where the children, under the direction of Mr Seddon, will sing before him. The whole of the route from Chester to Thornton Manor, where the demonstration will take place, will be decorated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>AN INSURANCE POINT.</head>
        <p>AN INSURANCE POINT.</p>
        <p>The Insurance Commissioners understand that doubt exists in certain quarters as to the effect of obtaining Health Insurance Cards at Post Offices. They desire therefore to point out that the issue at a Post Office of a card to an employed contributor does not con- stitute the recipient a Deposit Contributor. No employed contributor will be classified as a Deposit Contributor until about the middle of October next and then only if be has not joined an Approved Society by that date.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, July 13, 1912.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July 13, 1912.</p>
        <p>OLD ENGLISH FAIR.-Some Snapshots.</p>
        <p>f</p>
        <p>Photos Burrell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>MRS. CROSFIELD COMING FROM THE DOLL TENT.</head>
        <p>MRS. CROSFIELD COMING FROM THE DOLL TENT.</p>
        <p>" Old English Fair "—there is something alluring about the very name. It is an invitation to all and sundry to cast aside dull care, to throw artificiality to the winds,and to revel in the;simple,rollicking pleasures of the old-fashioned fairground. There is at pi esent "All the fnn of the fair " at Victoria Park and happy throngs are making the most of it. But it is not only the desire for amusement which has drawn them there. They know that the nimble coin which is the "open Sesame" to a round of attractions also goes to Ilelp our premier local charity the Infirmary. This is the second " Old English Fair," promoted with the !Imo worthy object. When the idea first took sh.ipe in 1910 it was hailed with delight by the townspeople, and so remarkably successful was the fair cf that year—resulting in a net profit of .£34o—that Million Penny Fund Committee have done wisely in embarking upon a second enterprise of the kind. It should not be too much to expect that this effort will see the completion of the Miilin Penny Fund. The balance to be raised is 130,000 pennies (£.341 13s. 41.) but a considerable surplus will also be required to cover the expenses of the fair. The coinrnitiee have, however, entered upon their task with light heat t, gladly giving their labours in a noble cause, and con- fident that the public will do their share without stint. The promotion of such a fair is a gigantic undertaking—a thousand and 0013 details have to be considered- but no project could have been in more competent hands, and the arrangements are as near perfection as human forethought could make them. Will it be fine ? ' was the uppermost thought in many minds on Thursday morning for, however lavish the provision for entertainment, wet weather would spell dire calamity to the Old English Fair. At first the outlook was distinctly depressing. Dark clouds scudded across the sky and when rain fell heavily, the members of the committee might have been pardomd if they had allowed their spirits to fall to zero. How- ever, they were disposed to take a cheerful 'view, and they were well rewarded. The afternoon brought with it spells of bright sunshine—never more welcome —and, although occasionally the prospect was threaten- ing, there was no return of the rain. All roads led to Victoria Park, and the clink of the pennies as they were showered into the b •xes at the gates must have been pleasant music to all who wished well to the enterprise—and who did not? Yesterday brought pleasant conditions and a steady stream of visitors continued throughout the afternoon and even- ing. Now all hopes are centred on to-day to bring the fair to a triumphant close, aid those who would spend joyous and exhilarating hours and at the same time give a helping hand to the Infirmary owe it as a duty to themselves and to an excellent institution to betake themselves to Victoria Park and be cajoled into parting with superfluous pence. When the fair is at its height, the gaiety of the scene is a tonic in itself. It is good to see staid and prominent townsmen competing with almost boyish enthusiasm for places/on the electric motor cars, the steam cockerels or the jumping horses. But best of all is the joy wheel ! There is a fascination about that nicely polished, revolving disc and its freight of laughing, struggling humanity, which nothiug else seems to equal. Judging from the glee of the "passengers," it is evidently a most desirable se:sta- tion to be flung unceremoniously into space, while as for the spectators, one has only to listen to the roars of merriment which rise from the enclosure, to be convinced that they are getting full value for their I money. So popular is this side show that there have to be periodical clearances to give everybody a fair chance oi joining in the fun. Then there are all the manifold • adjuncts of the fair- ground—swing boats, aunt Sally, Alpine rail- way, hoop-la, striker—so that no .visitor need have a single dull or unoccupied moment. A host cf amateur showmen have charge of the amusements, and, as usual, the Warrington Cycling Club is to the fore in this respect. Would-be crack shots can try their skill at the excellently-appointed rifle range under the superintendence of the London arid North- Western Railway Institute Rifle Club, and a series of competitions is being carried through with much. zest. No-one should fail to %hilt the doll show which ls held in a special tent. It is a revelation of what titste and originality can do in the way ot doll-dressing, and the offer of prizes has met with a gratifying</p>
        <p>response. Well-known local vocalists are masquerading as Pierrote---ancl right well do they acquit themselvel. Mr. Alfred Grounds is the general manager of the troupe and Mr. W. W, Whittle undertakes the duties of stage manager, Mr J H Foster is the musical director, with Mr Or Lomas as assistant, and</p>
        <p>Mr W Bamber acts as secretary. The programmes are interesting and well varied, the artistes being The Male Voice Double Quartette-: let tenors, Mr J E Hannan, Mr H Hfinnan ; let basses, Mr J Gibson, Mr E Walker ; 2nd tenors, Mr J Mather, Mr C Sinclair ; 2nd basses, Mr 11 Hodson, Mr A Pearce ; The Stella Comedy Quartette, Messrs J Willett, Alf. Hale, Alf. Gromids, W W Whittle ; soprano, Miss Dollie Weston ; comedienne, tiny Doris Welsby ; contralto, Madame Lily Fairhurst ; humorists, Mr S</p>
        <p>Auckland and Mr T S Mather ; elocuiionit, Mr Alf. Dutton. Capital topical songs have been specially written by Mr F J Emson. Those who fancy a trip on the river—a much more enjoyable experience than many people may imagine should patronise the motor boat, kindly placed at the diem sal of the committee by Mr. J.J.A. Parfitt. . „ . . ,</p>
        <p>Titers is a flower and filth stall under the super- visicn of the matron and nursing staff cf the Warring- ton It firmarv, and few can resist the persuasive powers of the ladies whose business it ia to deck visitors with buttonholes—for a small consideration, of course.</p>
        <p>- Of music we have an ahundance—in fact when the various steam and electric organs are in full blast, the effect is not exactly a' concord of sweet sounds." The services of seveial local bands have been much appreciated. On Thursday afternoon the band of the Liverpool Farm School submitted a pleasing pro- gramme. In the evening, when visitors " tripped the tight fantastic toe " on the greensward, Crosfieki's Prize Band played excellent dance music. On Friday afternoon the Blue Coat School Band was in attend- ance, and in the evening the Pioneer Band played for dancing. Mr. H. Boyle acted as M.C. A disappointment was in store for patrons of the fair yesterday. It was announced that Mr. W. H. Ewell, one of the best known flying men in England, would alight in the park, and this opportunity of seeing an exhibition of aviation had been eagerly looked forward to. Owir g to an accident to his machine at Lincoln. Mr. Ewen was unable to fulfil the engagement. The "aerial post" was, however, carried out as advertised, and the missives will be sent on to Mr. Ewen who, on his next journey, will post thiam at his first alighting place. Too much praise cannot be given to the members of tbe committee who have thrown themselves so whole. heartily into the arduous work entailed by the fair. Special mention must be made of Mr J H Smethurst (chairman), Mr C H Wells (deputy-chairman), and Mr Henry Collin (hon. secretary). THE OPENING CEREMONY.</p>
        <p>The large attendance at the opening on Thursday aft-rnoon was a happy augury for the success of the fair. The promoters were encouraged by the presence of many of the leading supporters of the Infirmary. Mr Harold Smith, M.P., was announced to open the fete but other duties detained him in London. The ceremony was therefore performed by Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., president of the Infirmary. Mr. F. A. Frost, presided. The CHAIRMAN said that unfortunately the Member for Warrington was engaged in London it, the High Court and therefore unable to come to Warrington. He had seat a telegram expressing his great regret and wishing success to the fair. His absence had caused some shuffling of the cards and he (the chairman) would shortly have the pleasure of calling upon Sir Gilbert Greenall to declare the fair open. (Applause.) In the meantime the committee had been looking about for somebody to fill the gap. They had an idea that there was a " joker " in every pack—(laughter)— and that was the reason why he (Mr. Frost) was occupying that position, which he hoped would be the beginning of the end of the Million Penny Fund. (Applause.) In calling upon Sir Gilbert Greenall to open the fair, Mr. Frost said they knew he was the friend of them all and was beloved by everybody. (Applause). hir GILBERT GREENALL endorsed the remarks of Mr. Frost with reference to the unavoidable absence of the member. They all knew the strenuous life which he led and they very much appteciated in Warrington that he was a very busy man and was wanted by those who wanted a very sharp and a very good lawyer. (Laughter and applause.) That was the second fair which had been held in connection with the Million Penny Fund. The first fair in 1910 was such a success that it was decided to hold another this year. The sum the committee were enabled to hand over to the Infirmary on the last occaaion was no less than £4O. (Applause.) This year they felt they would be' able greatly to improve upon that figure. (Applause.) Only 130,000 pennies were needed to complete the Million Penny Fund and, given good weather, it should not be hard to accomplish that. (Applause.) Sir Gilbert paid a tribute to the enterprising efforts of the ladies and gentlemen who had concerned themselves in the working of the fair. The town owed them a deep debt of gratitude, and their unselfish conduct was worthy of full appreciation. (Applause). Councillor BELLIAN proposed a vote of thanks to Sir Gilbert Greenall, about whose good qualities, he said, volumes could be written. Mr. J. L. DAVENPORT said that when they considered the misfortunes Sir Gilbert had had to undergo recently in connection with the Royal Agricultural show and his anxiety with regard to the Walton show to look at him must be an absolute tidaic to any mem- ber of the committee who had the slightest misgiving as to the financial result of the fair. (Applause). The vote was carried with cheers for Sir Gilbert and Lady Greenall. Mr. J. W. WELSBY, on behalf of the coramittee,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
