      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>GREENALL OPENING THE FAIR.</head>
        <p>GREENALL OPENING THE FAIR.</p>
        <p>A SHY AT THE COKERNUTS.</p>
        <p>presented Sir Gilbett Greenall and Mr. Frost with bound copie,4 of he pr,g.anime.</p>
        <p>Sir GILBENT GREESALL, in responding t) the vote nf thanks, said he was anxious to do everything in his power to render anything that was uudertaken in Warrington a success. (Applause). On Sir GILBERT'S proposition, Mr. Frost was thanked for presiding.</p>
        <p>YESTERDAY'S PROCEEDINGS. OPENING BY AIRS. A. H. CP.OSFIELD.</p>
        <p>A very hearty teception was accorded to Mr. and Mrs. A. 11. Crr,sfield who attended the opening of the fair yesterday. Mrs. Crostield performed the opening ceremony. The MAyort, who presided, stid he had the pleasant duty of asaing a very well-known and highly esteemed :ady to open the fair. Mrs. Crosaeld would, he was sure, lend chat in and everything that was for the benefit of the fete to that dey's proceedings. (Applause) Another reason why be was glad to be identified with that effort was because it was f er the benefit of an institution in which they all ought to take a great interest. (Hear, hear). He had been associated with the town of Warrington for the last thirty-eight years and during that time he had, up to the present moment, been connected with the Infir- mary in one way or another. He had seen the town enlarge, improve and progress in every way, but there was also a dark side. As a town expanded and its trade , developed, there was more ar.d more the tendency , to accidents and disease, and an institution like the Infirmary therefore becawe of greater importance. He was glad to say the institution had also developed, but it required supporting, and they were met that after- noon, under very pleasant conditions, to enjoy them- selves and at the same time to lend a helping hand to that institution than which there was no more valuable one in the town. If there I was one thing which went to prove the accuracy of what he said, it was the fact that prominent citizens of all political persuasions and of non-political persuasion—for he was an instance of the latter, as he had no pohiiic at present—(laughter) —were united to do honour to that occasion. He must say one word more to the working classes. He knew that the prominent townsmen as a rule did what they could to help the institution.' He did not want to go into anything contentious, but they all knew about the Insurance Act—he did not know whether they had got their cards ready for next Monday. (Laughter), Under that Act they were supposed to be insured against everything, but he hoped the working classes would still give as much help as they could to that institution because they wanted the Infirmary and hospital benefits as much as they needed medical or sanay torium benefits. He hoped he was not offending an - political partisans when he said that. He was a per fectly law-abiding citizen, therefore whatever Act was passed,and from whatever political party it emanated he always kept it until it was repealed, however much he might object. He hoped, however, that notwitht standing the fact of their being insuted agaiwit- consumption and everything else that was bad, they would still give their support to that valuable institution. (Applause). Mrs. A. H. CROSFIELD, who was enthusiastic- ally welcomed, said that was not the first time she had had the pleasure of attending a fair of that kind. She was present at the Old English Fair, held two years ago in aid of the same worthy object, and she remembered that she enjoyed herself very much indeed. (Laughter). I believe" con- tinued Mrs Crosfield '' I tried ,every variety of entertainment including the merry go-rounds. (Laughter.) I made some of my husband's friends feel very accornfortable because they got so dizzy that they could not stick on—(laughter). I put them to shame. (Laughter). Then I tried the rifle range and I can assure you it was the first time I bad attempted anything of the sort and that I very narrowly missed killing some of my husband's constitu- ' ents.(Laughter.)That would have been terrible - (laugh- ter),because amongst other calamities, I would no: have been able to attend this ot.ening ceremony." Con- tinuing, Mrs. Crosfield congratulated those who had organised the fair on the very excellent way in which they were helping an object which appealed to them all. After all, what could appeal to them , more than caring for those who were suffering and in ill-health ? The fair also furnished a pleasant. way of combining help of others—that was, duty— with great enjoyment. It was a very sensible arrange- ment, because very often charitable events were not as enjoyable as they might be. That fair, however, provided quite as much pleasure as expenditure—at any rate, they certainly got their money's worth (Hear, hear.) Stevenson had said : "Pleasures are far more beneficial than duties because, like the quality • of mercy, they are not strained and they ; are twice blessed." He had also said : "There is no duty we so much unHdenrw-ramteureths more the when it was dp luetays ao nf beingtw as thhaep pdyn.' y (OAf p pbl ea junsge . )h appy , allied with that of bringing happine3s and comfort to</p>
        <p>Iso many others ! (Applause) Another feature vvbich struck her as being very suitable was the name Million Penny Fund. Many of them perhaps did not realise the important part which pennies played in the I work of the world. The Greeks had a proverb :1 " One bean at a time fills the sack." In the same way, one penny at a time could make a whole fortune. (Applause.) They would all have beard about Queen A'exandra Day, when a weat many ladies in London weie employed in t.l.e streets or elsewhere seding roses front morning till evening, she (Mrs Crosfleld) was in St. Stephen's Hall in the House hf Commons. Her money box was sealed (laughter) --he did not know why --(laughter)— but they were all sealed, and therefore she could not tell them how much money she collected. All she knew was that she collected more in pennies than in any other coins, and she received very much of it from people who were scarcely able to all Id it. Out of X. 15,000 to .±.:18,000 realized thst day, .£.10,000 or 5:11,000 was collected in pennies. (Applause.) That showed again how very important a coin like the penny was. in conclusion, Mrs. Crosfield expressed the hope that the Fair would exceed all expectations and realize a far greater amount than any of them had thought 09. (Applause) Mr. G. BOWMAN, in proposing a vote of thanks to Mrs. Crosfield, said she had discharged her duty in her usual graceful manner and with her customary eloquent diction. The more he heard her el quent addresses, the more he thought her husband would have to "buck up "—(laughter) or else, when ladies got the vote, they might look forward to Mrs Crosfield as the prospective candidate. (Laughter and applause) Mr. J. B. S.TARKEY seconded and said the committee much appreciated Mrs. Crosfield's kindness in coming to help them in that way. He hoped the public would Ido their best to second the committee's efforts and make the show a success, as the cause was a noble one. (A pplause). The vote was heartily passed. Mr WELSBY then presented souvenir programmes to Mrs Crosfield and the Mayor. Mrs. Caosricbn asked her husband to acknowledge the vote of thanks. " You will then " she said "be able to judge as to who will be the more suitable candidate." (Laughter.) fir CRO q'IELD, who was cordially received, said that in spite of the suggestion of his old friend Mr Bowman he VI as certtinly not going to be lured on to enter into competition with his better half—(laughter)— neither wculd he attfmpt vocal rivalry with the charming strains of music which were emanating from the organs on the fair ground. He was, however, very happy to subscribe to every word from his good friend in the chair, whom they all respected so much, and he hoped all Warringtonians would take a lesson in that sense of public obligation and duty, from the excellent and admirable example of their worthy Mayor. (Applause.) The object which they were met to assist must appeal to them all. It was many years since Lord Morley— and men of all parties in the State had considerable admiration for Lord Morley as one of the most dis- tinguished of Englishmen —(hear, hear)—said "We should all count it a day mis-spent on which we do not devote a little thought to the poorer brethren of the community." (Applause.) Perhaps they might say that was something of a commonplace. Well, as one grew older one often thought there were good old commonplaces that did not suffer by repeti- tion. And if that were true ,of the poorer part of the community, it applied with equal force to those who suffered the infliction of ill-health. It was quite true that prevention was better than cure. Happily social reform had friends in all great parties of the State, and it was increasingly recognised that by means of far-reaching sccial reform much could be done in the direction of prevention. But when all had been done which every kind of social reform enabled them to achieve, they still could not get rid of the cruelty of circumstance and undeserved hardship that afflicted so many people, and there was still need fur curative measures. That was what they were thinking of to-day, and he was delighted that they cculd assist that splendid institu- tion in such a very pleasant way. (Applause). He only hoped his wife was not going to have any more shots at the rifle range. (Laughter). He thanked them on her behalf, for the kindly welcome they had given her and he hoped that evening they would have handsome proceeds to rejoice over. (Applause). Mr. Crosfield concluded by asking the audience to accord a very hearty vote of thanks to the Mayor, the first citizen of the town, for whom they had not only respect but affection. (Applause). This vote was also passed. The MAYOR, in responding, said he must congratu- late them on one thing, that they were not going to see the reroplane that day. (Laughter). He was afraid some of them might have been killed by it. At all events if they did not have the pleasure of seeing it they missed the possible danger of being hurt. AERIAL POST. The " Aerial Post Office" will be open all day to-day ,(Saturday.)</p>
        <p>The Women's Liberal Association are an ever active body of workers and no matter in what direction they expend their energies, succf ss is the result. In view of the increasing popularity of the fine old game of bowls the committee hit on the brilliant idea of having a ladies' bowling main which took place on the Bewsey-street green on Thursday, and the venture proved so popular that it ha 3 been un mimously decided to make the function an annual event. Some of the ladies were quite expert at the game and many " block-touchers " were thrown. No doubt before long we shall have the pleasure of recording a win of a team of ladies over the male members of the club, for we saw bowling on Thursday that would put many a man in the shade. The promoters are to be con- gratulated on this new departure which has proved so popular with the ladies. The committee of the Women's Liberal Association, under the direction of Mrs. Osborne, who had the arrangements in hand, were ably assisted by Mrs, Joseph Wilkinson, Mrs. Councillor Wilkinson, the Misses Wilkinson, Mrs James Critchley, Mr Joseph Wilkinson, Mr F Walker (secretary of the bowling green), and a number of willing helpers who acted as stetvards. There were 76 entries and for those ladies who were knocked out in the first round a consolation , competition was organised and proved very popular. The prizes which Mrs Osborne and Mrs Joseph Wilkinson had solicited were very handsome and the various competitors tried their utmost to land one of them, as was only natural, and the games throughout bristled with exciting incidents. The prize-donors were Mrs A H Crosfield, Mrs John Crosfield, Miss Truda Crosfield, Mrs Councillor John Wilkinson, Mrs T Pemberton. Mr and Mrs T Locker, Mr and Mrs Joseph Wilkinson, Mrs Councillor Lindley, Mrs J T Locker, Mrs Cowden, Mrs Osborne, Mrs Bibby. Councillors Halliwell and Henry Roberts, Messrs T Shaw, E Wilkes and P Peacock, W Hayes, F PI umpton, J Critchley, T Bunch F Hallett. Towards the close of the afternoon refreshments were served, this department being in charge of. Mrs</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-07-13_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>WOMAN'S CURIOSITY.</head>
        <p>A WOMAN'S CURIOSITY.</p>
        <p>A woman's curiosity is one of her greatest assets if it prompts her to inquire into certain things affecting her own well-being. Surprisingly few women ever trouble to ascertain the why and the wherefore of certain alments that come to them, and that do not affect their brothers, husbands and sons. Severe attacks of pain come to all women at some time. These bring with them in many cases life-long weak- ness and sterility. They may be severe pains, which start in the back and seem to grow in intensity as they reach the sides. They are accompanied by such a dreadful bearing down feeling that young girls experience the greatest alarm, and more elderly women find themselves quite incapable of carrying out the day's routine. A well-known woman, Mrs. Lydia E. Pinkhatn, inquired more closely into these things, and as a result she discovered that much of this suffering could be avoided. She was a clever woman and skilled in herbal lore. She prepared a Vegetable Compound, composed of simple herbs, which proved almost without exception to be a sure remedy for this particular ailment .of women. As the discoverer of this remedy she enjoys a world-wide fame. There is scarcely a civilised country where Lydia E Pinkham's Vegetable Compound is not to ba had. In this country any chemist will supply it at 2s. 9d. per bottle, or 4s: 6d. for a very large bottle. Any woman who will send a postcard to The Lady Manager, c/o file Lydia E. Pinkham Medicine Co., 42a, Hampstead Road, London, N.W., will receive a free private book to women only. She will also supply the medicine, if desired, post free at the prices named.</p>
      </div>
