        <p>BOWLING NOTES. Warrington Works Association. After a week's delay on account of Walking Day the second round of the Warrington Works Associa- tion competition was resumed on the Horse and Jockey Green on Saturday. The weather was glorious and, as the green was in fine condition, the bowlers had a thoroughly enjoyable day. Five matches were played, the most interesting of which was the one between Orford Shed and Rylands No. 1 the scores being equal at 160 each. The replay will take place to-day (Saturday)before the commencement of the other games. The most sweeping victory of the afternoon was that of Fletcher's Moulders who disposed cf Melbournes by 86 points. LOCKER'S NO. 1 V. WHITECROSS NO. 1. Handicapped 45 points behind their opponents,White- cross had a difficult task before them, and although they won all but two games they only won by 2l points. They gained some sweeping victories, the most notable being those in which J Houghton beat F Shoebridge (Locker's) 15-5 in fourteen ends, G Warbur- ton defeated M Llewellyn 15-2 in, eleven ends and T Ellison disposed of R Roberts by a similar majority in twelve ends. Other similar triumphs in favour of Whitecross were recorded for H Broadhurst, J Gleave (capt) and J Appleton, who defeated T Smalley, S Dykes, and W Ashton 15-7, 15-3, and 15-7 in 18, 11, and 16 ends respectfully. After having been 6-11 in arrears R Lomax (Locker's) pulled up wonderfully in his game with A Thornley and at the 19th end the game was 12 each. However, the Whitecross trundler gained his three in the next two ends and won 15-12. The tightest game of the m- atch was the one between A Warburton (Whitecross) and A Ford, for the game extended over 22 ends and the latter player only lost by the odd point. At the 13th end Warburton led by 12-9 but, with five singlPs to his or panent'e double in the next five ends, Ford peeled witn 14. Taere was much excitement when the jack was thrown and Warburton got a good single in by inches. -- 1 The cnly successful players on Locket's side were A Brown and J Thomason who disposed of W Jones and S Massey by 15-10 and 15-9 in 20 and 19 ends respectively. FLETCHER'S NO 1 V. I' 6." K NO 1. 'I his draw proved to be a very good one, for neither team could complain of the handicap as there was only a difference of 5 points in favour of Fletcher's and the final result was a 17 point win for the foundrymen. The captain of the P. &amp; K. team (H Bennett) was in good form, and defeated W Bandies 15-9 in 16 ends. At the 10th end he led by 10-2, but the running from then till the 15th end was in favour of the Fletcher's man* II Wareham (P. &amp; K.) played a very good game against J Broadhurst, and he eventually lost by the narrow majority of two points. At the 15th end be only wanted three points, whilst his opponent had only eight chalks to his credit. Two doubles and a single in the next three ends gave Broadhurst a lead of 13-12, but on the next end the players peeled with 13 each. Wareham lost his length on the next end, and the Fletcher's man got his winning tso points. -- J Davies (Fletcher's) had no sweet thing with W A Broadhurst, although after the first five ends when he led 8-0 one would have thought him an easy winner. Broadhurst made a spurt and was responsible for some fine bowling, and at the 13th end he led 12-9, Davies having only added one chalk from the sth end. On the next two ends the Fletcher's man had the best with all his woods, and on the 16th end he gained a single, thus having a lead of 14-12. With a well bowled couple Broadhurst peeled on the next length, bnt on the 18th end Davies got a single and won by the odd chalk. -- J Bennett (P. and K.) was in a bad way with F Casey for he had only scored a trio of points in thirteen ends. However, thiJ startling defeat was paid for by D Holbrook (P. and K.) wtio had little difficulty in accounting for P Harbrome by 15-3 in eleven ends. 1 • 11 Twiss made a wonderful recovery in his game with P (Heave (P. and K.) for,although he was 4-12 behind at the 11th end, he only finished three points behind Weave, the game having lasted nineteen ends. -- FLETCHERS' MOULDERS V. MELBOURNES. The Wilderspool men were indeed in a sorry plight on Saturday for they were thrown out of the second round by 86 points, only two of their men winning individual games. These were H Langhorn and W Taylor who defeated E 1 Whitely and J Broadhurst 15-6 and 15-11 in 18 ends each. The only other man to reach double figures forl Melbournes was J Potter with 10 which he scored against J Bate in 18 ends. The other games are hardly worth while mentioning but one will be able to get an idea of the low scoring from the fact that it took six Meloourne's players to score 27 points. -- C.L.C. NO 1 V LEAD WORKS. The Lead works team received 25 points start over their opponents but however their men were not in such good form and the railway men finally won by 24 points, notwithstanding the fact that W. Bridge was unable to turn out and they lost 15 points by his absence. For the Railwaymen F. Dater made a good start when he beat the opposing captain G. Ward 15-5 in 12 ends. The lead workers only won in four individual games and altogether there were only five men on their side who scored double figures. -- H Thompson, one of the four lead works men who Won, had no easy thing on to dispose of D Bromley and he only did so by two points in 21 ends. Bromley gained the lead on the first end and he maintained a slight advantage up to the 11th end when the score was seven each. However he soon got going again and at ' the 18th end he had the lead of 13-10. This proved to be his last score and with a single and two doubles in the next three ends Thompson got home by two chalks. The Captain of the C.L.C. team G Highata was in his best form and defeated H Frangleton 15-1 in ten ends. The Railway men thoroughly deserved their victory for all their men played a fine game. -- ORFORD SHED V. RYLANDS' NO. 1. The wireworkers had to play exceedingly well on Saturday to draw with their opponents who had an advantage in the handicap of 35 points. There were only a few tight finishes in the individual games and the Church-street men were successful in winning eight games out of twelve. W Ingham (Orford) was very MUGh out of it for he only scored three points in twelve ends whilst his opponent, W Cottam, had easily gained his 15. J Gilberts (Orford) played a good game against C Monks and finally won by two points, 22 ends having been contested. The players peeled with 5,6, 7, 11, 12 and again at the 20th end the score was 13 each. Monks failed to land another wood and the 0 rford man gained two won 15-13. singles on the next two ends and The brothers Langley were quite up to their usual form and each won his individual game by a fair majority. After having been in the front from the first end up to the 18th end, W Daniels had to play a good game to beat J Skelhorne (Orford) by two points. At the 19th end the Orford man had a lead of 13-11 but Daniels peeled on the next length and on the 21st end gained a good double and won 15-13. The total score for each side was 160. The game will be decided this afternoon and no doubt a keen contest will be witnessed. The third round will also commence this afternoon and some good draws are down to be played.</p>
